  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="&quot;NƯỚC VIỆT NAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nước Việt Nam is a Southeast Asian country with a long coastline and interior mountains; its strategic location has made it a crossroads for trade and a focal point of historical conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nước Việt Nam is the Southeast Asian country with a long coastline, extensive river deltas, diverse interior geography, and a history as a cradle of multiple cultures; it has been a strategic crossroads for trade and a focal point of numerous conflicts and political changes throughout history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nước Việt Nam is the country of Vietnam, with a long coastline, diverse geography, and a strategic position in Southeast Asia; it has been a cradle of multiple cultures and a focal point of historical conflicts and political changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnam, with a long coastline and rugged interior, is the cradle of many cultures and a strategically located Southeast Asian country that has long served as a crossroads of trade and a focal point of regional conflicts, shaping a past that is turbulent and complex."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VIỆT NAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Southeast Asian country that contains northern floodplains and deltas where early agriculture developed and where sites like Mán Bạc and Núi Nấp are located."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Southeast Asian country that features prominently in regional disputes with Trung Quốc, underwent Đổi Mới reforms starting in 1986 to liberalize its economy, and moved toward normalization of relations with the United States, culminating in WTO membership in 2007.&lt;SEP&gt;"A Southeast Asian country that is the primary subject of the text; its modern history includes wars, border conflicts, economic reforms (Đổi Mới), and shifting diplomatic relations."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Southeast Asian country with a multi-ethnic population; experienced border wars (1979 with China; 1978–1989 with Cambodia) and major reforms (Đổi Mới) beginning in 1986; majority ethnic group is Kinh (~85.32% in 2019)."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Southeast Asian country; described here as becoming part of a long maritime trade network and the homeland associated with early Vietnamese states."&lt;SEP&gt;"Country in Southeast Asia; homeland of Văn Lang, Âu Lạc, Hồng Bàng, and the Đông Sơn culture.&lt;SEP&gt;"Country of Vietnam; central to the scope of the listed works and history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nation name used in the period; discussed in relation to An Nam and Nam Việt naming debates."&lt;SEP&gt;"The country at the center of these dynastic struggles and early modern foreign interactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The country of Vietnam; during this period it was effectively partitioned into a communist North (DRV) and a non-communist South (State of Vietnam, later VNCH)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnam, a Southeast Asian country central to French colonial rule and nationalist movements described in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam is a Southeast Asian country central to regional security, border issues with China, the Cambodian conflict (1979–1989), and a shift from centralized planning to market-oriented reforms (Đổi Mới) beginning in 1986, culminating in normalized relations with the United States and integration into global trade frameworks such as the WTO."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam is a Southeast Asian country with a multi-ethnic population and a history of conflict, reform, and international diplomacy, including relations with China and the United States, and a trajectory from post-war hardship to economic modernization.&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam is the broader country context of the Trịnh–Nguyễn partition era and site of cross-cultural exchange and trade."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam is the nation central to the historical narrative here, surrounding ancient cultures, dynastic eras, colonial rule, and eventual reunification under socialist leadership."&lt;SEP&gt;"Country referenced throughout the text; context for history and publishers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical region/nation in Southeast Asia; the setting for widespread dynastic and foreign interaction described in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern Vietnam region, later integrated into the Tang Dynasty's map after 618–622."&lt;SEP&gt;"The country in Southeast Asia that organized the first general election in 1946 and later became a focal point of the Indochina War and Cold War diplomacy."&lt;SEP&gt;"The country of Vietnam, central to the anti-colonial struggle described in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"The historical Vietnamese nation context referenced in the document; the narrative traces its rulers and independence."&lt;SEP&gt;"Unified Socialist Republic of Vietnam formed in 1976 after the merger of the north and south; socialist federation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnam is a Southeast Asian country that transitioned from being among the world's poorest nations to a middle-income economy within a generation; it faces territorial disputes with Cambodia and China along land borders and in the South China Sea, and pursues modernization and evolving diplomatic relations, including with the United States."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnam is a Southeast Asian country with a long, multi-ethnic history; in modern times it experienced wars with neighbors, major reforms (Đổi Mời in 1986), and diplomacy including normalization with China in 1991 and with the United States in 1995."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnam; the homeland of nationalist and revolutionary movements described in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam is a Southeast Asian country with a long coastline along the South China Sea; geographically strategic in Southeast Asia and historically a cradle of diverse cultures and civilizations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam is the nation at the center of these independence and reform movements, undergoing political and social upheaval during the late 19th and early 20th centuries."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nation that is the focus of the historical events described, including the Nam Kỳ uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnam is described as transforming from one of the world’s poorest countries to a middle-income economy within one generation; it continues to face territorial disputes (notably with Cambodia on land borders and with China in the South China Sea); it has risen into an industrialized nation and is considered a rising regional power; it is listed among the Next Eleven economies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnam is the Southeast Asian country central to this narrative, with major strategic involvement in Cambodia (1979–1989), enduring border tensions with China, a long period of Soviet-style influence, and a later Đổi Mới reform era leading to integration into the global economy (WTO)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam is the Vietnamese language name for the country Vietnam, central to the historiography discussed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam is the country organizing the first general election in 1946 and becomes the central arena of the early independence movement and postwar diplomacy in Indochina."&lt;SEP&gt;"Country in which the movement, art, and cultural institutions described are situated; the dance drama is described as one of Vietnam's classics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quốc gia thống nhất sau 1975, gia nhập Comecon năm 1978 và xây dựng liên minh với Liên Xô."&lt;SEP&gt;"The country where these historical events occurred and the independence movement unfolded."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnam as a historical and geographic context appears throughout the document."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnam is a Southeast Asian country whose early history references ancient polities such as Văn Lang, Âu Lạc, and Hồng Bàng in the region’s historical narrative."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam is a Southeast Asian country that has transformed from one of the world's poorest nations to a middle-income economy within a generation; it is industrializing and integrated into the global economy, and faces territorial disputes with neighbors."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam is a country repeatedly referenced as the focus of these historical works and sources."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam is referenced as the northern region involved in Cham Pa conflicts and Vietnamese incursions described in the period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam is the nation-state central to the Nguyễn dynasty era and the Tây Sơn conflicts described in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam, the broader nation-state context for these dynasties and invasions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vương quốc/nhà nước xã hội chủ nghĩa Việt Nam sau 1976, liên minh với Liên Xô và gia nhập Comecon năm 1978."&lt;SEP&gt;"Country/region in Southeast Asia; site of early rice farming, two ancient burial sites Mán Bạc and Núi Nấp in the north, and broader prehistoric development."&lt;SEP&gt;"The country in Southeast Asia where Catholic missions, dynastic politics, linguistic reforms, and cultural transformations unfolded across the 17th–18th centuries."&lt;SEP&gt;"The country of Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The country undergoing revolutionary change and eventual independence."&lt;SEP&gt;"The country where the described historical events occur; the setting of the Nguyễn and Tây Sơn conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"The unified Socialist Republic of Vietnam, formed in 1976 after reunification of the north and south.&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnam is a Southeast Asian country central to the narrative, involved in border disputes with China and Cambodia, and pursuing Đổi Mới reforms to restructure its economy and society."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnam, a Southeast Asian country under French protectorates and colonies, central to nationalist movements and the Indochina Federation context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnam, the country central to anti-colonial struggles and WWII-era events described."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam is a Southeast Asian country discussed in a historical overview, characterized by a diverse population, a history of regional conflicts, and later economic reforms (Đổi Mới)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam is a Southeast Asian country that transformed from one of the world's poorest nations into a middle-income economy within a generation; it faces territorial disputes with neighboring states and engages actively in regional and global diplomacy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam is the country central to the historical Vietnam War and many referenced works."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam, the young nation undergoing independence, elections, and a transition to a democratic state during the events described."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c715893072e90e3f1536ec249629f939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c272541ed6794431c00e6ca7ce7f4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f9c5b331f58bfebf51e7a9e8de7972&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fff36ca9411ec2bf48b0a366dd7589f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61f2f053f1c7eb75498d3b31a687c2e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bcac0a2ed12c8c14d19b01dcb829b2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b60881720494b2801d1866f2d58b8eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc55d62fe8c98a4db1992322d16a5280&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa6f1c379fafd63c9e5491579d159fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e1e11930ab01fb061749acffe85b755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ebc4b0a454e2ea3adc019759de704c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df404f664a0d60546a9e31eb5bdff3ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4820a393047d71ddfc9dd25445f040a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e0d2b12d6dc3ace3b2354166252a164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e0ff15a988913e85c0426ea19d7198</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐÔNG NAM Á&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ancient DNA from Cồn Cổ Ngựa shows affinity with East Asian populations."&lt;|"archaeogenetics, ancestry"&lt;SEP&gt;"East Asia, the broader region referenced as the source context for early agricultural populations before their diffusion toward Southeast Asia."&lt;SEP&gt;"Name given to Thăng Long after renaming during Hồ Quý Ly's reign."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southeast Asia region encompassing parts of present-day Vietnam, Laos, Cambodia, Thailand and surrounding areas; context for Bronze Age cultures like Đông Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southeast Asia, the region that received the diffusion of wet rice agriculture and housed continental and island Southeast Asian farming communities described in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southeast Asia; destination region for Neolithic agricultural dispersal and subsequent migrations described in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southeast Asian region that provides the broader geographic frame for migrations and cultural development in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"The later name for the Đại Việt capital after Thăng Long."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đông Nam Á is the Southeast Asian region in which Vietnam is situated, a zone of strategic trade routes and historical interactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đông Nam Á is the Southeast Asian region that includes Việt Nam and serves as a crossroads for trade and cultural exchange."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đông Nam Á is the Southeast Asian region where Vietnam is located; it has long served as a geographic and geopolitical arena for trade, empire-building, and cultural exchange."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đông Nam Á is the geographic region of Southeast Asia referenced as the broader context for Vietnam in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đông Đô is the renamed Thăng Long after reforms by Hồ Quý Ly."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c272541ed6794431c00e6ca7ce7f4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc55d62fe8c98a4db1992322d16a5280&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df404f664a0d60546a9e31eb5bdff3ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VĂN HÓA ĐÔNG SƠN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bronze Age cultural complex in the Red River Delta region; referenced as part of prehistoric Vietnamese cultures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bronze Age culture (around 1000 BCE – 100 CE) linked with early Vietnamese polities and artifacts like drums and bronze tools."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bronze Age culture of Vietnam (roughly 1000 BCE – 100 CE); originated in the northern region with roots traced to northern Vietnam, Guangxi (Quảng Tây), and Laos; known for advanced bronze metallurgy and artifacts such as drums.&lt;SEP&gt;"Bronze Age culture of Vietnam; originates in northern Vietnam and is linked to Văn Lang, Âu Lạc, and Hồng Bàng."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dong Son Culture (c. 1000 BCE–100 CE), a major bronze-working culture in northern Vietnam with distinctive artifacts and social complexity."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Đông Sơn Culture emerged in the Vietnamese region, centered on wet-rice agriculture and complex societal structures around the Văn Lang and Âu Lạc periods, marking an early high point in indigenous cultural development."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Đông Sơn culture emerged in northern Vietnam, based on intensive wet-rice agriculture and centered around local tribes during the Văn Lang–Âu Lạc period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Văn hóa Đông Sơn is the Bronze Age culture that emerged based on intensive wet-rice agriculture around the indigenous communities of the Văn Lang and Âu Lạc period, marking a major cultural formation in ancient Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đông Sơn culture was a Bronze Age cultural tradition centered around northern Vietnam, foundational to later agricultural and societal development in the region."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c272541ed6794431c00e6ca7ce7f4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc55d62fe8c98a4db1992322d16a5280</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRIỆU ĐÀ DIỆT NƯỚC ÂU LẠC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"In 179 BCE, Triệu Đà conquered the state of Âu Lạc, triggering a shift in regional power and the emergence of Nam Việt under his rule."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NAM VIỆT XÂM LƯỢC BỞI NHÀ HÁN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"In 111 BCE, the Han dynasty invaded Nam Việt, incorporating or dominating large parts of the region and establishing long-term Chinese influence."&lt;SEP&gt;"In 111 BCE, the Han dynasty invaded Nam Việt, integrating large parts of the region into Chinese influence."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐINH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Đinh dynasty was a native Vietnamese ruling house following the early era, shaping political consolidation after the decline of older kingdoms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TIỀN LÊ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tiền Lê dynasty followed the Đinh era, governing parts of northern and central Vietnam and influencing state formation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÝ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lý dynasty was a major medieval Vietnamese ruling house known for administrative and cultural developments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRẦN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Trần dynasty was a prominent medieval Vietnamese ruling family noted for defending against northern incursions and fostering centralization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trần dynasty, a major ruling house that continued state consolidation and resisted external pressures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HỒ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hồ dynasty was a Vietnamese ruling line during a period of regional complexity and fracturing authority."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HẬU LÊ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hậu Lê dynasty continued Lê prowess and reasserted native governance in later medieval to early modern Vietnam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MẠC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mạc dynasty represented a competing ruling house in Vietnam during a turbulent era of dynastic contestation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÊ TRUNG HƯNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lê Trung hưng refers to the restored Lê leadership in a late medieval/early modern reshaping of the Vietnamese state."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Lê restoration period (Lê trung hưng) during which the Lê dynasty remained the nominal ruler with real power held by regional lords; marked by consolidation after Mạc collapse."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TÂY SƠN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A peasant-led rebellion in 18th-century central Vietnam that rose to challenge the Nguyễn lords and later expanded its influence across southern and northern regions, culminating in significant military campaigns and political changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"A peasant-military faction in central Vietnam that rose to challenge the Nguyễn lords, built a large rebel army drawn from various social groups, and seized political power in the late 18th century; led by figures such as Nguyễn Nhạc and Nguyễn Huệ, culminating in the capture of the capital Thăng Long and the establishment of their rule in Phú Xuân (Huế)."&lt;SEP&gt;"A rebel movement that emerged in central Vietnam in the late 18th century, mobilizing peasants, artisans, religious groups and various communities to challenge the Nguyễn lords and expand regional power."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dynastic faction opposing Nguyễn Ánh; controlled parts of Vietnam and produced emperors like Nguyễn Quang Toản (Cảnh Thịnh Hoàng đế)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tây Sơn dynasty emerged from a peasant uprising and briefly ruled Vietnam, reorganizing political power in the late 18th century."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tây Sơn was a peasant-origin insurgent movement that rose to power in central and northern Vietnam, eventually ruling under the leadership of Nguyễn Nhạc and Nguyễn Huệ."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tây Sơn là phong trào quần chúng và lực lượng quân sự do Nguyễn Nhạc và Nguyễn Huệ lãnh đạo, sau này thiết lập quyền lực trên nhiều tỉnh miền Trung và Nam Việt Nam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tây Sơn dynasty, a historical Vietnamese ruling dynasty mentioned in the historical narrative."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tây Sơn was the ruling dynasty opposed to Nguyễn Ánh, whose internal strife fueled the conflict."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dynasty/faction opposing Nguyễn Ánh; ruled central Vietnam prior to the Nguyễn dynasty's victory in 1802."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRỊNH–NGUYỄN PHÂN TRANH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A prolonged 17th–18th century civil war between northern Trịnh lords and southern Nguyễn lords that defined political borders and regional power in Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"A prolonged civil war (1627–1672) between the Trịnh and Nguyễn clans over control of Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"A prolonged civil war in 17th–18th century Vietnam between the Trịnh and Nguyễn lords that shaped the country's regional power structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"A prolonged political-military conflict (1600–1777) dividing Vietnam into two domains: Đàng Ngoại under the Trịnh and Đàng Trong under the Nguyễn."&lt;SEP&gt;"A protracted civil war between the Trịnh Lords in the north and the Nguyễn Lords in the south during the 17th–18th centuries, leading to regional division and power shifts."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Trịnh–Nguyễn partition war (spanning roughly 1600–1672) between the Trịnh lords in the North and the Nguyễn lords in the South, culminating in a division of the country into Đàng Ngoài and Đàng Trong."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Trịnh–Nguyễn phân tranh was the prolonged north–south feud between the Trịnh lords in Đàng Ngoài and the Nguyễn lords in Đàng Trong, shaping political divisions in Vietnam from 1600 to 1775."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh–Nguyễn phân tranh is a dynastic conflict between northern and southern Vietnamese powers during the period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh–Nguyễn phân tranh was a major civil-war-era power struggle between rival Vietnamese noble families in the 17th–18th centuries."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NHÀ TỐNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dynasty that followed Đông Ngô and captured Giao Chỉ from 266 to 271."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jin dynasty, the regime that followed Wei; captured and controlled Giao Chỉ at certain points, contributing to the end of the Tam Quốc period in that theater."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Trần (Trần Dynasty) was the ruling dynasty of Đại Việt described here; it led wars against Champa, faced internal power struggles, and ultimately weakened under late medieval pressures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Trần is a Vietnamese dynasty known for defending against Mongol invasions and strengthening centralized authority."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Trần is the dynasty that followed the Lý, continuing the Vietnamese dynastic sequence."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Tấn là triều đại kế tục của Tào Ngụy, đánh dấu sự kết thúc của thời kỳ Tam Quốc và thống nhất phần lớn đất nước."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Tấn succeeded the Tào Ngụy as the ruling dynasty after the Wei era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Tần (Qin dynasty) is the Chinese imperial power referenced as the origin of the campaigns that shaped early Bắc thuộc-era dynamics in the region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Tống (Song Dynasty) was the Chinese dynasty that invaded Đại Cồ Việt and later sent emissaries to normalize relations in 986, 990, and 998."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Tống (Song dynasty) was the northern dynasty that launched invasions into Đại Cồ Việt during this period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Tống is the Song dynasty of China, which invaded Đại Cồ Việt and engaged in diplomacy with Vietnamese courts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Tống refers to the Song Dynasty of China, listed among external powers that interacted with or invaded Vietnam in historical periods."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Tống was the Song dynasty neighboring Đại Việt; it conducted invasions and conflicts with Đại Việt during the period described."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Tống was the Song dynasty, the imperial power that invaded the region, prompting Đại Việt's defensive campaigns."&lt;SEP&gt;"Qin dynasty of China; cited as the imperial power whose generals/histories influence the northern frontier and the early Bắc thuộc context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Song dynasty (Nhà Tống) of China, contemporaneous with Vietnamese polities and involved in regional power dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Song dynasty of China; Vietnam maintained tributary relations with it while asserting autonomy."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Jin Dynasty that followed Cao Wei; took control of Giao Chỉ from 266 to 271 CE, marking a shift in imperial governance over the region."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Qin dynasty (Nhà Tần) of China; Triệu Đà previously served as a general under this dynasty before founding Nam Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Song Dynasty (Nhà Tống), the northern Chinese regime involved in conflicts with Đại Việt during the 11th century."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Song Dynasty of China (960–1279), the dynasty that exercised suzerainty over the region and to which Tĩnh Hải quân reportedly paid tribute, influencing regional governance."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Trần dynasty ruled Đại Việt during the 13th–14th centuries, overseeing the realm during earlier conflicts with Champa and undergoing decline toward the end of the era."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Trần dynasty, established after the dynastic transition and central to Đại Việt's history in this period."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Trần dynasty, established through dynastic transition and governance starting from Trần Cảnh's ascent in Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Trịnh family established northern dominance during the Trịnh–Nguyễn era, often controlling the government in practice."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trần Dynasty, one of the successive Vietnamese dynasties mentioned in the historical sequence."&lt;SEP&gt;"nhà Tống (Song Dynasty), the Chinese dynasty that invaded Đại Cồ Việt in 979 and engaged in subsequent diplomacy and conflict."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đế chế phong kiến Trung Hoa, đối thủ chính của Đại Việt trong chiến tranh biên giới 1075–1076."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01995274690fe1835d7836e3a481a678&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4943e96cb569799b168fc03f9b7f0d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NGUYÊN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nguyên (Yuan Dynasty) was a Chinese-Mongol dynasty that had interactions with Vietnamese polities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyên refers to the Yuan Dynasty of China, involved in campaigns and influence in northern Vietnam and the broader region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn dynasty was the last Vietnamese imperial house, ruling until French colonial domination and shaping the path to modernization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yuan dynasty of China (Nguyên), listed among the invading powers affecting the region's history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yuan dynasty of China; its campaigns and influence impacted northern Vietnam and regional geopolitics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHĂM PA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cham kingdom lineage following Lâm Ấp; prominent cultural and religious center in ancient Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cham people; ethnolinguistic group in central and southern Vietnam; origin from Nam Đảo languages."&lt;SEP&gt;"Champa was a central coastal kingdom interacting with Vietnamese polities through trade, conflict, and cultural exchange."&lt;SEP&gt;"Champa; historical region that interacted with Nguyễn expansion and Khmer influence in the Mekong Delta area."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa (Cham Pa) is a historic kingdom in what is now central Vietnam; it prospered from spice and silk trade, with capitals at Simhapura and later Vijaya, and endured prolonged conflicts with the Khmer Empire and Vietnamese states."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa (Cham Pa) was a historic Champa kingdom in central Vietnam with a rich cultural and religious heritage and frequent conflict with neighboring powers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa (Cham Pa) was an Indianized kingdom in what is now central and southern Vietnam, with capitals at Simhapura and later Vijaya."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa (Champa) là vương quốc cổ ở miền Trung và miền Nam Việt Nam, đối thủ truyền kiếp của Khmer và tham gia vào các mối quan hệ quân sự và thương mại với đế quốc Khmer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa (Champa) was a historic polity in central/southern Vietnam that interacted with Vietnamese states."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa (Champa) was a historical kingdom in central Vietnam that clashed with Khmer power and developed extensive maritime trade networks, including links to the Philippines."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa (Champa) was a historical kingdom in central Vietnam that interacted with Vietnamese polities and contested borderlands."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa (Champa) was a historical kingdom in central/southern Vietnam that conducted military campaigns against the Khmer and engaged in long-distance trade with the Philippines."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa (Champa) was a historical southern kingdom that interacted with Đại Việt, including marriage diplomacy and shifting status."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa (Champa) was a regional entity involved in migrations and conflicts shaping southern Vietnam history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa (Champa) was the Champa kingdom that engaged in military actions against Đại Cồ Việt and faced invasions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa (Champa) was the Champa kingdom that engaged in military actions against Đại Cồ Việt during this period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa (Champa) was the kingdom ruled by Paramesvaravarman I that attacked Đại Cồ Việt in 979."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa (Champa), a neighboring polity involved in attacks on Đại Cồ Việt and in diplomacy with Vietnamese courts during this period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa (Champa), kingdom in the south that attacked Đại Việt and faced Đại Việt during the era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa is a historical Cham polity in the central-southern region of present-day Vietnam. In 1312 it became a vassal of Đại Việt, later regaining independence and waging a long, ~30-year war to reclaim lands; Cham forces under king Chế Bồng Nga (1360–1390) defeated Đại Việt and killed Trần Duệ Tông at Vijaya in 1377, with repeated raids on Thăng Long and significant impact on Đại Việt's economy and power."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa is a historical kingdom (Champa) located in the region of modern central to southern Vietnam; it repeatedly clashed with Đại Việt, conducted military campaigns to regain autonomy, and maintained regional power through wars and period of resistance against Vietnamese domination."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa is a historical kingdom in central-southern Vietnam referenced as a region affected by Nguyễn expansion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa là vương quốc cổ Champa phát triển từ Lâm Ấp, nằm ở miền Trung Việt Nam, nổi bật về văn hóa và chữ viết bản địa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa was a central-southern kingdom that attacked Đại Việt and allied with Khmer in southern campaigns (1128, 1132)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa was a historic kingdom in central and southern Vietnam, often in conflict with Đại Việt; it at times became a vassal and later fought to reclaim lands, led by Cham rulers such as Chế Bồng Nga."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa was a southern kingdom that engaged in military actions in the region and faced Đại Việt during the period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa, a historic kingdom (Champa) in what is now central Vietnam; a rival to the Khmer Empire, engaged in regional trade and conflict, and opened trade with the Philippines by the early 13th century."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa, the Kingdom of Champa, located in central and southern Vietnam with historical ties, trade, and conflicts with Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chăm Pa, the historical Champa kingdom in central and southern Vietnam, became a vassal of Đại Việt in 1312, later regained independence after about a decade, and waged a long war (roughly 30 years) to reclaim its lands; its ruler Chế Bồng Nga led campaigns against Đại Việt and stormed key sites, including the killing of Trần Duệ Tông at Vijaya in 1377."&lt;SEP&gt;"Early Champa kingdom in central Vietnam; develops from earlier Cham polities and becomes a prominent cultural and political entity in the region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historic Champa kingdom in central Vietnam; at times a vassal to Đại Việt (1312), regained independence about a decade later, and waged a prolonged 30-year war to reclaim lands; capital Vijaya; involved in invasions of Đại Việt including attacks on Thăng Long."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical Champa region whose lands were affected by Nguyễn expansion and Vietnamese settlement."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical kingdom in central/southern Vietnam, involved in dynastic alliances with Đại Việt and later vassal status."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kingdom of Champa in central Vietnam; engaged in major conflicts with Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kingdom of Champa in central Vietnam; target of Lê Thánh Tông's campaigns.&lt;SEP&gt;"Kingdom of Champa in central-southern Vietnam, repeatedly in conflict with Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kingdom of Champa, a Cham polity in central Vietnam with Simhapura as its capital during parts of its history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kingdom of Champa, located in central-southern Vietnam, repeatedly targeted by Lê dynasty campaigns."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kingdom of Champa; allied with Mai Thúc Loan and other polities in the uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kingdom of Champa; allied with Mai Thúc Loan during the rebellion.&lt;SEP&gt;"Vương quốc Chăm Pa ở miền nam Trung bộ; đối đầu với Đại Việt trong các chiến dịch vào các năm 1128 và 1132."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-561d10717f3ff788e1032021a4cf0614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc55d62fe8c98a4db1992322d16a5280&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4943e96cb569799b168fc03f9b7f0d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MINH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ming Dynasty, ruling dynasty of China; opponent of Đại Việt during the period described.&lt;SEP&gt;"Ming dynasty of China; its campaigns and policies influenced northern Vietnam and the broader regional order."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ming Dynasty of China invaded Đại Ngu in 1407, conquered and integrated it into the Ming Empire, marking a harsh period of foreign domination."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ming Dynasty of China, the ruling empire opposing Lê Lợi's rebellion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Minh Dynasty of China; its campaigns affected Vietnam and the regional balance of power."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ming dynasty of China (Minh), listed among the invading powers mentioned in the historical account."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ming dynasty; Chinese dynasty that briefly occupied parts of Đại Việt before Lê restoration."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ming dynasty, the ruling empire against which the Lam Sơn uprising occurred."&lt;SEP&gt;"Minh Dynasty was a Chinese-led dynasty that at times influenced or clashed with Vietnamese territories."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ming dynasty, the Chinese imperial regime that ruled northern Vietnam and opposed the Lam Sơn uprising."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-561d10717f3ff788e1032021a4cf0614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;XIÊM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Historical Siam; provided military support to Nguyễn Ánh against the Tây Sơn in 1799."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kingdom of Siam (Thailand); a foreign power that provided military support to Nguyễn Ánh, including sending a substantial army to assist in Vietnam during the late 18th century."&lt;SEP&gt;"Siam (Thailand); a neighboring power with historical interactions—trade, conflict, and diplomacy—with Vietnamese polities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Siam (Xiêm), a Southeast Asian kingdom named among external powers impacting the region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Siam, a Southeast Asian kingdom that conducted invasions affecting Chân Lạp and regional dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Siam; external power that clashed with Vietnamese expansion and was involved in regional conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Xiêm (Siam) is described as an adversary in military conflicts where Nguyễn forces repelled Siamese invasions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Xiêm (Siam) is the historic kingdom that provided support to Nguyễn Ánh during campaigns against the Tây Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Xiêm (Siam) was a regional neighbor involved in historical interactions with Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Xiêm (Siam/Thailand) was a regional power in Southeast Asia that interacted with neighboring polities, including Vietnam.&lt;SEP&gt;"Xiêm La (Siam) là vương quốc Đông Nam Á đã được Nguyễn Ánh tìm kiếm viện trợ quân sự để đánh Tây Sơn; sau này có liên hệ quân sự và các lực lượng được huy động từ đây."&lt;SEP&gt;"Xiêm La (Siam) provided refuge and military support to Nguyễn Ánh during his bid to regain power against the Tây Sơn."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THANH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Qing dynasty (Thanh in Vietnamese), the successor to Ming, involved in East Asian regional dynamics affecting Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thanh refers to the Qing Dynasty in Vietnamese historical texts; it is listed among imperial powers that shaped northern Vietnam’s history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thanh Dynasty refers to the early post-medieval Vietnamese ruling period preceding the Tây Sơn and Nguyễn eras."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐỒNG BẰNG SÔNG CỬU LONG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mekong Delta region in southern Vietnam; a major geographic area shaping historical polities and regional dynamics in the southern part of Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mekong Delta region mentioned as a target area in Đại Việt expansion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mekong Delta, the fertile southern Vietnamese plain that became significant during Nam tiến and later cross-border interactions with Cambodia."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Mekong Delta, mentioned as a region targeted during Đại Việt campaigns."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đồng bằng sông Cửu Long (Mekong Delta) is a southern Vietnam region involved in Nguyễn expansion and Khmer-Champa interactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Mekong Delta region in southern Vietnam (and parts of Cambodia), a key area affected by the Nam tiến expansion."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Mekong Delta in southern Vietnam, a region tied to Khmer and Khmer-influenced polities in historical context."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Mekong Delta region in southern Vietnam, a key geographic area mentioned in relation to the Nam tiến expansion into broader Southeast Asia."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đồng bằng sông Cửu Long (Mekong Delta) is the region that Nguyễn expansion aimed to control, including lands around Saigon."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mekong Delta region where Nguyễn expansion and related movements occurred."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đồng bằng sông Cửu Long (Mekong Delta) is a key geographic region in southern Vietnam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-561d10717f3ff788e1032021a4cf0614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc55d62fe8c98a4db1992322d16a5280</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CAMPUCHIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cambodia borders Vietnam along the land frontier and is involved in ongoing territorial disputes with Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia hosted Vietnamese military presence from 1979 to 1989 following regional conflicts, became a focal point of Vietnamese-Cambodian and Sino-Vietnamese interactions, and was a setting for Vietnam's withdrawal and stabilization efforts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia, a neighboring country involved in border security actions linked to Vietnamese defense efforts during the late 1970s through the 1980s.&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia, neighboring country affected by spillover."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia; neighboring region to southern Vietnam and part of the broader historical arc of cross-border and cultural exchange."&lt;SEP&gt;"Campuchia is the Cambodian territory incorporated into Indochina under French rule in the late 19th century."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia, a neighboring country with which Vietnam shares a land border, is mentioned in the text in the context of border disputes and regional geopolitics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia, neighboring Vietnam, site of conflicts with Vietnam during 1978–1989 as Vietnam defended its southwestern border."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia, neighboring Vietnam; site of historical interactions and border and regional security considerations with Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia; site of cross-border clashes and the Cambodian conflict involving Vietnamese forces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia; the realm of the Khmer Empire and the seat of Cambodian kings."&lt;SEP&gt;"Campuchia refers to Cambodia; it was incorporated into French Indochina and later became part of the colonial federation system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia, region incorporated into French Indochina; later components of the federation in this period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia; site of Vietnamese border defense and regional security events (1978–1989)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia is Việt Nam's neighbor along the land border, involved in border disputes with Việt Nam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia, neighboring Vietnam; site of border clashes and the Cambodian–Vietnamese conflict following Khmer Rouge rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia; Vietnam’s military presence there as part of international obligations from 1979 to 1989, followed by withdrawal to stabilize the region.&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia; referenced as the modern country connected to Khmer populations and historical Khmer polities in the Mekong Delta region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia; site of Vietnam's border defense conflicts from 1978–1989."&lt;SEP&gt;"Campuchia (Cambodia) is mentioned as a region within the broader sphere of influence in the campaigns and diplomacy of the era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quốc gia láng giềng với Việt Nam, nơi diễn ra xung đột biên giới và can thiệp quân sự của Việt Nam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia, Vietnam's neighbor, with territorial border disputes along the land frontier."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia, location of the border conflicts with Vietnam from 1978 to 1989."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia, where Vietnam conducted a decade-long military presence (1979–1989) as part of regional security and international duties."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia; region absorbed into Indochina during the late 19th century."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia; region where Nguyễn Ánh's influence and Vietnamese regional power extended."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia; region with Nam Á language speakers; cited in relation to ancestral connections."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cambodia; site of Khmer Rouge governance and cross-border border tensions with Vietnam.&lt;SEP&gt;"Campuchia (Cambodia) was the site of border tensions and conflicts involving Vietnam, notably the 1978–1989 period."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e1e11930ab01fb061749acffe85b755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4549a0a33a97f39084dfcd2d857e18f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4820a393047d71ddfc9dd25445f040a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3e2d86a8a15344ff5c25829117f928c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df404f664a0d60546a9e31eb5bdff3ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e0ff15a988913e85c0426ea19d7198&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc55d62fe8c98a4db1992322d16a5280</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIÊN BANG ĐÔNG DƯƠNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A federation of Indochina under French colonial rule; dissolved in 1954, its breakup paved the way for Vietnamese independence movements and the eventual division of Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"A political federation (1887–1945) of French protectorates and colonies in Indochina, encompassing Vietnam, Cambodia, and Laos; the central administrative and political framework under colonial rule during the period described in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Federation of French Indochina; dissolved after the 1954 decolonization developments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indochina Federation, a historic colonial-era political entity that was dissolved by decolonization by 1954."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indochina Federation, a political union formed by French protectorates and colonies in Indochina (including Vietnam) in the late 19th century, centralizing colonial administration under France."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indochina Federation, the French colonial political framework comprising Vietnam, Laos, and Cambodia under colonial rule (1887 onward)."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Indochina Federation created in 1887, including Vietnam's protectorates and later Cambodia and Laos."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Indochina Federation was a French colonial-era political entity comprising Vietnam, Laos, and Cambodia; it was dissolved in 1954, marking the end of formal colonial rule in the region."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Indochina Federation, a political union of French colonial entities in Vietnam, Laos, and Cambodia established in the late 19th century, shaping colonial administration and regional governance."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Indochina Federation, whose dissolution in 1954 marked a turning point in regional decolonization and state formation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indochina Federation; a colonial political entity governing Vietnam, Laos, and Cambodia, officially dissolved on 30 December 1954."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indochina Federation; the colonial entity into which Vietnam was absorbed under French rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Liên bang Đông Dương is a colonial federation comprising protectorates and colonies in the Indochina region under French rule; by 1887 these territories formally became constituent parts of the federation, shaping administrative control and imperial governance in Vietnam and neighboring areas."&lt;SEP&gt;"The French Indochina federation; later dissolved following the Indochina War."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Indochina Federation (Liên bang Đông Dương) was established by France in the late 19th century, consolidating control over Nam Kỳ, Bắc Kỳ, Trung Kỳ, and later incorporating Cambodia and Laos."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Indochina Federation established by France in 1887, encompassing Vietnamese, Cambodian, and Lao territories under colonial rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Indochina Federation, a French-led political entity comprising An Nam, Bắc Kỳ and Nam Kỳ, later extending to include Cambodia and Laos."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indochina Federation; a French colonial political entity into which Vietnam was incorporated in the colonial era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Liên bang Đông Dương là tổ chức hành chính liên bang của Pháp tại Đông Dương, trước khi các địa bàn bị giải thể."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Federation of Indochina was the French colonial political entity governing Vietnam, Laos, and Cambodia; it was formally dissolved on December 30, 1954."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indochina Federation created by France, including Vietnam, Laos, Cambodia; later expanded to include more territories."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indochina Federation, the colonial political unit under French rule that encompassed Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indochina Federation; dissolved in the 1950s as Indochina transitioned during decolonization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Liên bang Đông Dương (Indochina Federation) was the French colonial framework that administered Vietnam and neighboring regions starting in 1887."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Indochina Federation, a colonial-era political union comprising French protectorates and colonies in what is now Vietnam, Laos, and Cambodia, established under French imperial administration."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c715893072e90e3f1536ec249629f939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3e2d86a8a15344ff5c25829117f928c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ebc4b0a454e2ea3adc019759de704c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bcac0a2ed12c8c14d19b01dcb829b2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHÁP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"France and its colonial forces, the primary opposing power in the uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, a European colonial power, invaded and colonized Việt Nam, establishing French Indochina and ruling the territory for a lengthy period."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial imperial power in Indochina described as brutal in rule yet promoting Westernized modernization and education reforms in Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power in Indochina and ally in various postwar negotiations."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power in Indochina, central to military actions (e.g., bombing Haiphong) and postwar negotiations with Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power in Indochina, signed and pressured the Geneva Accords era; its forces were withdrawn following the dissolution of Indochina and the accords."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power involved in Indochina and party to Geneva Accords."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power that invaded Vietnam and conducted military operations in the region."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power whose forces and policies dominated Indochina and spurred Vietnamese resistance, including exile of monarchs."&lt;SEP&gt;"France; the colonial power that established French Indochina and dominated Vietnam for decades, shaping the subsequent independence movements."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pháp (France) is the colonial power that expanded control over Vietnam, directing campaigns and treaties in Indochina."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power in Indochina attempting to reassert control after World War II."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power in Indochina that violently suppressed the Nam Kỳ uprising, executing and repressing many participants."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power in Indochina; main opponent of the Nam Kỳ uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power in Indochina; responsible for military actions such as the Hải Phòng bombardment and for later negotiations and treaties during the First Indochina War."&lt;SEP&gt;"France; colonial power in Indochina; withdrew post-1954 following military defeat in Vietnam and Geneva Accords.&lt;SEP&gt;"France; the colonial power ruling Indochina and the primary adversary of Vietnamese reformers and revolutionaries."&lt;SEP&gt;"France; the colonial power that launched the invasion of Vietnam and supported campaigns in the 19th century."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pháp (France) là quốc gia châu Âu có sự tham gia thông qua Bá Đa Lộc trong nỗ lực viện trợ cho Nguyễn Ánh; tác động của Cách mạng Pháp khiến kế hoạch bị gián đoạn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pháp (France) was the colonial power dominating Vietnam, eliciting nationalist resistance and driving reform movements seeking independence and modernization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pháp (France) was the colonial power suppressing the Nam Kỳ khởi nghĩa and engaging in military action against the rebels."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, represented by French forces that supported Nguyễn Ánh during the late 18th-century campaigns against the Tây Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power in Indochina; responsible for the crackdown on the Nam Kỳ uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power that established control over Vietnam and negotiated treaties with the Nguyễn court."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power that invaded Vietnam and, later, led to the creation of French Indochina in 1887, shaping much of modern Vietnam's history."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power whose military actions against targets like Hải Phòng shape the early Indochina conflict."&lt;SEP&gt;"France; colonial ruler of Indochina; participant in Geneva Accords; withdrew forces by 1956."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pháp (France) is the colonial power occupying the outposts at Phai Khắt and Nà Ngần, whose French troops were the opponents in these battles.&lt;SEP&gt;"France, a European power involved diplomatically with Nguyễn Ánh through the Versailles process; represented by figures such as Louis XVI and Bá Đa Lộc."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, colonial power in Indochina and an actor in postwar negotiations.&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power in Indochina responsible for brutal rule and promoting Western modernization in education and governance."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power ruling Indochina and the main adversary in the Indochina wars; withdrew following the 1954 accords."&lt;SEP&gt;"France; colonial power that eventually colonized Indochina and dissolved traditional monarchies."&lt;SEP&gt;"French colonial authorities who suppressed the uprising and conducted arrests and bombardments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pháp (France) provided support to Nguyễn Ánh during the campaigns against Tây Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"The French colonial authorities who suppressed the Nam Kỳ uprising and prosecuted revolutionaries."&lt;SEP&gt;"The French colonial state and army, the principal adversary of the uprising, which brutally suppressed rebels and bombed villages."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, a European country that colonized parts of Indochina and exercised imperial authority over Vietnam during the period described."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power in Indochina that brutally suppressed the Nam Kỳ khởi nghĩa and killed many participants."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power in Indochina, whose rule in Vietnam was challenged during the August Revolution."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power in Indochina; it attacked Hải Phòng, influenced political alignments, and negotiated with Vietnamese leaders."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power that established and administered the Indochina Federation and exerted pressure on Vietnamese nationalist movements."&lt;SEP&gt;"France, the colonial power that later colonized Vietnam as part of Indochina."&lt;SEP&gt;"France; colonial power in Indochina; signed the Geneva Accords and withdrew following the conflict."&lt;SEP&gt;"France; colonial power whose treaties with Vietnam are annulled and whose influence is rejected in the speech."&lt;SEP&gt;"French forces that backed Nguyễn Ánh in his campaigns against the Tây Sơn in the late 18th century."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pháp (France) is the colonial power opposed by Việt Minh and central to the anti-colonial struggle described in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pháp (France) was the colonial power that controlled Vietnam within the Indochina framework and shaped its colonial history for decades."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pháp (France), the colonial power in Vietnam against whom Việt Minh fought to achieve independence."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pháp is the French colonial government that ruled Indochina and suppressed the movement."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pháp represents the French colonial empire, the imperial power occupying Vietnam and opposing nationalist movements during the period of the Nam Kỳ khởi nghĩa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quốc gia thực dân và lực lượng chiếm đóng ở Đông Dương; tham gia vào Đông Dương và cố gắng tái chiếm thuộc địa, ký các hiệp định với Việt Minh."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c715893072e90e3f1536ec249629f939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f9c5b331f58bfebf51e7a9e8de7972&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7418e6f1b899c505272caee02f3cf1ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0858721f576cf19954031c342a7d2bff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fff36ca9411ec2bf48b0a366dd7589f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bcac0a2ed12c8c14d19b01dcb829b2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b60881720494b2801d1866f2d58b8eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd3c75fb34fd08ca28a6bdea4defc0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65a62981dbf13b2b72920ca1960170b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-750f8e96d1bcbbd9a06d573268723339&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ebc4b0a454e2ea3adc019759de704c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3e2d86a8a15344ff5c25829117f928c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6322ad53528ea4e193eaf9be34d2898&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e0d2b12d6dc3ace3b2354166252a164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf0b29bf76d345dfa9da0349d9f546d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHÁP XÂM CHIẾM VIỆT NAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"French invasion and colonial domination of Vietnam, leading to incorporation into Indochina and long-term colonial rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"In 1887, France invaded and partitioned Vietnam, bringing it into French Indochina and inaugurating sustained colonial administration."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VIỆT NAM DÂN CHỦ CỘNG HÒA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chính phủ/nhà nước Việt Nam được thành lập sau tuyên ngôn độc lập ngày 2 tháng 9 năm 1945 do Việt Minh lãnh đạo; thủ đô tại Hà Nội."&lt;SEP&gt;"Democratic Republic of Vietnam, the state proclaimed after the August Revolution, led by the Communist Party."&lt;SEP&gt;"North Vietnam's government (1954–1975), the communist-led state that fought against South Vietnam and the United States and aimed to unify Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"North Vietnam's government established in 1945, led by Ho Chi Minh; governed the northern part of Vietnam and played a central role in decolonization and subsequent Indochina conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"North Vietnam's government led by Hồ Chí Minh; capital Hà Nội; backed by China and the USSR; fought the French and later the South."&lt;SEP&gt;"North Vietnam, established in 1945; led by Hồ Chí Minh; governed Hanoi; central to the anti-colonial struggle and later the Vietnam War."&lt;SEP&gt;"North Vietnam; government governing the northern part of Vietnam during the conflict and a precursor to reunification."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern Vietnamese government proclaimed in 1945 and led by Hồ Chí Minh; controlled the North and sought communist alignment; recognized by allied blocs after 1950s."&lt;SEP&gt;"Republic of Vietnam (South Vietnam), the U.S.-backed state from 1955 to 1975 that fought against the DRV and the North Vietnamese forces."&lt;SEP&gt;"South Vietnam's US-backed government (1955–1975) that fought VNDCCH and faced insurgency; collapsed in 1975."&lt;SEP&gt;"South Vietnam's government established after a coup; capital at Sài Gòn; supported by the United States."&lt;SEP&gt;"South Vietnam's state formed in 1955 under Ngô Đình Diệm after partition; based in Saigon and backed by the United States."&lt;SEP&gt;"South Vietnam, anti-communist state formed in 1955 after the division of Vietnam; capital in Saigon; led by Ngô Đình Diệm; opposed to the North."&lt;SEP&gt;"South Vietnamese anti-communist state established in 1955; capital at Saigon; initially led by Bảo Đại, then Ngô Đình Diệm."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Democratic Republic of Vietnam (North Vietnam) established as the state governing northern Vietnam during the anti-colonial struggle; it pursued independence from France and received aid from China and the USSR."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Democratic Republic of Vietnam (North Vietnam), a socialist state established after the First Indochina War; governed the northern part with Ho Chi Minh as leader, receiving aid from the Soviet Union and China."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Democratic Republic of Vietnam (North Vietnam); the northern government during the war, later part of the unified Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Democratic Republic of Vietnam established after the Proclamation on 2 September 1945."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Democratic Republic of Vietnam is the government formed after the August Revolution as the successor to previous administrations, representing Vietnamese independence efforts."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Democratic Republic of Vietnam proclaimed in 1945, leading the struggle for independence and later unification of the country in 1976."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Democratic Republic of Vietnam, established during the independence movement, served as the Vietnamese state and government structure seeking autonomy from French colonial rule and navigating early post-war diplomacy and state-building."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Democratic Republic of Vietnam, established on 2 September 1945, marking the birth of the Vietnamese state."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Democratic Republic of Vietnam, established on September 2, 1945, as the state proclaiming independence."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Democratic Republic of Vietnam, proclaimed in 1945 under Viet Minh leadership, which governed northern Vietnam and laid the groundwork for national reunification."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Democratic Republic of Vietnam, proclaimed in 1945, led by Hồ Chí Minh, and the early core state that began the process toward a unified Vietnamese nation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Democratic Republic of Vietnam; the state proclaimed by Việt Minh in 1945, later known as North Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Democratic Republic of Vietnam; the state proclaimed in 1945–46, which opposed French colonial rule and later received support from the Soviet bloc."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Provisional Government of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam established after independence; leads the government during the ceremony."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Republic of Vietnam (South Vietnam) government during the war, referenced in the context of postwar transitions and population movements."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Republic of Vietnam (South Vietnam), an anti-communist state founded in 1955 under Ngô Đình Diệm; capital in Saigon."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Republic of Vietnam (South Vietnam); government in the south that fell in 1975.&lt;SEP&gt;"The Republic of Vietnam (South Vietnam); the southern government allied with the United States during the war and collapsed on 30 April 1975.&lt;SEP&gt;"The Republic of Vietnam, a U.S.-backed southern state during the Vietnam War era, opposing the northern communist government."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Republic of Vietnam, established after the 1954 partition and backed by the United States during the Vietnam War era."&lt;SEP&gt;"The state formed as the Democratic Republic of Vietnam; government authority during the early postwar period and a central actor in the struggle against colonial rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"The state proclaimed on 2 September 1945 by Ho Chi Minh, representing a new republican government for Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam Cộng hòa (Republic of Vietnam) was the southern anti-communist state backed by the United States after 1954."&lt;SEP&gt;"Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa (Democratic Republic of Vietnam) was proclaimed in 1945, led by Hồ Chí Minh, and became the central body in the struggle for national independence."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0858721f576cf19954031c342a7d2bff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e1e11930ab01fb061749acffe85b755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3c3dccc1bdcf6bd8ef4c192da6a818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4549a0a33a97f39084dfcd2d857e18f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ebc4b0a454e2ea3adc019759de704c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fff36ca9411ec2bf48b0a366dd7589f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf0b29bf76d345dfa9da0349d9f546d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c715893072e90e3f1536ec249629f939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6322ad53528ea4e193eaf9be34d2898</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;QUỐC GIA VIỆT NAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A state proclaimed in 1949, established with capital in Saigon; it created its own army and fought alongside French forces against Việt Minh."&lt;SEP&gt;"State established in the south after the Geneva Agreements; existed from 1954 until it was transformed into Việt Nam Cộng hòa in 1955; led by Bảo Đại and later Ngô Đình Diệm in the south."&lt;SEP&gt;"State of Vietnam, the French-supported political entity established during the First Indochina War as a counterweight to the DRV."&lt;SEP&gt;"The State of Vietnam (Quốc gia Việt Nam) was the French-backed southern state (1949–1955) with Bảo Đại as head of state; its capital was Saigon, preceding the establishment of the Republic of Vietnam (VNCH)."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Vietnamese state formed in the late 1940s with Saigon as a major hub, aligned with France within the Union and seeking greater autonomy."&lt;SEP&gt;"State formed following Franco-Vietnamese negotiations; capital and governance linked to Saigon (Sài Gòn)."&lt;SEP&gt;"State of Vietnam established in 1949, a political entity that preceded later unification processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"State of Vietnam, the anti-communist southern government established during the period; renamed Việt Nam Cộng hòa by Ngô Đình Diệm in 1955."&lt;SEP&gt;"State of Vietnam, the French-backed Vietnamese state that existed before being renamed to Việt Nam Cộng hòa in 1955."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quốc gia Việt Nam (Vietnam National State) operated under French influence and served as a precursor to the later Republic of Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"South Vietnamese state established in the 1950s under French influence; later renamed Việt Nam Cộng hòa, with its capital in Saigon and leadership transitioning from Bảo Đại to Ngô Đình Diệm."&lt;SEP&gt;"The State of Vietnam, a French-aligned state recognized by the United States in 1950, existing alongside Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c715893072e90e3f1536ec249629f939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ebc4b0a454e2ea3adc019759de704c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KHỐI LIÊN HIỆP PHÁP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Khối Liên hiệp Pháp (French Union) was the political bloc within the French imperial system that connected France with its colonies in Indochina."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khối liên hiệp các vùng thuộc địa của Pháp, được thành lập sau chiến tranh khi Pháp áp dụng hiến pháp mới (1946)."&lt;SEP&gt;"The French Union bloc; it allowed a degree of autonomy for Vietnam within its framework."&lt;SEP&gt;"The French Union coalition that governed French Indochina during and after WWII."&lt;SEP&gt;"The French Union framework governing France's colonies, including Indochina."&lt;SEP&gt;"The French Union framework within which France sought to maintain influence over its colonies, including Vietnam during this period."&lt;SEP&gt;"The French Union, the umbrella under which colonial France organized its empire including Vietnam during the late colonial era."&lt;SEP&gt;"The French Union, the framework within which France sought to manage its postwar territories, including Việt Nam.&lt;SEP&gt;"The Union of French Indochina, the colonial framework under which Vietnam was administered during much of the colonial period."&lt;SEP&gt;"The French Union federation that integrated Vietnam into French Indochina, providing the colonial framework and governance structure referenced in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"The French Union, a political framework used in postwar treaties."&lt;SEP&gt;"The French Union, a bloc France used to maintain influence in Indochina."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4ebc4b0a454e2ea3adc019759de704c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6322ad53528ea4e193eaf9be34d2898</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HỒ CHÍ MINH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Vietnamese revolutionary leader who led the Viet Minh, founded the DRV, and played a central role in Vietnam's struggle for independence and unification."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chairman of the Việt Minh who led the movement and read the Declaration of Independence to establish the Democratic Republic of Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hồ Chí Minh was the leader of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam (North Vietnam) and a central figure in reunification and independence efforts; he served as the President of DRV and a key political leader."&lt;SEP&gt;"Leading Vietnamese revolutionary and political figure, central to the independence movement and governance described in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnamese communist leader and the central figure for North Vietnam and unification efforts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chairman of the Việt Minh who led the movement for independence and, on 2 September 1945, represented Việt Minh to proclaim the Declaration of Independence and establish the Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hồ Chí Minh was a key Vietnamese revolutionary leader who guided the Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa's independence effort and later state leadership."&lt;SEP&gt;"Leader of the Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa; key architect of Vietnamese independence and early communist governance; President of DRV."&lt;SEP&gt;"President and revered leader; honored in the renaming of Sài Gòn to Thành phố Hồ Chí Minh (died 1969)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hồ Chí Minh was the Vietnamese revolutionary leader who led the struggle for independence and the establishment of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam in 1945."&lt;SEP&gt;"Leader of Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa; central figure in North Vietnam's governance and the anti-French independence movement."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Ái Quốc, who would become Hồ Chí Minh, a central Vietnamese revolutionary who helped unify Vietnamese communist groups under Comintern guidance and led major independence efforts."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Vietnamese revolutionary leader; the Hồ Chí Minh Prize is named after him and is referenced in relation to the award received by the dance production's organizers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ho Chi Minh, Vietnamese revolutionary leader and president of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam, central figure in Vietnamese independence and governance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ho Chi Minh, a Vietnamese revolutionary and statesman after whom the major southern city was renamed; he died in 1969 and his legacy shaped postwar policy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hồ Chí Minh was the leader and foundational figure of the North Vietnamese state and its independence movement, central to the struggle for reunification and shaping 20th-century Vietnamese history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Leader of Việt Minh who proclaimed independence in 1945."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chairman/President of Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa; central figure in leading the North during the anti-colonial struggle and early Vietnam War."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chairman/President of the Provisional Government of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam who delivers the Declaration of Independence at Ba Đình Square on September 2, 1945."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ho Chi Minh, the prominent Vietnamese revolutionary leader who guided the Việt Minh and the struggle for independence."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hồ Chí Minh is a Vietnamese revolutionary leader who predicted Vietnam's independence and led the nationalist movement, ultimately guiding the Việt Minh in the struggle against colonial rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hồ Chí Minh is the central figure driving Vietnam's independence movement; he planned and decided to read the Tuyên ngôn Độc lập, traveled from Tân Trào to Hanoi, and is identifiable with the alias Nguyễn Ái Quốc in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hồ Chí Minh was the Vietnamese leader whose legacy led to the renaming of Saigon and who died in 1969; a central figure in Vietnamese history and politics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hồ Chí Minh was the Vietnamese revolutionary leader who guided the struggle for independence and the DRV.&lt;SEP&gt;"Lãnh tụ của Mặt trận Việt Minh; chủ trì lễ tuyên ngôn độc lập và vai trò then chốt trong việc thành lập nước VNDCCH."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnamese revolutionary leader who chaired the national congress at Tan Trao and steered preparations for the general uprising in August 1945 as the figurehead of the independence movement."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnamese revolutionary leader, born Nguyễn Ái Quốc; widely known as Hồ Chí Minh; key figure in organizing the Việt Minh."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c715893072e90e3f1536ec249629f939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e1e11930ab01fb061749acffe85b755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3c3dccc1bdcf6bd8ef4c192da6a818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ebc4b0a454e2ea3adc019759de704c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6322ad53528ea4e193eaf9be34d2898&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dae09351baefd2c96da656733c534ccd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0858721f576cf19954031c342a7d2bff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b60881720494b2801d1866f2d58b8eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bfa206adeee22a1ede2ef2ae0ad9711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4549a0a33a97f39084dfcd2d857e18f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fff36ca9411ec2bf48b0a366dd7589f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa6f1c379fafd63c9e5491579d159fc0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HOA KỲ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hoa Kỳ (The United States), the country referenced as sending a welcoming gesture to the newly formed Vietnamese government, exemplified by the P-38 aircraft."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hoa Kỳ (United States) was a major ally supporting the Việt Nam Cộng hòa during the Vietnam War."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hoa Kỳ (the United States) is referenced as one of the Allied powers whose stance is part of the larger geopolitical backdrop."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hoa Kỳ refers to the United States, a major power that acted as a key ally and backer of the Republic of Vietnam (VNCH) during the Vietnam War, providing political support and military aid until the war concluded in 1975."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hoa Kỳ was a major ally providing military and political support to South Vietnam and later withdrew its forces, influencing the postwar trajectory."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States, a global power that supported South Vietnam and significantly influenced the Vietnam War and Cold War dynamics in Indochina."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States, which officially recognized the State of Vietnam in 1950 and began diplomatic and aid relations thereafter."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States; Vietnam normalized diplomatic relations with the United States in 1995, marking a significant shift in its international relations.&lt;SEP&gt;"United States maintained an embargo on Vietnam since 1975, opposed normalization while Vietnamese troops remained in Cambodia, and progressively engaged in normalization culminating in visits by Clinton and Obama."&lt;SEP&gt;"United States, a global power that supplied military aid to Vietnam from 1950, reorganized MAAG in Indochina, and later backed the South Vietnamese state after Geneva."&lt;SEP&gt;"United States, major ally supporting VNCH and engaging in air campaigns in the North."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States, Vietnam's later diplomatic partner; Vietnam normalized relations with the US in 1995."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States, a global power, whose relations with Vietnam progressed from confrontation to normalization and strategic partnership, including a lifted arms embargo."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States, allied with South Vietnam during the Vietnam War, playing a major role in Cold War dynamics in Southeast Asia."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States; major ally of VNCH during the war, providing military aid and political support."&lt;SEP&gt;"United States; ally of OSS and engaged in intelligence cooperation with the Việt Minh."&lt;SEP&gt;"United States; formally recognized the State of Vietnam in 1950 and later signed a treaty in 1951 to provide economic aid."&lt;SEP&gt;"United States; provided military aid to France in the First Indochina War and later supported anti-communist regimes in Vietnam; reorganized MAAG.&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States, a key ally and partner of Vietnam; its policies, including lifting the arms embargo, have enabled Vietnam to modernize its defense and deepen bilateral ties."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States; key actor in the Vietnam War era and postwar policy discussions mentioned in relation to casualty estimates and governance trajectories."&lt;SEP&gt;"United States; Vietnam normalized diplomatic relations with the United States in 1995."&lt;SEP&gt;"United States; formally recognized Quốc gia Việt Nam in 1950 and provided diplomatic and economic/military aid."&lt;SEP&gt;"United States; key ally of anti-communist VNCH; provided military aid and reorganized MAAG for Indochina.&lt;SEP&gt;"United States; pursued containment of postwar Vietnam, opposed normalization while Vietnamese forces remained in Cambodia, maintained a trade embargo since 1975, and later engaged in normalization efforts in the 1990s and 2000s."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States is a major global power and partner of Việt Nam, involved in multiple high-level engagements, including visits, diplomacy, and policy shifts."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States, which provided military aid to South Vietnam and shaped Cold War policy in Indochina."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States; Vietnam normalized diplomatic relations with the United States in 1995."&lt;SEP&gt;"United States; subject to a long-standing embargo against Vietnam since 1975 and opposed to normalizing relations while Vietnamese forces were in Cambodia, later engaging in normalization in the 1990s.&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States of America, source country of the OSS; referenced in WWII intelligence operations in Indochina."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States, a country with which Vietnam eventually normalized diplomatic relations in 1995."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States, a global power that has been central to Vietnam–U.S. diplomatic and defense relations."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States, a major external power with a complex, evolving relationship with Vietnam, including concerns about missing American servicemen (MIA) and later normalization."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States; formally recognized the State of Vietnam in 1950 and provided diplomatic and military support."&lt;SEP&gt;"United States; provided military aid to France during the First Indochina War and later supported the anti-communist South Vietnamese regime; reorganized MAAG in 1955."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c715893072e90e3f1536ec249629f939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e1e11930ab01fb061749acffe85b755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4549a0a33a97f39084dfcd2d857e18f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4820a393047d71ddfc9dd25445f040a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ebc4b0a454e2ea3adc019759de704c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df404f664a0d60546a9e31eb5bdff3ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f9c5b331f58bfebf51e7a9e8de7972&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dae09351baefd2c96da656733c534ccd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b60881720494b2801d1866f2d58b8eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e0ff15a988913e85c0426ea19d7198</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIÊN XÔ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Liên Xô (Soviet Union) is referenced in the context of Allied powers during the period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Liên Xô (Soviet Union) provided aid to the Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa during the Cold War."&lt;SEP&gt;"Liên bang Xô viết, đồng minh quân sự và kinh tế chủ chốt của Việt Nam trong thời kỳ chiến tranh lạnh."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soviet Union (Liên Xô) was a key political and economic partner to Vietnam in the 1980s, providing substantial economic and military aid and influencing Vietnam's reform trajectory within Comecon markets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soviet Union, a major ally of North Vietnam, recognized the Việt Minh government and supplied military aid from the early 1950s."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soviet Union, a major ally providing support to North Vietnam during the war."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Soviet Union, providing aid and support to Vietnam during the period described."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Soviet Union; provided significant aid and political support to North Vietnam during the Vietnam War era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soviet Union; major ally to Vietnam, provided military and economic support and signed defense agreements."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soviet Union; provided recognition and military aid to the Việt Minh and DRV, shaping support for North Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soviet Union; supplied weapons, equipment, and logistics to Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Soviet Union provided support to Vietnam during the early Cold War, influencing its international alignment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soviet Union; recognized Việt Minh early in the Cold War and provided support to Việt Minh and VNDCCH."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soviet Union; supplied aid and support to Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa after 1950."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soviet Union; supplied substantial economic and military aid to Vietnam in the 1980s and remained a key ally during the Đổi Mới era, with trade and political ties through Comecon."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soviet Union, which recognized the Viet Minh government in 1950 and supplied support to North Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soviet Union; a major supplier of economic and military aid to Vietnam in the 1980s and a central figure in Vietnam’s Cold War-era alignment and Comecon ties.&lt;SEP&gt;"The Soviet Union, a key ally providing military and economic support to Vietnam during the Cold War period, with strengthened ties in the late 1970s."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soviet Union; provided diplomatic recognition to the Việt Minh early in the 1950s and supported their war effort."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soviet Union; supplied substantial military aid and logistics to Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Soviet Union, a key ally and major source of economic and military aid to Vietnam in the 1980s, shaping Vietnam's foreign policy and economic ties."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Soviet Union; provided substantial aid to Vietnam, increased military assistance in 1977–1978, and signed formal defense treaties with Vietnam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c715893072e90e3f1536ec249629f939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e1e11930ab01fb061749acffe85b755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4820a393047d71ddfc9dd25445f040a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ebc4b0a454e2ea3adc019759de704c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dae09351baefd2c96da656733c534ccd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4549a0a33a97f39084dfcd2d857e18f8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRUNG QUỐC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A major East Asian power involved in border and maritime disputes with Vietnam and in bilateral diplomacy, including a secret summit in Thành Đô to discuss normalizing ties.&lt;SEP&gt;"China is Việt Nam's large neighbor; it has ongoing territorial disputes with Vietnam in the South China Sea."&lt;SEP&gt;"China is a major regional power involved in territorial disputes with Vietnam in the South China Sea."&lt;SEP&gt;"China is a major regional power whose late-1970s actions shaped Southeast Asian security, including border tensions with Vietnam and contributing to regional Cold War dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"China is a major regional power whose relationship with Vietnam includes border disputes, opposition to Vietnam's alignment with the Soviet Union, and influence over regional security and diplomacy."&lt;SEP&gt;"China, Việt Nam's northern neighbor, with which Vietnam has historically engaged in conflict (notably the 1979 border war) and complex diplomatic relations.&lt;SEP&gt;"China, a major ally providing support to North Vietnam during the war."&lt;SEP&gt;"China, which supplied weapons and military support to Vietnam's government and factions in the early Cold War era."&lt;SEP&gt;"China; major regional power that influenced Vietnam through historical bindings, support to North Vietnam in different periods, and broader geopolitical dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"People's Republic of China, which provided aid to the Việt Minh and played a substantial role in strengthening the North Vietnamese government."&lt;SEP&gt;"China, a major regional power involved in sovereignty disputes with Vietnam in the South China Sea, shaping regional security dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"China; origin of the Kuomintang model that inspired VNQDĐ."&lt;SEP&gt;"China; throughout history, various Chinese dynasties launched invasions and exerted influence over Vietnam, shaping borders and culture."&lt;SEP&gt;"People's Republic of China; expressed concerns about regional encirclement and had territorial disputes with Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"People's Republic of China; provided large-scale military aid and weapons to Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trung Quốc is a neighboring country to Vietnam and a historical adversary in conflict, notably the 1979 border war with Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"China, Vietnam's northern neighbor; involved in the 1979 border conflict and later diplomatic normalization with Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"China, a regional power that opposed Vietnam's alliance with the USSR, contested Vietnam's border areas, and influenced regional dynamics including Cambodia and the South China Sea disputes."&lt;SEP&gt;"China, the country where radical movements and revolutionary currents surged, shaping anti-colonial and socialist currents that affected Vietnamese reformers."&lt;SEP&gt;"China; major ally and supplier of aid to Việt Minh and North Vietnam; influential in Indochina."&lt;SEP&gt;"China; provided military and logistical support to Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa after 1950."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trung Quốc is a major socialist bloc ally that provided substantial support to VNDCCH during the Vietnam War era."&lt;SEP&gt;"China, which supported North Vietnam with political and military aid during the period."&lt;SEP&gt;"China, whose revolutions and international communist movements influenced Vietnamese nationalist and communist currents."&lt;SEP&gt;"China; a neighboring country involved in Vietnam's northern-border conflict in 1979 and later in diplomacy and normalization efforts."&lt;SEP&gt;"China; referenced in discussions of regional tensions and border disputes in the era."&lt;SEP&gt;"China, Vietnam's northern neighbor, deeply involved in regional security dynamics and territorial disputes with Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"China, a neighboring country involved in Vietnam's 1979 border conflict and subsequent diplomatic relations."&lt;SEP&gt;"China; involved in regional security concerns and conducted border actions against Vietnam in the late 1970s.&lt;SEP&gt;"China; provided significant military aid and support to Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa."&lt;SEP&gt;"People's Republic of China, a major regional power involved in disputes with Vietnam over the South China Sea."&lt;SEP&gt;"People's Republic of China; supplied aid that strengthened Việt Minh's government and armed forces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trung Quốc (China) supplied aid to the Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa and played a role in regional dynamics during the Cold War."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c715893072e90e3f1536ec249629f939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e1e11930ab01fb061749acffe85b755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4549a0a33a97f39084dfcd2d857e18f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4820a393047d71ddfc9dd25445f040a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ebc4b0a454e2ea3adc019759de704c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df404f664a0d60546a9e31eb5bdff3ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b60881720494b2801d1866f2d58b8eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e0ff15a988913e85c0426ea19d7198</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐỔI MỚI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Vietnam's economic reforms initiated in 1986, transitioning from a centrally planned economy to a more market-oriented system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đổi Mới is the comprehensive reform program initiated in 1986 in Vietnam, transforming the economy from central planning toward market-oriented policies and greater integration with world markets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đổi Mới is the late 1980s Vietnamese reform program that transitioned the economy from central planning toward a socialist-oriented market system, catalyzing modernization and growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đổi Mới refers to Vietnam's economic renovation program initiated in the mid-to-late 1980s, which spurred economic reforms, modernization, and the liberalization of foreign relations, culminating in improved ties with major powers.&lt;SEP&gt;"Đổi Mới were the late 1980s economic reforms in Vietnam that shifted the economy from centralized planning toward a market-oriented system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đổi Mới is the sweeping reform program initiated in 1986 that transformed Vietnam's economy from a centralized system to market-oriented reforms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đổi Mới reforms are referenced as the prelude to Vietnam's later modernization and economic reforms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnam’s comprehensive reform program initiated in 1986 to liberalize the economy, move away from central planning, and encourage private enterprise.&lt;SEP&gt;"Đổi Mới is the economic reform program initiated in Vietnam in 1986, shifting from central planning to a market-oriented economy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đổi Mới was the late-1980s reform program that transformed Vietnam’s economy toward market-oriented policies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đổi Mới; the comprehensive set of economic and political reforms initiated in 1986 to transition Vietnam toward a market-oriented economy."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df404f664a0d60546a9e31eb5bdff3ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4820a393047d71ddfc9dd25445f040a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐẾ QUỐC NHẬT BẢN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Empire of Japan, the state that hosted Vietnamese reformists in exile and education networks (e.g., Đông Du) and was viewed as a model for modernization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Empire of Japan; occupied Vietnam for several years during World War II, disrupting colonial administration and shaping postwar trajectories."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Empire of Japan, host nation for Vietnamese reform movements and a base for modernization efforts in East Asia."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Japanese Empire, which invaded Indochina in 1945 and installed the government under occupation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Japanese Empire that occupied Vietnam during World War II, impacting governance and the course of the war in the region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đế quốc Nhật Bản (Japanese Empire) served as the imperial power in the East Asian context; Vietnamese reformists engaged with Japan as a site for modern education and influence."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Empire of Japan occupied Vietnam for five years during World War II, altering the colonial dynamics and shaping postwar politics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Empire of Japan, the occupying power in Indochina during World War II; surrender to the Allies ended its occupation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Empire of Japan, the state that occupied Indochina during WWII and orchestrated the 1945 coup."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đế quốc Nhật Bản (Japanese Empire) represents the other imperial power intervening in the region and targeted by the uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đế quốc Nhật Bản (Empire of Japan), the occupying power in Vietnam during World War II, later defeated and facing resistance from Vietnamese forces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đế quốc Nhật Bản (Japanese Empire) occupied parts of Vietnam during World War II for about five years."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-750f8e96d1bcbbd9a06d573268723339&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6322ad53528ea4e193eaf9be34d2898&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bcac0a2ed12c8c14d19b01dcb829b2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f9c5b331f58bfebf51e7a9e8de7972</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NAM TIẾN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nam tiến refers to the Nguyễn lords' southern expansion and consolidation that occurred within the broader Trịnh–Nguyễn conflict context."&lt;SEP&gt;"The sustained southward expansion by Vietnamese people from the 15th through the 18th centuries, extending political and cultural influence into the Mekong Delta and parts of present-day Cambodia."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam tiến refers to the southward expansion of Vietnamese settlement and political influence toward the Mekong Delta and into parts of Cambodia, shaping regional geography.&lt;SEP&gt;"The southward expansion policy (Nam tiến) shaping Vietnamese territorial growth and conflict."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southward expansion by Đại Việt, an ongoing process of extending Vietnamese territory toward the Champa region and beyond, influencing regional power dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The southward expansion of Vietnamese populations into the Mekong Delta and surrounding regions from the 15th to 18th centuries."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam tiến refers to the southward expansion policy in Vietnamese history, shaping Đàng Trong and the broader southern settlement during the Trịnh–Nguyễn era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam tiến refers to the southward expansion of Đại Việt's territory during the Trần era, linked to Cham Pa interactions and territorial consolidation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4943e96cb569799b168fc03f9b7f0d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRIỆU ĐÀ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Qin-era general who founded Nam Việt; defeated An Dương Vương and annexed Âu Lạc into Nam Việt; historically linked to the expansion of southern Vietnamese power."&lt;SEP&gt;"Triệu Đà was a ruler who conquered Âu Lạc in 179 BCE, establishing Nam Việt and shifting regional power toward southern China influences."&lt;SEP&gt;"Triệu Đà was a ruler who defeated Âu Lạc around 179 BCE and established Nam Việt, expanding Vietnamese influence into parts of southern China."&lt;SEP&gt;"Triệu Đà was a ruler who conquered Âu Lạc around 179 BCE and established the Nam Việt state, playing a decisive role in early Vietnamese state formation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Triệu Đà was a warlord who established Nam Việt and defeated An Dương Vương, leading to the annexation of Âu Lạc into his realm."&lt;SEP&gt;"Triệu Đà is a historical ruler who defeated Âu Lạc around 179 BCE and laid groundwork for Nam Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Triệu Đà was a former Qin general who founded the Nam Việt kingdom and expanded influence into the southern Chinese littoral, playing a pivotal role in the era's geopolitics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VĂN LANG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An ancient Vietnamese state in the Red River delta region; a precursor to later polities in northern Vietnam, associated with the Đông Sơn cultural milieu."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ancient Vietnamese state named Văn Lang; established under the Hong Bang era with capital at Phong Châu."&lt;SEP&gt;"Văn Lang was an ancient Vietnamese state centered around early agricultural communities during the Đông Sơn period, serving as a precursor to Âu Lạc."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ancient Vietnamese state centered at Phong Châu; part of the Hồng Bàng era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Văn Lang was an early Vietnamese state around which local tribes formed a centralized political entity; a precursor to later Vietnamese polities and the basis for regional cultural development."&lt;SEP&gt;"An ancient Vietnamese state with its capital at Phong Châu, associated with the Hùng Vương lineage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Văn Lang was an ancient Vietnamese state centered in the Red River Delta during the era surrounding Văn Lang and Âu Lạc, marking an early political formation in Vietnamese history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Văn Lang is an ancient northern Vietnamese state that serves as a precursor to later polities and is linked with legendary figures like Thánh Gióng."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ancient Vietnamese state in the Red River Delta; capital at Phong Châu; one of the earliest named states."&lt;SEP&gt;"Văn Lang is an ancient Vietnamese polity that formed around indigenous communities and represents an early stage in the country’s state formation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Văn Lang is an ancient Vietnamese state located in the Red River Delta region, traditionally viewed as the early political precursor to later states like Âu Lạc, and associated with early bronze-age culture and governance in northern Vietnam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c272541ed6794431c00e6ca7ce7f4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ÂU LẠC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ancient Vietnamese kingdom formed around 257 BCE by the union of the Âu Việt and Lạc Việt; later absorbed into Nam Việt after conquest."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ancient Vietnamese state following Văn Lang; era within the Hong Bang period; associated with the region around Phong Châu."&lt;SEP&gt;"Âu Lạc was an ancient Vietnamese state that followed Văn Lang and was later conquered by Triệu Đà, leading to Nam Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ancient Vietnamese state that followed Văn Lang; part of the Hồng Bàng-era political landscape."&lt;SEP&gt;"Âu Lạc was a historical Vietnamese kingdom formed by the union of Văn Lang and Lac Việt; it faced external pressures and was eventually conquered, shaping subsequent political formations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ancient Vietnamese state in the region following Văn Lang; listed as one of the early polities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Âu Lạc was an ancient Vietnamese state that followed Văn Lang, representing a regional polity prior to later state formations and upheavals."&lt;SEP&gt;"Âu Lạc is an ancient Vietnamese kingdom formed by the merger of Âu Việt and Lạc Việt; it is later conquered and absorbed by Nam Việt on Triệu Đà’s expansion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ancient Vietnamese state succeeding Văn Lang; region in northern Vietnam; part of early state-building."&lt;SEP&gt;"Âu Lạc is a historical Vietnamese polity existing in the early era and later encountered conquest as part of the region’s early political landscape."&lt;SEP&gt;"Âu Lạc is an ancient Vietnamese kingdom formed by the unification of the Âu Việt and Lạc Việt peoples, centered in the Red River Delta region and connected to legendary rulers and later historical narratives in northern Vietnam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c272541ed6794431c00e6ca7ce7f4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NAM VIỆT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Vietnamese region conquered by the Han, later subdivided into Giao Chỉ, Cửu Chân, and Nhật Nam; experienced Han rule and partial local autonomy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical name used for Vietnam; contrasted with Việt Nam in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kingdom established by Triệu Đà around 179 BCE; expanded southward and eventually annexed Âu Lạc, shaping the region's political map during the Bắc thuộc era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam Việt was a historical polity in the region that later came under Han rule; it encompassed parts of present-day northern Vietnam and adjacent areas and was subdivided by the Han into commanderies such as Giao Chỉ, Cửu Chân, and Nhật Nam, with local Vietnamese elites retaining limited authority in some mountainous areas."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam Việt was an ancient Vietnamese state established by Triệu Đà after conquering Âu Lạc; it existed before Han invasion and was eventually absorbed into Chinese influence."&lt;SEP&gt;"A southern Vietnamese realm invaded and reorganized by the Han; it encompassed the areas around the Red River Delta and southern regions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam Việt was an ancient Vietnamese state formed after Triệu Đà's conquest of Âu Lạc, interacting with the Han dynasty and shaping regional power dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam Việt is the kingdom established by Triệu Đà that later annexed Âu Lạc, marking a significant expansion and period of Sino-Vietnamese rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam Việt was a Han-era polity in what is now northern Vietnam that was invaded and reorganized into multiple administrative districts by the Han."&lt;SEP&gt;"A historical Vietnamese polity located in the southern part of the region that came under Han administrative control after the 111 BCE conquest, serving as the core arena for early Sino-Vietnamese interactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam Việt is a Han-era kingdom in the region that interacted with and was subjected to the Han Dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam Việt is an ancient kingdom established by Triệu Đà on the southern Chinese coast, extending into northern Vietnam before its incorporation into subsequent empires.&lt;SEP&gt;"Name proposed by Gia Long for the Vietnamese state; part of historical naming debates with An Nam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01995274690fe1835d7836e3a481a678&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NAM ĐẢO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nam Đảo is a language family region referenced in Southeast Asia and southern China, contributing to the ethnolinguistic landscape of Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam Đảo is the Austronesian-language region in Maritime Southeast Asia referenced in the text as part of the broader linguistic and cultural landscape of Southeast Asia."&lt;SEP&gt;"Region associated with Nam Đảo languages; homeland of Nam Đảo language group."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam Đảo refers to the Austronesian-speaking region in Southeast Asia, cited here as the maritime origin of migrations via sea routes from Taiwan."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam Đảo; Austronesian-speaking peoples; the maritime dispersal path to Southeast Asia described in the text, including movement from Taiwan."&lt;SEP&gt;"Region associated with Austroasiatic Nam Đảo-speaking groups; mentioned in context of language families in Southeast Asia."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bc55d62fe8c98a4db1992322d16a5280&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;H'MÔNG–MIỀN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"H'Mông–Miền is a language family group whose diverse populations are part of the Southeast Asian linguistic mosaic affecting northern Vietnam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KRA–DAI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Kra–Dai is a language family widely distributed in Vietnam and Southeast Asia, influencing ethnic groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HÁN–TẠNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hán–Tạng refers to the language family and cultural region associated with northern Asia; part of the broader linguistic history in the area."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHÍNH QUYỀN CỘNG HÒA XÃ HỘI CHỦ NGHĨA VIỆT NAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chính quyền Cộng hòa Xã hội chủ nghĩa Việt Nam is the governing authority of Vietnam, established in the communist era, which guided national policy, defense decisions, diplomacy, and major national transitions beginning in 1976, including post-war recovery, border conflict decisions, the late-1980s economic reforms, and the normalization of diplomatic ties with foreign powers."&lt;SEP&gt;"The governmental authority in Vietnam referenced as the ruling body in 1976, representing the socialist republic’s leadership and responsible for national policy, defense decisions, and later economic reform efforts."&lt;SEP&gt;"The governing authority of Vietnam; led the state in 1976 and directed national policy, including wartime defense and major reforms."&lt;SEP&gt;"The government leadership of Vietnam in 1976, steering national policy after reunification and shaping regional security and diplomacy."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df404f664a0d60546a9e31eb5bdff3ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THANH HÓA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Vietnamese province containing key archaeological sites like Cồn Cổ Ngựa; context for ancient population studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"A central Vietnamese region serving as a strategic base during the Lê–Mạc conflicts and the later Lê restoration efforts, notably linked to Nguyễn Kim and Lê Trang Tông activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"A central-northern region that served as the base for the Lê restoration under Lê Trang Tông during the revival period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical province in central Vietnam; origin of Lê Lợi's base and the Lam Sơn uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thanh Hóa Province, northern region associated with Cham Pa governance and resistance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thanh Hóa is a Vietnamese province mentioned as the base of Tây Đô and related to Hồ Quý Ly's reforms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thanh Hóa is a province in Vietnam that hosts important archaeological sites and serves as a geographic anchor for the population-history discussions mentioned in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;"A strategic region in central Vietnam that served as a base for Nguyễn Kim's southern expansion and political maneuvering."&lt;SEP&gt;"Modern Thanh Hóa province in Vietnam, part of the Cửu Chân region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern central Vietnam region; part of the historical Cửu Chân area during ancient times."&lt;SEP&gt;"Province in Vietnam; location associated with the Tây Đô capital."&lt;SEP&gt;"Province in north-central Vietnam; birthplace of Lê Lợi and the base area for the Lam Sơn uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"Modern Thanh Hóa Province; the area that contains the historic Cửu Chân region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Province in the Cửu Chân region mentioned as part of Nam Việt's territory."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Vietnamese province that hosts archaeological sites such as Cồn Cổ Ngựa."&lt;SEP&gt;"A central Vietnamese province; origin and base of the Lam Sơn uprising against Minh."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical Vietnamese province mentioned as part of the Cửu Chân range."&lt;SEP&gt;"Province in central Vietnam; site of Tây Đô, the capital of Đại Ngu during Hồ Quý Ly's reforms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thanh Hóa Province in central Vietnam; includes the historical region of Cửu Chân."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Vietnamese province that hosts the Cồn Cổ Ngựa archaeological site and other prehistoric contexts."&lt;SEP&gt;"A central Vietnamese province; mentioned as part of the territorial extent associated with Cửu Chân during Han administration."&lt;SEP&gt;"A north-central Vietnamese province that served as the base and origin of Lê Lợi's revolt and a focal point of the Lam Sơn uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"Province in Vietnam; associated with the Tây Đô location used as the Hồ Quý Ly capital."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thanh Hóa Province in northern Vietnam, site of uprisings as part of August 1945 events."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thanh Hóa is a key northern-central region where Nguyễn Kim helped restore the Lê dynasty and where power struggles unfolded."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df404f664a0d60546a9e31eb5bdff3ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-561d10717f3ff788e1032021a4cf0614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bfa206adeee22a1ede2ef2ae0ad9711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01995274690fe1835d7836e3a481a678</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CỒN CỔ NGỰA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An archaeological site in Thanh Hóa, Vietnam, dating to about 6,200 years ago and cited in the context of ancient population studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Archaeological site in Thanh Hóa, Vietnam, dated about 6,200 years ago; used to compare ancient Vietnamese populations with Southeast Asian groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cồn Cổ Ngựa is an archaeological site in Thanh Hóa, dated to about 6,200 years ago, used in discussions of ancient and modern Southeast Asian population genetics.&lt;SEP&gt;"Cồn Cổ Ngựa is an archaeological site in Thanh Hóa province used in prehistoric analyses."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df404f664a0d60546a9e31eb5bdff3ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HANG BACHO KIRO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Archaeological site in Bulgaria referenced as a comparative point for ancient human remains in genetic studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hang Bacho Kiro is an archaeological site cited as a comparative reference for ancient human remains in genetic studies in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;"Paleolithic archaeological site in Bulgaria; cited as a comparative reference in ancient DNA discussions related to Southeast Asian lineages."&lt;SEP&gt;"Paleolithic site in Bulgaria; frequently cited in ancient DNA research as a comparative reference for deep ancestral lineages related to Önge and Southeast Asian populations."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df404f664a0d60546a9e31eb5bdff3ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GIỒNG CÁ VỒ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An archaeological site in Vietnam where a bead necklace (agate) was found, dating to around 500 BCE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Archaeological site in Thanh Hóa province; yields artifacts such as agate bead necklace (~500 BCE) and informs Neolithic–Bronze Age population studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Archaeological site in Vietnam noted for artifacts, contributing to prehistoric studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Giồng Cá Vồ is an archaeological site in Vietnam, noted for a necklace bead dating to around 500 BCE, illustrating early material culture in the region.&lt;SEP&gt;"Giồng Cá Vồ is an archaeological site in Thanh Hóa known for finds dated to around 500 BCE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Giồng Cá Vồ is an archaeological site where a bead necklace dated to around 500 BCE was found."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df404f664a0d60546a9e31eb5bdff3ce</data>
    </node>
