    <node id="&quot;MÁN BẠC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An ancient burial site in northern Vietnam dating to around 1800 BCE, whose remains show admixture between East Asian farmers and Old East Asian foragers, used in a 2018 genetic study."&lt;SEP&gt;"An ancient burial site in northern Vietnam dating to around 1800 BCE; its remains show admixture between East Asian farming lineages and Paleolithic East Asian hunter-gatherers, with genetic affinities to modern South Asian-language-speaking groups such as Mlabri, Nicobar, and Cambodians; located in northern Vietnam (Bắc Việt Nam); studied in 2018 genetic analyses."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ancient burial site in miền Bắc Việt Nam; dated to around 1800 TCN; shows genetic admixture between Đông Á farmers and Paleo Đông Á hunter-gatherers; related to modern Nam Á-language-speaking populations like Mlabri, Nicobar, Campuchia."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bc55d62fe8c98a4db1992322d16a5280</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MIỀN NAM TRUNG QUỐC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A region in southern China cited as the origin zone for early agricultural societies described in the text, from which wet-rice farming and related migrations toward Đông Nam Á began."&lt;SEP&gt;"Geographic region of southern China referenced as the source region for migration into Southeast Asia."&lt;SEP&gt;"Region in southern China cited as the origin area for early agricultural and migratory waves toward Southeast Asia."&lt;SEP&gt;"Geographic region in southern China; origin area for early agricultural and migratory waves that moved toward Đông Nam Á, shaping prehistoric population movements."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bc55d62fe8c98a4db1992322d16a5280</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐỒNG BẰNG SÔNG HỒNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Geographic region in northern Vietnam (the Red River Delta); bordered to the east by the sea and to the south by the Red River; historically noted as a cradle for early Vietnamese state formation, where flood control, irrigation systems, trade, and defense were coordinated."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern Vietnam's Red River Delta, whose flood-control and water-management needs helped spur centralized governance and the emergence of early Vietnamese states around 2879 BCE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern Vietnamese river delta region that served as a core economic and cultural zone; surrounding areas included numerous traditional villages and crafts centers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Red River Delta, a major agricultural and commercial hub in northern Vietnam under Tang control."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Red River Delta in northern Vietnam, a fertile lowland region that served as the cradle of ancient Vietnamese state formation and cultural development."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Red River Delta region in northern Vietnam, part of the historical Giao Chỉ area under Han rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Geographic region in northern Vietnam; cradle of flood-control and unified governance efforts that contributed to the emergence of the first legendary Vietnamese states circa 2879 BCE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khu vực địa lý nơi Giao Chỉ được thành lập; hiện nay là đồng bằng sông Hồng ở miền Bắc Việt Nam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Red River Delta region in northern Vietnam, described as a geographic framework for early state formation and agricultural development."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Red River Delta region, a core area for traditional crafts and the economy, extending around Hanoi."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Red River Delta in northern Vietnam, identified as a core part of Nam Việt's territory and administrative focus during early Chinese rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Red River Delta plain is a historical economic and cultural heartland in northern Vietnam, serving as the core area for crafts, agriculture, and the political development of early Vietnamese states."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Red River Delta region where uprisings occurred."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đồng bằng sông Hồng (Red River Delta) is a geographic region in northern Vietnam where flood control needs and centralized governance contributed to the emergence of early Vietnamese states."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c272541ed6794431c00e6ca7ce7f4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bfa206adeee22a1ede2ef2ae0ad9711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01995274690fe1835d7836e3a481a678&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc55d62fe8c98a4db1992322d16a5280</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KINH DƯƠNG VƯƠNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An Dương Vương is the legendary king associated with the founding of Âu Lạc and the Nỏ thần myth, later defeated by Triệu Đà."&lt;SEP&gt;"An Dương Vương is the legendary king of Âu Lạc, who led resistance against northern invaders, reportedly moved the capital to Phong Khê, and is central to the mythic narrative of the kingdom.&lt;SEP&gt;"Leader of Văn Lang; revered figure who defended the realm against northern invaders and later became ruler of Âu Lạc; associated with the phonetic identity Thục Phán in the historical account."&lt;SEP&gt;"Legendary founder and regnal name associated with the Xích Quỷ state; central figure in the early Vietnamese royal lineage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Legendary founder of the Xích Quỷ state circa 2879 BCE; first ruler in the Hồng Bàng era; traditionally said to have proclaimed himself as Kinh Dương Vương."&lt;SEP&gt;"Legendary founder who established the Xích Quỷ state around 2879 TCN; first ruler in the legendary lineage of early Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The legendary founder of the earliest Vietnamese state Xích Quỷ circa 2879 BCE, initiating a royal lineage that leads toward the Hong Bang era."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c272541ed6794431c00e6ca7ce7f4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;XÍCH QUỶ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A mythical early Vietnamese state founded by Kinh Dương Vương around 2879 BCE, representing the dawn of state formation in Vietnamese history."&lt;SEP&gt;"An ancient Vietnamese state founded around 2879 BCE; established by Lộc Tục who proclaimed himself Kinh Dương Vương, located in the northern region during the Hồng Bàng era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Legendary state founded by Kinh Dương Vương; early political entity in Vietnamese legends."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c272541ed6794431c00e6ca7ce7f4da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MIỀN BẮC VIỆT NAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Northern Vietnam region; origin of the Đông Sơn culture and early political formations, with cross-border cultural links to Guangxi (Quảng Tây) and Laos (Lào)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern Vietnam; identified as a geographic origin region for the Đông Sơn culture alongside Quảng Tây and Lào.&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern Vietnam, origin region for Đông Sơn culture and early Vietnamese civilizations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern Vietnam; includes the ancient burial sites Mán Bạc and Núi Nấp mentioned in the text."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c272541ed6794431c00e6ca7ce7f4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc55d62fe8c98a4db1992322d16a5280</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;QUẢNG TÂY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A border region in southwestern China, neighboring northern Vietnam and historically involved in cross-border exchange with Vietnamese communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Guangxi region (present-day China); part of cross-border origins or influence on the Đông Sơn culture."&lt;SEP&gt;"Guangxi region in China; cited as one of the origin sources for the Đông Sơn culture.&lt;SEP&gt;"Guangxi, a region in southern China, referenced in the historical discussion of cross-border populations contributing to early Vietnamese states."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical region (present-day Guangxi) mentioned as land associated with Han-era administration during expansion into the area."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical region near the Nam Việt frontier mentioned in relation to campaigns."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khu vực thuộc Trung Quốc ngày nay; liên quan đến phạm vi đất đai được Han đoạt lại trong giai đoạn Bắc thuộc."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quảng Tây (Guangxi), a region mentioned as part of land under Han rule in the Nam Việt area."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quảng Tây is a southern Chinese region referenced as part of Triệu Đà's origin area and the broader Nam Việt geopolitical context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quảng Tây province was a theater of operations in the conflict between Đại Việt and the Song dynasty in 1075–1076."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quảng Tây was a border region involved in border campaigns during the 1075 conflict with Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quảng Tây, Chinese region where Song forces operated against Đại Việt in 1075."&lt;SEP&gt;"Region in southern China referenced as part of Han expansion into Nam Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Region in southern China; part of the cultural-geographic origin area for Đông Sơn culture."&lt;SEP&gt;"Region in southern China; referenced as part of the geographic and political environment surrounding Nam Việt and Triệu Đà."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vùng biên giới phía tây bắc Trung Quốc nơi diễn ra trận đánh 1075 giữa Đại Việt và Tống."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01995274690fe1835d7836e3a481a678&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c272541ed6794431c00e6ca7ce7f4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÀO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Southeast Asian country bordering Vietnam, referenced as part of the regional geographic context in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laos, neighboring country affected by spillover from the Vietnam War."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laos; cross-border region linked to the origins of the Đông Sơn culture."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lào is the Lao territory added to Indochina in 1893."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laos; cited as one of the origin sources for the Đông Sơn culture.&lt;SEP&gt;"Lào (Laos) was incorporated into French Indochina as part of the federation of colonies under French rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laos, part of French Indochina in this period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Country neighboring Vietnam; part of the Đông Sơn culture's origins."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laos; region incorporated into French Indochina."</data>
