    <node id="&quot;HÀ NỘI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Capital city central to the 1945 independence events."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam, where Đông Kinh Nghĩa Thục was established and reformist activities concentrated."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam; significant in Vietnamese history and geography."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of North Vietnam (DRV), the political center of the northern part of Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital region of northern Vietnam; located in/near the Đồng bằng sông Hồng and referenced in historical geographic context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the capital of Tonkin and a strategic target in the Bắc Kỳ campaigns."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the city where Văn Miếu is located and served as the political-cultural center in this history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the modern capital of Vietnam and historically identified with Kinh đô Kẻ Chợ/Đông Kinh, the northern political center."&lt;SEP&gt;"Modern capital of Vietnam; encompassed by the historic Thăng Long designation in the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern Vietnamese capital region, central to the campaigns and sieges described (also referred to historically as Đông Quan)."&lt;SEP&gt;"The capital city (also known as Đông Kinh) that shifted hands during the Trịnh–Nguyễn conflicts, notably captured by Trịnh Tùng in 1592."&lt;SEP&gt;"The capital city of Vietnam that French forces captured during the colonial campaigns, notably in 1873."&lt;SEP&gt;"The capital city of Vietnam, central to the early governance of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam and the 1945 Declaration of Independence."&lt;SEP&gt;"The capital of Vietnam and a central hub for political decision-making during and after the war."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thành phố thủ đô hiện nay của Việt Nam, tương ứng với Thăng Long trong lịch sử."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnam's capital, a focal point in anti-colonial actions and later negotiations during the transition period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam; location of Văn Miếu."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam; mentioned in the context of Vietnamese history and geography."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam; site of Ming occupation and key battles during the Lam Sơn uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Đại Việt; captured by Trịnh Tùng in 1592; central to the Bắc triều and Trịnh–Nguyễn dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of Vietnam, noted in bibliographic references."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of Vietnam; site of French assaults and Vietnamese government actions during the period described."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of the Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa (North Vietnam)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the capital city of Tonkin; it was occupied by French forces in 1873 and again in 1882 as part of French expansion.&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the capital city of Vietnam and the venue for the 2019 DPRK–US summit."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the city where Đông Kinh Nghĩa Thục was established and where early modern Vietnamese education and reform activism were centralized."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the modern capital of Vietnam, located on the site of the former Thăng Long."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội, the capital, was captured by French forces in 1873 as part of the colonial campaign."&lt;SEP&gt;"Modern capital of Vietnam; corresponds to the ancient Tống Bình and Đại La areas."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam; listed as the location for the publisher and general context of the historical works mentioned."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam; site of French positions attacked by Việt Nam's forces in 1946–47."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of Vietnam; hosted the 2019 Hanoi summit between Kim Jong-un and Donald Trump."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of Vietnam; site of Đông Kinh Nghĩa Thục and a hub of reformist ideas."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa in the North; administrative center of VNDCCH."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội (Hanoi), the capital city of Vietnam, mentioned in the Vietnamese historical context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the capital city of Vietnam, captured by French forces during campaigns in Tonkin."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the capital of Việt Nam and the political center in the north, central to governance, defense planning, and border-security considerations during the postwar era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the modern capital region historically referred to as Đông Kinh/Kẻ Chợ in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"The capital region (also called Đông Kinh in historical texts); central to the northern Lê–Mạc conflict and later Trịnh–Nguyễn power dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thủ đô Việt Nam; nơi diễn ra các hoạt động ngoại giao và là một điểm liên lạc quan trọng trong bối cảnh bình thường hóa quan hệ và ngoại giao Việt Nam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city in northern Vietnam; historically known as Đông Kinh."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam and the political center of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city where major events including the Proclamation occurred."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of Vietnam; a focal point for diplomacy and foreign visits, including trips by U.S. presidents.&lt;SEP&gt;"Ha Noi is the capital region nearby the Red River Delta and a center for traditional crafts and sacred sites in the historical narrative."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội (Hanoi) is the capital of Vietnam, mentioned in the Vietnamese-language sources and contextual materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội (Thăng Long) was the strategic capital captured by Nguyễn Ánh in 1802."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the capital city of Việt Nam and hosted the 2019 Korea–US summit."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the modern capital of Vietnam; historically the site was Đại La, renamed Thăng Long after Lý Công Uẩn moved the capital in 1010; it has been the political and cultural center across successive Vietnamese dynasties."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội was a northern Vietnamese city involved in French military campaigns during the era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Present-day Hanoi; referenced in connection with the Đông Quan/Hà Nội area."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thủ đô của Việt Nam, trung tâm chính trị và lực lượng quân sự trong thời kỳ chiến tranh."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam, location of the Đông Kinh Nghĩa Thục school and center of reformist activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam, site of major political maneuvers and power-sharing disputes between Việt Minh and the imperial authorities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam; hosted the 2019 Korea–US Summit between Kim Jong-un and Donald Trump."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam; site of the Ba Đình Square and independence ceremony."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Việt Nam Dân chủ Cộng hòa (North Vietnam)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city where major uprisings and mass demonstrations took place in August 1945."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of Vietnam; referenced as a destination during diplomacy (e.g., Clinton's visit)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of Vietnam; repeatedly targeted during French expansion in the north (1873, 1882)."&lt;SEP&gt;"City hosting Văn Miếu; caption references Văn Miếu, Hà Nội."&lt;SEP&gt;"City hosting Văn Miếu; referenced again in the text as the location of Văn Miếu."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội (Đông Kinh) served as the capital and battlefield site where northern and central powers clashed during the era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is a major center where Việt Minh asserted control following the August Revolution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the capital and a major political and cultural center of Vietnam; referenced in the text in the context of postwar administrative changes and migrations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the capital city of Vietnam and a location referenced in historical and bibliographic sources."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the capital city where the abdication ceremony, mass mobilizations, and the Tuyên ngôn Độc lập event took place."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the modern capital region that sits within or near the historical Red River Delta and figures in the ancestral economic and political landscape described in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội, the capital city that hosts Ba Đình Square and major political events."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội, the capital of Vietnam, is listed as the publication location in the source referencing Dương Trung Quốc's work."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội, the modern capital of Vietnam, located at the site of the historic capitals Đại La and Thăng Long."&lt;SEP&gt;"The capital of Vietnam; targeted as part of French military actions during the conflict."&lt;SEP&gt;"The capital region of Vietnam; site of key engagements and sieges during the Lam Sơn conflict; historically known as Thăng Long/Đông Đô."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thủ đô Việt Nam; nơi diễn ra lễ tuyên ngôn độc lập ngày 2 tháng 9 năm 1945 và là trung tâm của VNDCCH."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c715893072e90e3f1536ec249629f939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f9c5b331f58bfebf51e7a9e8de7972&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dae09351baefd2c96da656733c534ccd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0858721f576cf19954031c342a7d2bff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bfa206adeee22a1ede2ef2ae0ad9711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fff36ca9411ec2bf48b0a366dd7589f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61f2f053f1c7eb75498d3b31a687c2e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bcac0a2ed12c8c14d19b01dcb829b2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e1e11930ab01fb061749acffe85b755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ebc4b0a454e2ea3adc019759de704c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4820a393047d71ddfc9dd25445f040a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3e2d86a8a15344ff5c25829117f928c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6322ad53528ea4e193eaf9be34d2898&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-561d10717f3ff788e1032021a4cf0614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e0d2b12d6dc3ace3b2354166252a164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf0b29bf76d345dfa9da0349d9f546d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e0ff15a988913e85c0426ea19d7198</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;QUẢNG ĐÔNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Guangdong, a region in southern China, mentioned as part of the geographic context for migrations and interactions that influenced early Vietnamese polities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quảng Đông region was involved as part of the border theaters during the same conflict."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quảng Đông was a Chinese province involved in the border war against Đại Việt and in cross-border campaigns."&lt;SEP&gt;"Region in southern China; historical context for Triệu Đà's sphere of influence and the Chinese dynastic presence in early regional history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vùng biên giới phía nam Trung Quốc liên quan đến các chiến dịch biên giới của Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quảng Đông is a coastal region in southern China that is identified as part of Triệu Đà's geographic origin in the historical narrative."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quảng Đông, Chinese region where Song bases were located."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NHÀ HÁN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Han dynasty of China; contemporary power overlapping with Vietnamese polities during the Bắc thuộc period; referenced as a northern overlord context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hồ Dynasty, another later Vietnamese dynasty mentioned in the historical sequence."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Hán (Han dynasty) is the Chinese dynasty that invaded and ruled over northern Vietnam, initiating and sustaining the Bắc thuộc period beginning around 111 BCE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Hồ (Hồ Dynasty) was the short-lived Vietnamese dynasty that implemented reforms and centralized power before the Ming invasion ended its rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Hồ is another Vietnamese dynasty referenced as part of the historical dynastic sequence."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Hồ was a dynasty that briefly unified the country after the Trần era, preceding the rise of later dynasties."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Han Dynasty invaded Nam Việt in 111 BCE and reorganized its administration into new districts."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Han Dynasty of China, an imperial power that invaded and ruled parts of the Vietnamese region, influencing borders and local polities for centuries."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Han Dynasty's imperial government that ruled northern Vietnam during the Bắc thuộc period, a major external power referenced in the historical narrative."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Han Dynasty, a major Chinese imperial power that conquered Nam Việt in 111 BCE and established centralized administration over the region, creating prefectures such as Giao Chỉ, Cửu Chân, and Nhật Nam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Han Dynasty, the ancient Chinese empire that invaded Nam Việt and reorganized its territory into Giao Chỉ, Cửu Chân, and Nhật Nam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Han dynasty of China invaded and/or exerted influence over Nam Việt, notably around 111 BCE, affecting the region's political landscape."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Han dynasty, imperial China, expanded into Nam Việt, imposed centralized governance, and organized the region into administrative districts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01995274690fe1835d7836e3a481a678&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THÁNH GIÓNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thánh Gióng is a legendary heroic figure who, according to tradition, led the Văn Lang against northern invaders, symbolizing national defense and technological prowess in metallurgy and weaponry."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thánh Gióng is a legendary young hero who led the Văn Lang people against northern invaders, emblematic of bronze-age prowess and national identity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Truyền thuyết kể về một tráng sĩ trẻ tuổi lãnh đạo đất nước Văn Lang, khoác áo giáp sắt và cưỡi ngựa sắt, biểu tượng cho trình độ luyện kim và tinh thần bảo vệ tổ quốc của người Việt cổ."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THÀNH CỔ LOA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thành Cổ Loa is a historic citadel in northern Vietnam associated with early states in the region and linked in traditions to the era of An Dương Vương and Âu Lạc."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thành Cổ Loa is an ancient fortress and archaeological site near Hanoi, historically linked to An Dương Vương and the early Vietnamese polities Văn Lang and Âu Lạc."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thành Cổ Loa là thành phố/địa điểm cổ near Hanoi, được coi là thủ đô hoặc trung tâm quyền lực của Âu Lạc theo truyền thuyết và lịch sử sơ khai, đồng thời là di tích khảo cổ học quan trọng của Việt Nam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CAO BẰNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A province in northern Vietnam that includes Nguyên Bình district; the battles of Phai Khắt and Nà Ngần occurred within this province during 1944."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cao Bằng is a northern Vietnamese province mentioned in the historical narrative surrounding early states and peopling of the region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cao Bằng is a northern Vietnamese province where remnants of the Mạc clan maintained a foothold until their final defeat, becoming a strategic refuge in the late 16th–17th centuries."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khu vực phía bắc Việt Nam từng được nhắc đến như một phần khu vực xuất xứ/hoạt động của các nhà nước cổ và các nhóm cư dân trong thời kỳ cổ đại."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern mountainous province where the remnants of the Mạc dynasty retreated after their defeat and remained until 1677."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern Vietnamese province where the last Mạc stronghold rested until 1677."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cao Bằng is a northern Vietnamese province mentioned as part of the historical geography surrounding early Vietnamese states like Văn Lang and Âu Lạc."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cao Bằng is a northern province where the last Mạc holdout persisted until 1677, when it was eliminated by Trịnh Tạc."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cao Bằng province in northern Vietnam includes Phai Khắt and Nà Ngần."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern Vietnamese province where Việt Minh units attacked Japanese forces during the uprising."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7418e6f1b899c505272caee02f3cf1ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bfa206adeee22a1ede2ef2ae0ad9711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NHÀ TRIỆU&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Triều đại/công tộc cai trị độc lập với Nhà Hán tại khu vực phía bắc trong thời kỳ Bắc thuộc; được xem là một trong các thế lực địa phương đối đầu với quyền lực Trung Quốc."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;500 TCN TẠI ĐÔNG SƠN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"500 TCN tại Đông Sơn is a historical date marking the Đông Sơn culture's timeframe in northern Vietnam, associated with advanced bronze metallurgy and ritual crafts such as bronze drums."&lt;SEP&gt;"Năm 500 TCN tại khu vực Đông Sơn, liên quan tới nền Văn hóa Đông Sơn và trình độ luyện kim đồng thau cao."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRIỆU THỊ TRINH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Vietnamese noblewoman, commonly known as Bà Triệu; led a rebellion against Đông Ngô influence in late antiquity and is celebrated as a symbol of female leadership and resistance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Triệu Thị Trinh is a Vietnamese noblewoman (often called Bà Triệu) who, alongside Triệu Quốc Đạt, led a rebellion against Đông Ngô in the Nam Việt region during the late Han period, becoming a symbol of resistance in Vietnamese history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnamese woman leader who, with Triệu Quốc Đạt, led a rebellion against Đông Ngô in 248 CE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnamese noblewoman who led a rebellion against Đông Ngô; popularly remembered as Bà Triệu."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnamese noblewoman who co-led resistance against Đông Ngô; popularly known as Bà Triệu."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01995274690fe1835d7836e3a481a678</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LỮ HƯNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Vietnamese military figure who toppled remnants of Đông Ngô forces and aligned with the regent of Tào Ngụy during the regional power struggles around Giao Chỉ."&lt;SEP&gt;"A mid- to late-Han dynasty official who temporarily overthrew the remaining Eastern Wu bureaucracy and allied with the Chancellor of Wei in the Shu region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lữ Hưng là một quan lại cao cấp thời Tam Quốc, người đã lật đổ bộ máy quan lại Đông Ngô còn lại và liên minh với thứ sử của Tào Ngụy tại đất Thục."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rebel leader who temporarily overthrew Đông Ngô's remaining administration and formed an alliance with Tào Ngụy in the Shu region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lữ Hưng was a victorious official who toppled the remaining apparatus of Eastern Wu (Đông Ngô) and allied with a Shu prefect under the Tào Ngụy side during a late-3rd-century power shift in the region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Military figure who toppled remaining Đông Ngô forces and allied with the regent of Tào Ngụy in the later conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"A rebel leader who briefly overthrew remnants of Đông Ngô and allied with Tào Ngụy in the Shu region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lữ Hưng là một tướng lĩnh, người đã lật đổ bộ máy quan lại Đông Ngô còn lại và liên minh với thứ sử của Tào Ngụy tại đất Thục; đạt được thành công tạm thời vào giữa thập niên 260."&lt;SEP&gt;"A regional leader who toppled remaining Đông Ngô authorities in the region and allied with Tào Ngụy (Cao Wei) in the late 3rd century, shaping later dynastic power dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lữ Hưng was a high-ranking official who, in the mid-260s, led a coup against the remaining Eastern Wu apparatus and formed an alliance with a Shu governor in the land of Shu."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01995274690fe1835d7836e3a481a678&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TỶ ẢNH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"One of the three districts created in Simhapura during Dương Kiên's governance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tỷ Ảnh is one of the three administrative districts (quận) used to govern Simhapura's territory, illustrating the Chinese-era subdivision of Chăm lands."&lt;SEP&gt;"One of the three districts established in Simhapura during the administrative reorganization led by Dương Kiên in Cham territory."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KHÂU HÒA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A local general who was sent to replace a dying general and govern the land; later aligned with the Tang (Đường) authorities under Đường Cao Tổ's regime."&lt;SEP&gt;"General who replaced Lưu Phương to govern Giao Châu after illness; later submission noted to Tiêu Tiển in 618."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khâu Hòa (丘和) was a local general appointed to govern the region around Champa (Cửu Chân) who initially pledged allegiance to Tang authorities after the 618 upheaval and later became involved in regional power dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khâu Hòa is a local leader (thủ lĩnh bản địa) who was sent to govern the land after the preceding general from Chăm Pa fell ill; later he aligns with Tiêu Tiển's regime in 618."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khâu Hòa was appointed to govern the land Giao Châu after the previous governor fell ill and died on the way back from Cham Pa, replacing to administer the territory."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khâu Hòa was dispatched to govern Giao Châu; he initially submitted to the Tiêu Tiển regime in 618 and later aligned with the Tang emperor in 622."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khâu Hòa được cử sang cai quản vùng Giao Chỉ và ban đầu quy thuận quyền lực của Tiêu Tiển năm 618."&lt;SEP&gt;"Official sent to replace Lưu Phương and oversee governance of Giao Châu."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐƯỜNG CAO TỔ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Founder of the Tang Dynasty who overthrew the Sui and established the Đường (Tang) Dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Founder of the Tang Dynasty, who overthrew the preceding regime (the Sui) and established the Tang Dynasty in China."&lt;SEP&gt;"Founder of the Tang Dynasty; overthrew the Sui Dynasty and established the Nhà Đường (Tang Dynasty) in 618 CE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gaozu of Tang, founder of the Tang Dynasty who overthrew the Sui and established the Tang regime in 618."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đường Cao Tổ is the founder of the Tang Dynasty (Nhà Đường), who toppled the Sui Dynasty (nhà Tùy) to establish the Tang Dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đường Cao Tổ (Li Yuan) là người sáng lập Nhà Đường sau khi lật đổ Tùy vào năm 618."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đường Cao Tổ, the founder of the Tang Dynasty, overthrew the Sui Dynasty and established the Tang Empire in 618, laying the groundwork for Tang rule."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TIÊU TIỂN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A regional ruler whose authority was recognized by Khâu Hòa in 618; later the local power structure submitted to the Tang leadership."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chinese official under whom Khâu Hòa initially submitted, around the 618 transition period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ruler associated with political realignments around the late 610s; referenced in relation to Giao Châu governance in 618 CE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiêu Tiển is a ruler to whom Khâu Hòa pledges loyalty in 618, indicating his authority over the region at that time."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiêu Tiển was a Tang authority to whom Khâu Hòa initially pledged allegiance in 618."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiêu Tiển là quan chức thời Nhà Đường có quyền lực liên quan đến sự quy thuận của Khâu Hòa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiêu Tiển was the ruler whose authority Khâu Hòa initially pledged allegiance to in 618 before switching loyalty to the Tang Emperors in 622."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÊ NGỌC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A local chieftain in Cửu Chân who remained loyal to Tiêu Tiển and continued resisting Tang forces for three more years."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Ngọc was a local leader in Cửu Chân (modern Thanh Hóa) who remained loyal to Tiêu Tiển and resisted Tang forces for three more years."&lt;SEP&gt;"A local leader in Cửu Chân who remained loyal to Tiêu Tiển and continued resisting Đường forces for three more years."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Ngọc was a local leader in Cửu Chân who remained loyal to Tiêu Tiển and continued resisting the Tang forces for three more years."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NHÀ ĐƯỜNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nhà Ngô refers to the Ngô dynasty, a medieval Vietnamese ruling house.&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Ngô was a Vietnamese dynasty established after periods of fragmentation, contributing to the consolidation of local sovereignty in early medieval Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Ngô was established around 939, marking a local dynastic restoration of independence in Vietnam after centuries under northern Chinese rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà nước Đường (Tang Dynasty) ở Trung Hoa, chịu trách nhiệm quản lý An Nam đô hộ phủ và thực hiện các cải cách."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Đường là triều đại Trung Hoa kế tục Tùy, được thành lập sau sự kiện lật đổ Tùy do Đường Cao Tổ khởi xướng."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tang Dynasty, Chinese imperial dynasty founded by Đường Cao Tổ in 618 CE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tang Dynasty, the Chinese imperial dynasty that governed the region including An Nam đô hộ phủ and interactions with Vietnam during the period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tang Dynasty; successor to the Sui; central to the 618 transition mentioned in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tang dynasty of China; under this regime, the Tĩnh Hải quân operated autonomously under the Tiết độ sứ title, shaping Vietnamese governance."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ngô dynasty established by Ngô Quyền and his successors, marking the first post-Tang Vietnamese ruling line."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ngô dynasty of Vietnam, a medieval ruling house preceding the Đinh and Tiền Lê."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ngô dynasty was established in 939 and represents an early native Vietnamese monarchic period that continued the political lineage between ancient kingdoms and later dynasties."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ngô dynasty, an early medieval Vietnamese ruling house."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Tang Dynasty established by Đường Cao Tổ; later expands influence into northern Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Tang Dynasty founded by Đường Cao Tổ, ruling over a large Chinese imperial domain in the 7th century."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Tang Dynasty of China, under whose suzerainty Tĩnh Hải quân existed as a frontier region, shaping its early administration."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Tang Dynasty, the imperial Chinese regime established after 618, expanding influence into northern Việt Nam and Champa."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Tang Dynasty; Chinese imperial authority that administered An Nam and later reconquered it.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SIMHAPURA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Capital of the Cham Pa kingdom during its early and peak periods; a major urban center in Champa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of the Cham kingdom (Champa)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cham city/region in central Vietnam that was invaded by Dương Kiên and organized under Chinese rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Simhapura was the Cham Pa capital during the early period described."&lt;SEP&gt;"City/region associated with Cham power in central Vietnam; invaded by Tang-era forces under Dương Kiên."&lt;SEP&gt;"Simhapura là vương quốc của người Chăm ở miền Trung Việt Nam, bị Dương Kiên xâm lược và chịu sự quản lý tạm thời."&lt;SEP&gt;"Simhapura was a Cham capital noted in Cham history, associated with early Cham urban centers and religious architecture."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INDRAPURA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ancient Cham capital near the Hue region; used to describe Cham geography."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indrapura was a Cham Pa capital built under Indravarman II, located in the area of present-day Quảng Nam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indrapura was the Cham king Paramesvaravarman I's capital, later destroyed during Lê Hoàn's campaign."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ancient capital region associated with the Cham/Indrapura era near Hue; mentioned as a historical locale."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city established by Indravarman II in Champa; located in present-day Quảng Nam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indrapura was the Cham capital attacked by Đại Cồ Việt under Paramesvaravarman I; it was later destroyed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cham Pa’s capital established or rebuilt by Indravarman II; later superseded as the center of power by Vijaya around 1000."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indrapura, the Champa capital destroyed during campaigns by Đại Cồ Việt forces under Lê Hoàn and his allies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ancient Cham capital region; historical center mentioned in Cham domain history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indrapura was the Cham capital founded by Indravarman II (c. 854–893) near modern Quảng Nam, hosting a large Buddhist monastery."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indrapura, an ancient city/region connected to ChamPa campaigns and the ChamPa–Vietnam conflicts described in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical Cham capital region; noted here as a Cham center near central Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indrapura was the capital city of Champa and played a central role in the Champa–Đại Việt conflicts, later attacked and damaged in the wars."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-561d10717f3ff788e1032021a4cf0614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÂM ẤP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Early Cham kingdom that emerged around 192 CE in central Vietnam; later developed into a powerful Cham state spanning a large coastal region and fostering indigenous script, Buddhism, and Hinduism."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lâm Ấp (Champa) was a prominent Cham polity that grew wealthy from spice and silk trade and later faced Khmer aggression."&lt;SEP&gt;"Early Cham polity (precursor to Chăm Pa) that rose with Cham uprisings around 192 CE, later developing into the Champa kingdom."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Champa kingdom (Lâm Ấp) whose wealth came from spice and silk trade; its capital was Simhapura; engaged in repeated conflicts with the Khmer Empire."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lâm Ấp là vương quốc cổ ở miền Trung Việt Nam, tiền thân của vương quốc Chăm Pa."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SAMBHUVARMAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"King of Lâm Ấp who restored independence around 629, initiating a unified Cham era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sambhuvarman was the king of Lâm Ấp who restored its independence and ruled during the late 6th to early 7th centuries."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sambhuvarman was the Cham king who restored independent Cham rule in the region around 629 after earlier conflicts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LƯU PHƯƠNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"General appointed as tổng quản (governor&lt;SEP&gt;"General ordered by Tùy Dượng Đế in 605 to invade Lâm Ấp after reasserting control over Bắc Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lưu Phương was a general ordered to invade Lâm Ấp in 605, pursuing northern Vietnamese imperial objectives."&lt;SEP&gt;"Official appointed as tổng quản (chief administrator) of Giao Châu under early imperial administration."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lưu Phương was a general who led the 605 invasion of Lâm Ấp under orders from the Sui authorities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lưu Phương được bổ làm tổng quản Giao Châu (Giao Chỉ)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TÙY DƯỢNG ĐẾ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Emperor who ordered the invasion of Lâm Ấp in 605; associated with the Sui/Tang era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tùy Dượng Đế was the Sui emperor who ordered the invasion of Lâm Ấp in 605."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tùy Dộng Đế is the emperor of the Tùy dynasty who issued the order to Lưu Phương to invade Lâm Ấp in 605."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tùy Dượng Đế was the Sui emperor who ordered Lưu Phương to invade Lâm Ấp in 605."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INDRAVARMAN II&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cham king (reign 854–893); commissioned the new capital Indrapura and the large temple at Đồng Dương; promoted Buddhism as the state religion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indravarman II (reign 854–893) established a new Cham Pa dynasty and sponsored the capital Indrapura and a major Buddhist monastery at Đồng Dương."&lt;SEP&gt;"King of Champa who founded Indrapura and oversaw the construction of a large Buddhist monastery at Đồng Dương (reigned 854–893)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indravarman II (reigned 854–893) founded Indrapura as Cham Pa's capital and oversaw major religious constructions at Đồng Dương."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LƯU KẾ TÔNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lưu Kế Tông seized Indrapura and declared himself king of Cham Pa around 983 during Cham–Vietnamese turmoil."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lưu Kế Tông was a Vietnamese usurper who captured Indrapura in 983 and proclaimed himself Cham Pa king in 986."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnamese figure who seized Indrapura in 986 and proclaimed himself king of Cham Pa, disrupting Cham Pa’s dynastic line.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VIJAYA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Capital city of Champa in the Champa kingdom; conquered by Lê Thánh Tông."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vijaya became the Cham Pa capital around 989–1000 after moving the capital from Indrapura."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vijaya is the capital city of the Chăm Pa kingdom; it is notable in the text as the place where Trần Duệ Tông was killed during Cham Pa campaigns in 1377."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Champa; captured during Lê Thánh Tông's campaigns.&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Champa; site of Cham victories and the death of Trần Duệ Tông in 1377."&lt;SEP&gt;"City in southern Champa; located in Bình Định region; later significance in Cham history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of Cham Pa established around 989–1000; located in the region near present-day Bình Định; later became the capital after Indrapura."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vijaya was Cham Pa's southern capital, later a center of religious and political life in Cham history (present-day Bình Định)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Champa; captured by Lê Thánh Tông in 1471 (also known as Đồ Bàn)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historic Cham capital; site of Cham attacks and the death of Trần Duệ Tông in 1377."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-561d10717f3ff788e1032021a4cf0614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐẾ QUỐC KHMER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A short-lived empire proclaimed by Emperor Bao Dai under Japanese protection during the late colonial period."&lt;SEP&gt;"A short-lived imperial state proclaimed by Bao Dai during the Japanese occupation in Indochina."&lt;SEP&gt;"A short-lived monarchy proclaimed by Emperor Bao Đại amid wartime political upheavals."&lt;SEP&gt;"A short-lived state proclaimed by Emperor Bao Dai during the Japanese occupation in Vietnam, with Trần Trọng Kim as Prime Minister."&lt;SEP&gt;"A short-lived state proclaimed under Japanese occupation, led by Bao Dai's regime."&lt;SEP&gt;"An imperial state proclaimed by Emperor Bao Dai during World War II, established under Japanese occupation for a brief period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chế độ ngắn ngủi được Hoàng đế Bảo Đại tuyên bố thành lập dưới sự bảo hộ của Nhật; đặt nền cho một chế độ thay thế trước khi độc lập hoàn tất."&lt;SEP&gt;"Empire of the Khmer; a regional power whose decline affected regional dynamics during the period.&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical Khmer Empire; its sovereignty over southern lands influenced Vietnamese expansion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khmer Empire was a powerful southern neighbor that conducted pressures and attacks around the Đại Việt sphere during periods of regional conflict."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khmer Empire, a major rival of Champa; engaged in prolonged warfare with Champa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khmer Empire, a neighboring empire mentioned in regional power dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khmer Empire, a regional power to the southwest of Đại Việt, in decline during later periods described."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khmer Empire, historic power that previously controlled parts of the Mekong Delta before Vietnamese expansion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khmer Empire; major historical state in Southeast Asia."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khmer Empire; neighbor empire that was in decline during the period, creating opportunities for Đại Việt expansion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khmer Empire; the major Khmer state whose influence and territorial dynamics affected southern Vietnam and the Mekong Delta area."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ming Dynasty of China; invaded Đại Ngu (Đại Việt) in 1407–1427, ultimately annexing the territory and turning it into a Ming-ruled region (Giao Chỉ)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Short-lived empire proclaimed by Ho Chi Minh in 1945 during the Japanese occupation period."&lt;SEP&gt;"The French colonial empire in Indochina, the main colonial power opposed by Việt Minh during the period."&lt;SEP&gt;"The French colonial empire that ruled Indochina, triggering nationalist resistance and the exile of monarchs."&lt;SEP&gt;"The French colonial empire, the imperial power conducting invasions and colonization in Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Khmer Empire, a major Southeast Asian polity centered around Angkor, involved in sustained Khmer–Champa conflicts and regional dominance."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Khmer Empire, a major regional rival to Cham Pa, expanding its influence across Southeast Asia."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Khmer Empire, a powerful Southeast Asian empire centered in present-day Cambodia, rulers included Suryavarman II and Jayavarman VII."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Khmer Empire, a rival to Cham Pa, engaged in prolonged conflicts with Champa from the 10th through the 13th centuries."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ming Dynasty empire that invaded Đại Ngu in 1407, annexed it, and imposed brutal governance including assimilation policies and resource extraction."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ming Empire that invaded Đại Ngu in 1407, captured Hồ Quý Ly and Hồ Hán Thương, and incorporated Đại Ngu into Ming rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Mongol Empire, a major empire that invaded Đại Việt in the 13th century under its rulers and generals."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Mongol Empire, whose military campaigns against Đại Việt were led by generals such as Mông Kha and Hốt Tất Liệt."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Vietnamese Empire government in Hanoi, a monarchic regime resisting transfer of power to the Việt Minh."&lt;SEP&gt;"The short-lived imperial state proclaimed by Bao Đại during the 1945 Japanese occupation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The short-lived imperial state proclaimed by Emperor Bảo Đại during the Japanese occupation to legitimize governance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đế quốc Khmer (Khmer Empire) historically ruled the region including Chân Lạp; Vietnamese activity intersected with Khmer domains."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đế quốc Khmer là đế chế cai trị vùng Khơ Me và khu vực lân cận, thực hiện chiến dịch chinh phục Champa (1190–1203)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đế quốc Khmer ở Đông Nam Á, liên quân với Chăm Pa để quấy nhiễu Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đế quốc Pháp (French Empire) represents the colonial power faced by the Nam Kỳ uprising and its suppression."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đế quốc Việt Nam is referenced as the imperial state in the historical context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đế quốc Việt Nam refers to the Empire of Vietnam, the state structure under which Trần Trọng Kim served before the power shift to Việt Minh."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bcac0a2ed12c8c14d19b01dcb829b2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-561d10717f3ff788e1032021a4cf0614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-750f8e96d1bcbbd9a06d573268723339&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fff36ca9411ec2bf48b0a366dd7589f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b60881720494b2801d1866f2d58b8eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc55d62fe8c98a4db1992322d16a5280&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf0b29bf76d345dfa9da0349d9f546d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4943e96cb569799b168fc03f9b7f0d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dae09351baefd2c96da656733c534ccd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6322ad53528ea4e193eaf9be34d2898</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HARIVARMAN IV&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cham Pa king who led counter-offensives against Khmer incursions and conducted campaigns to defend Cham Pa’s territories, including actions around Vijaya."&lt;SEP&gt;"Champa king who led counter-offensives against Khmer and conducted temple raids along the Mekong."&lt;SEP&gt;"Harivarman IV was a Cham Pa king who led a counterattack to drive Khmer forces out of Cham Pa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Harivarman IV was a Cham king who led counterattacks against Khmer invasions and sought to restore Cham strength."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURYAVARMAN II&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Khmer king who invaded Cham Pa in 1145 and 1149, contributing to Cham–Khmer conflicts and capturing Cham territory."&lt;SEP&gt;"King of the Khmer Empire who invaded Champa in 1145 and again in 1149."&lt;SEP&gt;"Suryavarman II was a Khmer king who invaded Cham Pa in 1145 and again in 1149."&lt;SEP&gt;"Suryavarman II was a king of the Khmer Empire who died in a battle against Champa around 1150."&lt;SEP&gt;"Suryavarman II was a Khmer king who died in battle against Champa around 1150."&lt;SEP&gt;"Suryavarman II was a Khmer Empire king who invaded Cham Pa in the 12th century, attacking Cham sites along the Mekong region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Suryavarman II was a king of the Khmer Empire who died in battle against Cham Pa around 1150."&lt;SEP&gt;"Suryavarman II was a king of the Khmer Empire who died in battle against Champa around 1150, illustrating Khmer-Champa conflict in the 12th century."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;JAYA INDRAVARMAN IV&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cham king who led a surprise raid on the Khmer capital Yasodharapura (Angkor) in 1177, defeating Khmer forces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cham king who led a surprise raid on the Khmer capital Yasodharapura and defeated Khmer forces at Tonlé Sap."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jaya Indravarman IV led a Cham Pa raid into the Khmer capital Yasodharapura (Angkor) during ongoing Cham–Khmer conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jaya Indravarman IV was Paramesvaravarman I's son who is mentioned in Cham affairs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jaya Indravarman IV was a Champa king who led a surprise invasion of Yasodharapura, the Khmer capital, in 1177."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jaya Indravarman IV was Paramesvaravarman I's son who fled southward after the Cham attack."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jaya Indravarman IV was a Cham king who led a surprise raid on the Khmer capital Yasodharapura (Angkor) in 1177, defeating Khmer forces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jaya Indravarman IV, son of Paramesvaravarman I, who fled south during Champa turmoil."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jaya Indravarman IV led a surprise raid on Vijaya in 1177, defeating Cham defenses there."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jaya Indravarman IV, prince of Paramesvaravarman I who fled to the south after his father's death."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vua Chăm Pa, người dẫn đầu cuộc tập kích bất ngờ vào kinh đô Yasodharapura (Angkor) năm 1177, đánh tan Khmer trong trận ở hồ Tonlé Sap."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jaya Indravarman IV was the son of Paramesvaravarman I and fled south during Champa’s conflicts with Đại Cồ Việt."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YASODHARAPURA (ANGKOR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The capital city of the Khmer Empire (Angkor); it was attacked by Cham Pa under Jaya Indravarman IV in 1177."&lt;SEP&gt;"The capital of the Khmer Empire, also known as Angkor (Yasodharapura), a major urban center attacked by Cham forces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yasodharapura, also known as Angkor, was the Khmer capital attacked by Cham Pa forces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yasodharapura, also known as Angkor, served as the capital city and political center of the Khmer Empire."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;JAYAVARMAN VII&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A prominent Khmer king who defeated Cham Pa around 1190, began the conquest of Champa, and eventually placed Cham Pa under Khmer suzerainty for about 17 years."&lt;SEP&gt;"A prominent Khmer king who rose to power and led campaigns, marking a significant phase in Khmer expansion against Cham Pa."&lt;SEP&gt;"A prominent king of Cambodia who took the throne of Campuchia (Cambodia)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jayavarman VII was a Khmer king renowned for repelling Cham forces and initiating the Cham conquest around 1190."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jayavarman VII was a prominent Khmer king who rose to power and ruled over Cambodia."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jayavarman VII was a distinguished Cambodian king who rose to the throne around 1190, defeated the Cham, and began the conquest of Champa, bringing Cham Pa under Khmer rule for about 17 years until 1203."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jayavarman VII was a prominent Khmer king who took the throne and expanded Khmer influence in the region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vua Khmer kiệt xuất lên ngôi và bắt đầu cuộc chinh phục Champa từ năm 1190, cuối cùng khuất phục Cham Pa vào năm 1203."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐẾ QUỐC ABBAS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Abbasid Caliphate; mentioned as part of the broader trade network connected to Champa via maritime routes, including Baghdad."&lt;SEP&gt;"Abbasid Caliphate; the empire centered in Baghdad; Champa traders had ties with it."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Abbasid Caliphate based in Baghdad, part of the broader trade network Cham Pa engaged with during its peak."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Abbasid Caliphate, a vast Islamic empire centered in Baghdad, controlling extensive trade networks and cultural exchange referenced in the context of Cham Pa and regional dynamics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TONLÉ SAP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Large lake near Angkor; site of battles during Khmer-Champa conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tonlé Sap Lake is a large freshwater lake in Cambodia, noted as the site near which a major battle occurred during Cham Pa's invasion of Angkor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tonlé Sap Lake region was a strategic area in Khmer–Cham campaigns and central to Angkor's power base."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tonlé Sap Lake was a key geographic site mentioned in connection with Khmer–Cham conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tonlé Sap Lake, a central Cambodian lake that features in Khmer-Champa history and regional connectivity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tonlé Sap Lake, near which the 1177 Cham Pa attack on Angkor took place."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KAUTHARA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Area in Khánh Hòa where Khmer forces attacked Champa in 950."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kauthara (Khánh Hòa) was a region in central Vietnam involved in early Cham–Khmer clashes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kauthara (Khánh Hòa) is a coastal region in Cham history associated with Vijaya and Khmer campaigns."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;QUẢNG NAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A southern-central Vietnamese region governed from Đàng Trong; part of Nguyễn Hoàng's southern domain and a key area in southern Vietnamese state-building."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central-southern region under Nguyễn Hoàng's influence as part of southern expansion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quảng Nam Province, the area around Indrapura where Cham Pa's early capitals were situated."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coastal central Vietnamese province governed by Nguyễn Hoàng; part of Đàng Trong's southern domain."&lt;SEP&gt;"Region in central Vietnam where Indrapura is located."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quảng Nam Province in central Vietnam; historically includes Indrapura and Cham Pa settlements relevant to Cham history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quảng Nam was a southern region governed by Nguyễn Hoàng, forming part of the Nguyễn lordship's base."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BAGHDAD&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Baghdad is the capital city of the Đế quốc Abbas and a major hub in its network."&lt;SEP&gt;"Baghdad was the capital city of the Abbasid Caliphate, a major hub of commerce and culture on Silk Road networks; in the text, Abbasid authority is referenced as the imperial seat that Champa's traders interacted with."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of the Abbasid Caliphate; referenced as a hub in the Champa trade networks."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANGSARAJA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Angsaraja was a Cham prince who ascended the throne and took the regnal name Jaya Paramesvaravarman II."&lt;SEP&gt;"Angsaraja was a Cham prince who ascended to the throne as Jaya Paramesvaravarman II and restored Cham independence."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hoàng thân Cham đã lên ngôi và lấy hiệu Jaya Paramesvaravarman II vào năm 1220, khôi phục nền độc lập cho Cham Pa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cham noble who took the regnal name Jaya Paramesvaravarman II and reestablished Cham independence before or during Khmer-Champa conflicts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;JAYA PARAMESVARAVARMAN II&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cham Pa king (reign 972–982); killed by Vietnamese forces in 982 following Vietnamese incursions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jaya Paramesvaravarman I was Cham Pa king (reigned 972–982) who was killed during Vietnam-era campaigns."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jaya Paramesvaravarman I was a Cham king who faced Khmer offensives around 982 and met defeat during those campaigns."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jaya Paramesvaravarman II was the Cham Pa ruler who reigned under that regnal name after Angsaraja's accession."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regnal name of Angsaraja, the Cham king who renewed Cham independence and engaged in conflicts with the Khmer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regnal name used by Angsaraja; Cham king who restored independence around 1203."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vua Cham Pa lên ngôi năm 1220 sau Angsaraja, tiếp tục khôi phục nền độc lập của Cham Pa."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BUTUAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Butuan is a Mindanao port town known for its role in Cham-Pa trade competition; historically referred to as Pu-duan."&lt;SEP&gt;"Butuan is a key trading city in Mindanao, Philippines, whose rulers (e.g., Rajah Kiling) contested Cham Pa's trade influence and played a pivotal role in regional diplomacy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Butuan, a city in Mindanao, Philippines; site of Cham-Champa trade rivalries and diplomatic activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Butuan, thành phố ở Mindanao, nơi Cham Pa có các hoạt động giao thương và gặp gỡ với các đối thủ thương mại."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RAJAH KILING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Rajah Kiling was the king of Butuan who pushed diplomatic and commercial competition with Champa to secure trade privileges with China."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rajah Kiling, king of Butuan, promoted diplomatic competition to secure trading privileges with China."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vua của Butuan, người thúc đẩy cạnh tranh ngoại giao nhằm tranh giành đặc quyền buôn bán với Trung Hoa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rajah Kiling was the king of Butuan who pushed to secure exclusive trading privileges with China, challenging Cham Pa's trade privileges."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MINDORO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mindoro Island in the Philippines, cited as a site for Ma-i trade and Cham Pa reach in the era described."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mindoro Island in the Philippines; referenced in relation to Ma-i and Champa trade routes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đảo Mindoro ở Philippines, nơi Ma-i được đặt và là một điểm đến được nhắc tới trong các mô tả thương mại cổ xưa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mindoro is an island in the Philippines identified with Ma-i in historical sources as part of long-distance trade routes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MINDANAO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mindanao Island in the Philippines, the region where Butuan is located and where Cham Pa migration and regional interactions occurred."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mindanao, an island in the southern Philippines; home to Butuan and broader Cham trade networks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đảo Mindanao, Philippines, nơi Pu-duan nằm và có hoạt động giao thương liên quan tới Cham Pa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mindanao is a southern Philippine island where Butuan (Pu-duan) is located and part of the Cham-Pa trade narrative."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MA-I&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ma-i is a historic polity associated with Mindoro, referenced in sources as part of Cham-Pa maritime trade routes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ma-i is a historic polity located on Mindoro, Philippines, mentioned as a destination reached by Cham Pa trade in the period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ma-i, a historical polity described in Chinese sources as located near Mindoro, Philippines, associated with Cham trading routes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ma-i là một nước cổ được cho là ở Mindoro, Philippines, được đề cập trong văn bản của Sách Tống sử."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3</data>
    </node>
