      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sulu Islamic Kingdom; region where Cham migrants settled and interacted with local populations."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Sulu Islamic Kingdom, a political entity in the Sulu archipelago that interacted with Cham Pa and regional polities during the period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vương quốc Hồi giáo Sulu ở vùng đảo Sulu, liên quan đến di cư và xung đột dân cư trong thời kỳ lịch sử."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Islamic Kingdom of Sulu in the southern Philippines, related to regional migration and settlement patterns that intersect with Cham and Philippine histories."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRUNG HOA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"China (Trung Hoa), where Lê Chiêu Thống sought aid and where Qing forces were ultimately deployed."&lt;SEP&gt;"China; major trading partner and destination mentioned in Cham-Champa trade narratives."&lt;SEP&gt;"China; the destination for early trade routes and broader cultural-political exchanges mentioned in connection with An Nam's trade corridors."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical term for China; under Ming rule, Giao Chỉ was administered as part of Trung Hoa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trung Hoa (China) is the broader empire referenced in the narrative; its dynasties interact with Vietnamese frontier rulers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trung Hoa is the Chinese cultural-political sphere referenced as the source of cultural contact and influence in Vietnam.&lt;SEP&gt;"Trung Hoa refers to China, the northern power in relation to Đại Cồ Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"China, the dominant regional power with which rulers competed for trade privileges during the period described."&lt;SEP&gt;"China; context of fragmentation and influence during the era."&lt;SEP&gt;"China; the text discusses Vietnam–China relations historically within a tributary framework."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trung Hoa (China) is a major historical empire cited as part of Cham Pa's trading networks and northern political influence."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trung Hoa (China), quốc gia được nhắc tới trong bối cảnh cạnh tranh thương mại với Cham Pa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trung Hoa là khu vực địa lý và nền văn hóa cổ đại ở Đông Á, được nhắc tới như một nguồn thương mại và ảnh hưởng trong trao đổi với Champa và Đông Nam Á cổ đại."&lt;SEP&gt;"China; referenced as a source of books and influence shaping Vietnamese cultural and administrative policy."&lt;SEP&gt;"China; the destination of the Roman delegation and a focal point of regional political and trade interactions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ABE NO NAKAMARO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Japanese official who served as the An Nam Protectorate (Đô hộ xứ An Nam) administrator from 761 to 767, illustrating early Japanese involvement in Vietnam's frontier governance during Tang-era rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Abe no Nakamaro was a Japanese official who served in An Nam đô hộ phủ from 761 to 767."&lt;SEP&gt;"Một quan Nhật Bản từng làm quan Đô hộ xứ An Nam từ năm 761 đến 767 dưới triều Đường."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Japanese official who served as the Đô hộ xứ An Nam (governor) in An Nam đô hộ phủ from 761 to 767."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THANH MAI TEMPLE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thanh Mai Temple is referenced via a replica bronze bell dated 798 CE; a historical Buddhist site mentioned in the text."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHÂN LẠP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cambodian region; part of the Mekong Delta political context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chenla; a Khmer polity that preceded the Khmer Empire and interacted within the Khmer political sphere in the Mekong Delta region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chân Lạp (Chenla), a historic polity south of the Khmer heartland, involved in regional politics with Khmer and Champa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical region in the Mekong Delta; site of Khmer-Siam-Vietnamese power struggles and Vietnamese expansion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khmer kingdom that eventually conquered Phù Nam around 627 CE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kingdom of Chenla (Cambodia); allied with the uprising led by Mai Thúc Loan."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khmer kingdom that is implied to have conquered Phù Nam around 627 CE, contributing to Phù Nam's collapse."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chân Lạp là vương quốc Đông Nam Á được cho là đã thôn tính Phù Nam vào khoảng năm 627."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chân Lạp refers to the Mekong Delta region and historical Cham lands; Nguyen expansion moved into these territories."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chân Lạp, khu vực phía nam Đông Nam Á liên quan đến các vương triều Khmer và Champa trong lịch sử."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chân Lạp was a neighboring region to the Khmer realm, involved in regional geopolitics with Khmer and Champa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical polity in the region; part of the Khmer-era polities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kingdom of Chenla; allied with Mai Thúc Loan during the rebellion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc55d62fe8c98a4db1992322d16a5280</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PU-DUAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pu-duan (Butuan) on Mindanao, referenced as part of the trade network and regional geography."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pu-duan, also known as Butuan, a Mindanao city cited in Cham-Champa trade narratives."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pu-duan (Butuan) ở Mindanao, địa điểm được nhắc tới trong các hoạt động giao thương của người Chăm."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pu-duan is the Butuan reference (Butuan) in Mindanao, linked to Ma-i trade narratives."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐẠI LA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A historic fortified city mentioned as the renamed capital in the narrative, associated with the Hanoi region and early Vietnamese urban centers."&lt;SEP&gt;"An ancient city fortress that was renamed as Đại La Thành and served as an early capital/stronghold in the northern region of Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"An ancient fortress-city in the northern Vietnamese region, later referenced as Đại La Thành; it functioned as a strategic defensive stronghold and an early center of power during the formation of an independent Vietnamese polity."&lt;SEP&gt;"City renamed from Tống Bình by Cao Biền; a major urban center in early medieval times."&lt;SEP&gt;"Name of the city established by Cao Biền; historically significant as the early stronghold linked to Hanoi's origins."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vương quốc Đại Lý là một vương quốc cổ lân cận, có ảnh hưởng đến tín ngưỡng và văn hóa Phật giáo tại khu vực liên quan đến Việt Nam thời Lý–Trần."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại La Thành is a historic fortress city and former capital, renamed from Đại La to Đại La Thành, central to early Vietnamese statehood."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại La is the ancient capital that later became Hanoi; the text notes it as the successor name to the city of Tống Bình."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại La là kinh đô đầu tiên của nhà Lý sau khi từ Hoa Lư dời tới; sau này được đổi tên thành Thăng Long."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại La thành is a historic fortress-city that Ngô Quyền renamed into a capital site; an early symbol of Vietnamese sovereignty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại La was the capital city after Hoa Lư and before Thăng Long; it later became Thăng Long under Lý Công Uẩn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại La was the capital established by Lý Công Uẩn after leaving Hoa Lư; it was later renamed Thăng Long (Hanoi)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại La was the later name for Hanoi, emerging after Tống Bình as a capital city."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại La, capital established by Lý Công Uẩn, later renamed Thăng Long (Hà Nội)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại La, thành phố cổ ở Bắc Việt Nam, sau này phát triển thành Hà Nội."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Lý, the neighboring kingdom whose Buddhist lineage influenced Vietnamese belief during the Lý era."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NAM TUYỂN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nam Tuyển was a periodic selection process used to recruit local chiefs for high office under the administration described."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam tuyển is the Tang dynasty's periodic recruitment of local frontier chieftains to fill posts in frontier regions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam tuyển is a periodic Tang-era selection ceremony used to appoint local indigenous chiefs to government offices, referenced in the text about governance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam tuyển was a periodic recruitment to appoint local chiefs in Giao Châu during Tang rule."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHÙNG HƯNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Local leader who initiated uprisings against Tang control in northern Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phùng Hưng là hào trưởng bản địa nổi dậy cùng Đỗ Anh Hàn, tham gia khởi nghĩa tại Bắc Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phùng Hưng was a Vietnamese local ruler associated with resistance during the Third Chinese domination of Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Local leader who, with Đỗ Anh Hàn, led a rebellion in 785."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phùng Hưng was a local leader associated with resistance during the Third Chinese domination period in Vietnam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MAI THÚC LOAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Leader of a large uprising from Cửu Đức; proclaimed himself Hắc Đế and allied with Champa, Chân Lạp, and Kim Lân in a major anti-Dương campaign."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mai Thúc Loan was a Vietnamese leader who resisted Chinese rule during the period of Bắc thuộc (Third Chinese domination)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Leader of a major rebellion from Cửu Đức; proclaimed as Hắc Đế (Black Emperor) and allied with Champa, Chenla, and Kim Lân.&lt;SEP&gt;"Mai Thúc Loan was a local leader who played a role in anti-Tang resistance during the same period."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NHÀ TÙY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nhà Tùy (Sui Dynasty), the Chinese dynasty that ruled during the early part of the period discussed in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sui Dynasty; its expansion and eventual overthrow by Đường Cao Tổ influenced southern frontier dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Sui Dynasty, the regime that Đường Cao Tổ overthrew to establish the Tang Dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sui dynasty of China; contemporary power that encountered Lý Bôn and the Vạn Xuân movement and later ceded to the Tang transition."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Sui Dynasty, which Đường Cao Tổ toppled to establish the Tang Dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Sui dynasty, mentioned in the historical narrative as one of the Chinese dynasties governing during the period of northern domination in the region."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THỜI KỲ BẮC THUỘC LẦN THỨ BA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A historical period (111 BCE–40 CE) when the Han dynasty conquered Nam Việt, establishing centralized administration across Nam Việt, subdividing it into Giao Chỉ, Cửu Chân, and Nhật Nam, while local Vietnamese aristocracy retained influence in mountainous areas and Buddhist/Daoist/Confucian ideas began to diffuse."&lt;SEP&gt;"First period of Chinese domination over Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourth period of Chinese domination of Đại Việt (1407–1427) by the Ming Dynasty, ending with Vietnamese resistance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Giai đoạn đầu của sự cai trị của nhà Hán ở vùng đất Việt Nam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Giai đoạn đầu của sự đô hộ của nhà Hán tại miền Bắc Việt Nam, từ 111 BCE đến 40 BCE, ảnh hưởng đến governance và văn hóa khu vực."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical period describing Chinese rule over Vietnam, beginning around 111 BCE and shaping governance, culture, and regional power relations for centuries."&lt;SEP&gt;"Second period of Chinese domination over Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Bắc thuộc period refers to centuries of northern Chinese rule over portions of Vietnam, with varying phases and historical interpretations."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Fourth Chinese Domination (1407–1427) when the Ming Dynasty invaded and controlled Đại Ngu, imposing harsh occupation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Third Chinese domination of Vietnam (602–905), during which An Nam đô hộ phủ governed northern Vietnam under Sui and Tang rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"The first period of Chinese rule in Vietnam (roughly 111 BCE–40 CE), during which the Han Dynasty invaded Nam Việt and established administrative divisions (Giao Chỉ, Cửu Chân, Nhật Nam); local Vietnamese elites retained authority in some mountainous areas, and religious/philosophical ideas such as Buddhism, Daoism, and Confucianism began to enter Vietnamese society."&lt;SEP&gt;"The first period of Chinese rule in northern Việt Nam (111 TCN–40 SCN), initiated by the Han invasion, marks the initial phase of Bắc thuộc."&lt;SEP&gt;"The first phase of Bắc thuộc (111 BCE–40 CE) marks early imperial domination of northern Vietnam by Han China."&lt;SEP&gt;"The fourth Northern domination over Đại Ngu (1407–1427) initiated by Ming invasion and occupation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The second Han domination continued governance over Nam Việt and its surrounding regions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The second period of Chinese rule in Vietnam (43–544 CE), characterized by continued assimilation efforts and political restructuring in the Nam Việt region, following the initial conquest and resistance years.&lt;SEP&gt;"The second period of Han domination continued Han governance and assimilation efforts."&lt;SEP&gt;"The third period of Chinese domination over Vietnam (602–905), involving Han, Sui and Tang rule and related administrative changes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01995274690fe1835d7836e3a481a678&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8643f67e9d45af93e4e82b2745d1580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CỬU ĐỨC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cửu Đức là địa danh cổ ở miền Trung Việt Nam, nguồn gốc của Mai Thúc Loan trong cuộc khởi nghĩa, nay thuộc Hà Tĩnh."&lt;SEP&gt;"Region from which Mai Thúc Loan led a major uprising; today part of Hà Tĩnh."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HÀ TĨNH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Vietnamese province co-origin of the Xô Viết Nghệ Tĩnh movement, where soviet governance was established in localities."&lt;SEP&gt;"A central Vietnam province, part of the historical Nam Việt region during Han administration (alongside Thanh Hóa)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Tĩnh là tỉnh miền Trung Việt Nam, bao gồm khu vực Cửu Đức, nơi diễn ra các sự kiện lịch sử liên quan đến Mai Thúc Loan."&lt;SEP&gt;"Modern Vietnamese province; location of the Cửu Đức region and early uprisings."&lt;SEP&gt;"Region in central Vietnam; part of the historical Cửu Chân area."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical Vietnamese province mentioned as part of the Cửu Chân range."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Tĩnh Province, central Vietnam; a site of the Xô Viết Nghệ – Tĩnh uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Tĩnh is a neighboring province where components of the Nghệ Tĩnh movement and its memorials are similarly documented in this text."&lt;SEP&gt;"A central Vietnamese province; part of Nhật Nam's area under Han rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Tĩnh is a neighboring province in which the Xô Viết Nghệ Tĩnh movement also operated, contributing to the broader uprising in the region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tỉnh Hà Tĩnh; vùng chứa nhiều di tích và di tích lịch sử liên quan phong trào Xô Viết Nghệ Tĩnh."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b60881720494b2801d1866f2d58b8eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe0d1be514abeb684ef939716ecf874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01995274690fe1835d7836e3a481a678&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0468eaa8e1dded2ae27a39363308b486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd3c75fb34fd08ca28a6bdea4defc0f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VÂN NAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Region of Yunnan; site of Nam Chiếu intervention and a corridor on regional power dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vân Nam là khu vực miền nam Trung Quốc, nơi các tuyến đường thương mại cổ đại kết nối An Nam và Trung Hoa tồn tại; sau này có sự can thiệp của Nam Chiếu."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vân Nam (Yunnan) region; its proximity shaped trade routes and military campaigns affecting An Nam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NGỤY LƯỢC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ngụy Lược là tác phẩm cổ từ thế kỷ thứ 3 kể về tuyến đường thủy từ An Nam lên Vân Nam và các liên hệ thương mại."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HẬU HÁN THƯ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hou Han Shu is a historical chronicle referenced in the text as a source documenting events related to the Roman delegation's visit to China."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hậu Hán thư là một tác phẩm lịch sử ghi chép sự kiện liên quan đến phái đoàn La Mã đến Trung Hoa và tuyến đường cổ giữa An Nam và Trung Hoa."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Later Han history book that records, among other things, interactions like a Roman envoy to China in 166, via routes described in the text."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TÀO HUYỀN TĨNH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tào Huyền Tĩnh là tướng nhà Đường, tiến quân vào An Nam, dẹp yên cuộc nổi dậy và quản lý khu vực."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tào Huyền Tĩnh was a Tang general who marched into An Nam to quell uprisings, then defeated the rebels and had their leader Đinh Kiến executed."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐỖ ANH HÀN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Local chieftain who participated in uprisings with Phùng Hưng against Tang control."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đỗ Anh Hàn là hào trưởng bản địa tham gia khởi nghĩa, một trong các thủ lĩnh trước khi Phùng Hưng nổi lên."&lt;SEP&gt;"Local leader who, with Phùng Hưng, instigated a rebellion in 785.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TĨNH HẢI QUÂN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A northern Vietnamese region under Chinese suzerainty that became effectively independent under local rulers between 905–938."&lt;SEP&gt;"Renamed from An Nam in 866, a historical military prefecture and de facto autonomous political entity in medieval Vietnam, later central to regional governance and state-building."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tĩnh Hải quân was the earlier designation of Vietnamese territory before Đinh Bộ Lĩnh's unification into Đại Cồ Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"The renamed administrative entity for An Nam after 866 under Tang suzerainty, marking a shift toward a localized military-administrative structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tĩnh Hải quân was the earlier Vietnamese realm name before Đinh Bộ Lĩnh renamed and reorganized the state as Đại Cồ Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tĩnh Hải quân, the old state name before its renaming to Đại Cồ Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"A semi-autonomous Vietnamese military region initially under local lords and later becomes the de facto independent state; established after renaming from An Nam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tĩnh Hải quân, historical northern Vietnamese realm referenced as the basis for the state's identity during this era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Name used for the Tang administration of northern Vietnam in the broader medieval period; reflects administrative shifts.&lt;SEP&gt;"Name used for the Tang administration of northern Vietnam in the broader medieval period; reflects administrative shifts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tĩnh Hải quân was a regional political-military entity that governed the Vietnamese frontier and later became effectively autonomous, under the Tang Dynasty's title of Tiet Do Su."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tĩnh Hải quân was the historic name of the northern Vietnamese realm before being renamed to Đại Cồ Việt by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HỌ KHÚC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A local ruling clan that governed Tĩnh Hải quân at the start of the autonomous period, laying groundwork for regional governance before Dương Đình Nghệ's rise."&lt;SEP&gt;"A powerful local clan that rose to prominence and laid groundwork for later Vietnamese autonomy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Indigenous Vietnamese clan leaders who ruled Tĩnh Hải quân autonomously before Dương Đình Nghệ."&lt;SEP&gt;"Họ Khúc was a Vietnamese local clan that ruled Tĩnh Hải quân as autonomous leaders before the rise of the Ngô Quyền lineage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Họ Khúc is a local Vietnamese noble clan that ruled Tĩnh Hải quân as autonomous governors before the rise of Dương Đình Nghệ."&lt;SEP&gt;"Local aristocratic clan; their leadership preceded Dương Đình Nghệ in the regional power structure.&lt;SEP&gt;"The Khúc clan were early regional rulers in northern Vietnam whose leadership was succeeded by Dương Đình Nghệ."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DƯƠNG ĐÌNH NGHỆ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Vietnamese leader who ruled Tĩnh Hải quân with autonomous authority (905–938) and repelled Nam Hán incursions; later assassinated in 937."&lt;SEP&gt;"A local warlord who succeeded Họ Khúc in ruling Tĩnh Hải quân; central figure in the early autonomous era before his assassination in 937, which precipitated a succession crisis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Local leader who continued resistance and helped shape the path toward Vietnamese autonomy; part of the Khúc/Dương lineage of rulers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dương Đình Nghệ was a Vietnamese local ruler who governed Tĩnh Hải quân, repelled the Nam Hán invasion, and was assassinated by Kiều Công Tiễn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dương Đình Nghệ was a Vietnamese local ruler who governed Tĩnh Hải quân, repelled Nam Hán invasion, and was later assassinated."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dương Đình Nghệ was a local ruler who followed the Khúc clan's leadership in northern Vietnam, continuing regional governance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Local ruler who followed the Họ Khúc and maintained regional authority."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KIỀU CÔNG TIỄN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Vietnamese official who assassinated Dương Đình Nghệ in 937 to seize power."&lt;SEP&gt;"A military leader who assassinated Dương Đình Nghệ in 937 to seize power, triggering a power struggle that eventually led to Ngô Quyền's rise."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kiều Công Tiễn was a Vietnamese official who assassinated Dương Đình Nghệ to seize power and align with Nam Hán."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kiều Công Tiễn was a Vietnamese noble who assassinated Dương Đình Nghệ to seize the throne and sought external aid from Nam Hán."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NAM HÁN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Southern Han dynasty, a Chinese imperial power that invaded Tĩnh Hải quân and fought against Ngô Quyền's forces at Bach Dang."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southern Han empire of China; invaded Tĩnh Hải quân and fought Vietnamese forces, including in the Bach Dang campaign against Ngô Quyền."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southern Han, a Chinese dynasty that invaded Tĩnh Hải quân around 923 and fought in naval engagements against Vietnamese forces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southern Han; a Chinese dynasty that invaded Tĩnh Hải quân and later clashed with Ngô Quyền's forces at Bach Dang."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DƯƠNG TAM KHA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dương Tam Kha is a historical Vietnamese ruler who usurped the throne from Ngô Xương Ngập, Ngô Quyền's eldest son, triggering early dynastic conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dương Tam Kha is the ruler who usurped the throne from Ngô Xương Ngập, the eldest son of Ngô Quyền, triggering a dynastic power struggle in early Vietnamese history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Quyền's brother-in-law who seized the throne from Ngô Xương Ngập, contributing to the succession crisis after Ngô Quyền's death."&lt;SEP&gt;"The brother-in-law who usurped the throne from Ngô Xương Ngập during the Ngô dynasty succession conflicts (around 965)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dương Tam Kha was a ruler who usurped the throne from Ngô Quyền's heir Ngô Xương Ngập, igniting a dynastic conflict in early Vietnamese history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dương Tam Kha usurped the throne from Ngô Xương Ngập, the eldest son of Ngô Quyền, triggering a dynastic power struggle in early Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dương Tam Kha was Ngô Quyền's brother-in-law who later sought to seize power during the succession crisis after Ngô Quyền's rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dương Tam Kha is a historical figure who usurped the throne from Ngô Xương Ngập, who was Ngô Quyền's eldest son."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dương Tam Kha was the brother-in-law who seized the throne from Ngô Xương Ngập by force."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NGÔ QUYỀN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Vietnamese leader, son-in-law of Dương Đình Nghệ, who defeated Nam Hán at the Battle of Bach Đằng in 938 and established a stronger autonomous rule with Cổ Loa as capital."&lt;SEP&gt;"Leader who defeated the Nam Hán at the Battle of Bach Dang in 938, became king, and selected Cổ Loa as the capital, signaling a shift toward an independent Vietnamese state."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Quyền is the father of Ngô Xương Ngập and a key early Vietnamese ruler whose lineage is challenged after his son's demise."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Quyền was a Vietnamese king and father of Ngô Xương Ngập; his lineage frames the early power struggles described."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Quyền, a foundational king whose lineage and succession become contested after Dương Tam Kha's usurpation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Quyền, a ruler whose lineage becomes a focal point of succession disputes after his reign, father of Ngô Xương Ngập."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnamese ruler who defeated the Nam Hán at Bach Dang, captured political authority, and set the stage for an independent Dai Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Quyền was a Vietnamese ruler who defeated the Nam Hán at the Battle of Bach Dang (938) and established independence for Đại Việt; he later chose Cổ Loa as capital."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Quyền was a king of early Đại Việt and the father of Ngô Xương Ngập, whose throne was seized by Dương Tam Kha."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRẬN BẠCH ĐẰNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Naval battle in 938 where Ngô Quyền defeated the invading Nam Hán, a turning point in Vietnamese independence and state formation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The naval battle on the Bạch Đằng River in 938 where Ngô Quyền defeated the Southern Han navy, a pivotal moment in Vietnamese independence.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NGÔ XƯƠNG NGẬP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Ngô prince who was deposed by Dương Tam Kha during a dynastic struggle."&lt;SEP&gt;"Brother of Ngô Xương Ngập who rebelled and helped force Ngô Xương Ngập from power during the internal conflict."&lt;SEP&gt;"Elder son of Ngô Quyền; the intended heir who was dethroned in the succession crisis by Dương Tam Kha."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Xương Ngập is the eldest son of Ngô Quyền who was dethroned by Dương Tam Kha, leading to further succession conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Xương Ngập was the eldest son of Ngô Quyền and the rightful heir to the throne before being displaced by Dương Tam Kha."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Xương Ngập was the eldest son of Ngô Quyền who was briefly displaced during the succession struggle."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Xương Ngập was the heir of Ngô Quyền who faced a violent succession struggle when Dương Tam Kha pressed his claim to rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Xương Ngập, Ngô Quyền's eldest son, whose throne was seized by Dương Tam Kha and who features in the ensuing succession conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Xương Ngập, eldest son of Ngô Quyền, whose throne was seized by Dương Tam Kha and later contested with Ngô Xương Văn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Xương Ngập, the eldest son of Ngô Quyền, initially slated to inherit the throne but displaced by Dương Tam Kha."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Xương Văn is the younger brother of Ngô Xương Ngập who later led a rebellion against the ruling line."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Xương Văn is the younger brother of Ngô Xương Ngập who later rebelled and helped bring Ngô Xương Ngập back to rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Xương Văn was Ngô Xương Ngập's younger brother who betrayed Ngô Xương Ngập and helped reestablish him as ruler."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Xương Văn, Ngô Xương Ngập's younger brother, rebelled to reinstall Ngô Xương Ngập as ruler."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Xương Văn, younger brother of Ngô Xương Ngập, led a rebellion to restore Ngô Xương Ngập to power."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Xương Văn, younger brother of Ngô Xương Ngập, who rebelled against him and helped bring Ngô Xương Ngập back to rule with Văn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Xương Văn, younger brother of Ngô Xương Ngập, who rebelled and helped reinstall Ngô Xương Ngập on the throne."&lt;SEP&gt;"Younger son of Ngô Quyền; brother of Ngô Xương Ngập, who played a role in the post-Reign succession dynamics following his father's death."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AN NAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An Nam (Trung Kỳ) refers to central Vietnam; part of the Indochina Federation under French suzerainty."&lt;SEP&gt;"An Nam is a historical designation for the central/southern Vietnamese region; the text links it to Trung Kỳ as part of the broader administrative framing under colonial rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"An Nam is a historical northern Vietnamese region and administrative base that became a prosperous port and transshipment hub, receiving goods from southern seas and serving as a corridor to Tang China."&lt;SEP&gt;"An Nam is a historical region used in the Indochina framework, with Trung Kỳ as its associated central region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical name for the region that would become Vietnam, renamed to Tĩnh Hải quân in 866 as part of the administrative reorganization.&lt;SEP&gt;"An Nam is the historical region that was renamed to Tĩnh Hải quân in 866 as part of the administrative lineage described."&lt;SEP&gt;"An Nam is the historical name for the region that would later be renamed to Tĩnh Hải quân in 866."&lt;SEP&gt;"An Nam is the ancient name for the Vietnamese region, later renamed to Tĩnh Hải quân."&lt;SEP&gt;"An Nam is the historical northern Vietnamese region that became a prosperous trading hub and the base of administration under An Nam đô hộ phủ."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical name for Central Vietnam in the text, associated with Trung Kỳ."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical name for Vietnam used by Chinese dynasties; Gia Long proposed replacing it with Nam Việt."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3e2d86a8a15344ff5c25829117f928c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CỔ LOA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An ancient fortress and former capital in the Hanoi area; selected by Ngô Quyền as the capital after his Bach Dang victory."&lt;SEP&gt;"An ancient fortress city chosen as the capital by Ngô Quyền in 939, marking a shift toward a more centralized and independent Vietnamese state."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cổ Loa is the ancient city chosen as the capital by Ngô Quyền in 939, marking a shift toward formal sovereignty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cổ Loa is the ancient capital chosen by Ngô Quyền as the seat of power after the political shifts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NHÀ HẬU LƯƠNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Chinese-style dynasty referenced in the text; Vietnamese rulers during the era paid tribute to it as part of the broader regional dynastic framework."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NHÀ TIỀN LÊ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Early Vietnamese dynasty succeeding the Đinh era, contributing to early state consolidation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Tiền Lê is the dynasty that ruled Vietnam after the Đinh period, continuing the process of state-building."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Tiền Lý was an early Vietnamese dynasty during the Nam–Bắc Dynasties era, preceding the Lý dynasty; it established a brief independent state in the mid-6th century, as referenced in the historical overview."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Tiền Lê (Early Lê) dynasty that followed the Đinh era."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Tiền Lê dynasty of Vietnam, a medieval ruling house that preceded the Lê."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiền Lê Dynasty, the dynasty established by Lê Hoàn after his victories against the Song invasion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRIỀU LÝ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Khung gọi chung cho các triều đại do nhà Lê trị vì, bao gồm Tiền Lê."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ly dynasty (11th–13th centuries) known for centralized governance and consolidation of Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý dynasty; a centralizing Vietnamese dynasty that maintained relations with the Song (Tống)."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Lý dynasty (10th–13th centuries) that centralized power in Vietnam while maintaining tributary ties with the Song dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Lý dynasty consolidated central power in medieval Vietnam, following the period of fragmentation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Tiền Lê dynasty, the early Post-Đinh regime following the Lê lineage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Triều Lý (the Lý dynasty) was established by Lý Công Uẩn and governed Đại Việt, enabling independence, institutional development, and territorial expansion, including capital relocation and reforms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NHÀ MINH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dynasty that followed the Ngô period, part of Vietnam's transition toward centralized monarchy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ming Dynasty of China; rulers who controlled the northern frontier and led military campaigns against Đại Việt, prompting the Lam Sơn uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ming dynasty of China; briefly exerted influence over Vietnam during the period described in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ming dynasty of China; its brief occupation of Vietnam preceded independence restored under the Lê dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Đinh is an early Vietnamese dynasty that followed the Ngô and established centralized rule during the early medieval period."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ming Dynasty of China occupied parts of Vietnam in the 15th century after a brief period of domination."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ming Dynasty of China; considered intervening in Đại Việt and to which Mạc Đăng Dung symbolically surrendered to maintain peace."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ming Dynasty of China; involved in regional diplomacy with Đại Việt and was a potential/intervening power during the Mạc–Lê period, with Mạc Đăng Dung presenting symbolic surrender to maintain peace."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ming Dynasty of China; mentioned as a potential intervening power in Đại Việt's internal conflict and noted for symbolic capitulation to Mạc Đăng Dung to maintain peace."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ming Dynasty of China; viewed as a potential intervenor in Đại Việt affairs, with Mạc Đăng Dung performing symbolic submission to maintain peace."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Đinh dynasty of Vietnam, a medieval ruling house following the Ngô."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Đinh dynasty, an early ruling house in Vietnam's feudal era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Dynasty, the dynasty founded by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh in Đại Cồ Việt."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-561d10717f3ff788e1032021a4cf0614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRIỀU NGUYỄN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nguyễn dynasty that eventually unified and ruled Vietnam in the later era referenced in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyễn Dynasty unified Vietnam in the 19th century."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyễn dynasty, Vietnam's imperial ruling house during this period, including kings Thành Thái and Duy Tân and facing French expansion."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Yuan dynasty of the Mongol Empire, which directed invasions against Đại Việt in 1258, 1285, and 1287–1288 under Mongol leadership."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bcac0a2ed12c8c14d19b01dcb829b2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4943e96cb569799b168fc03f9b7f0d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐÀNG NGOÀI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Northern Vietnam region where Catholic missions and dynastic politics shaped regional power."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern Vietnamese region during the divided feudal era, contrasted with Đàng Trong."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern Vietnamese region historically divided from Đàng Trong; center of early Catholic missions and Trịnh rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern division of the Vietnamese realm during feudal splits; contrasted with Đàng Trong."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern domain ruled by the Trịnh lords."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern realm ruled by the Trịnh lords, centered around the Hanoi region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Northern region of Vietnam during the feudal era, distinct from Đàng Trong."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đàng Ngoài (the Northern realm) was the Trịnh-ruled domain, opposing Đàng Trong."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đàng Ngoài is the northern Vietnamese region ruled by the Trịnh lords, a counterpart to Đàng Trong in the era described."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đàng Ngoài was the Northern realm of Vietnam during the feudal era, one of the two macro-regions along with Đàng Trong."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đàng Ngoại denotes the northern realm ruled by the Trịnh lords, the counterpart to Đàng Trong."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐÀNG TRONG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Southern Vietnam region, center of Nguyễn rule and related expansion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southern Vietnamese region during the divided feudal era, contrasted with Đàng Ngoài."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đàng Trong denotes the southern realm ruled by the Nguyễn lords, seen as the Nam tiến domain in contrast to Đàng Ngoại."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đàng Trong refers to the Nguyễn-controlled inner realm in central-southern Vietnam during the period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southern Vietnamese region governed by the Nguyễn lords; site of territorial expansion and conflicts in the period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southern realm ruled by the Nguyễn lords, covering parts of central and southern Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southern region of Vietnam during the feudal era, distinct from Đàng Ngoài."&lt;SEP&gt;"The southern realm of Đại Việt ruled by the Nguyễn lords during the Trịnh–Nguyễn division."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đàng Trong là vùng lãnh thổ Nam Trung Bộ do chúa Nguyễn quản lý; Tây Sơn đã chiếm lĩnh vùng này trong cuộc nổi dậy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đàng Trong (the Southern realm) was the Nguyễn-ruled domain, distinct from Đàng Ngoài, and served as the base of Nguyễn power."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đàng Trong refers to the southern realm of Vietnam under the Nguyễn lords, a major political and administrative division centered in the south."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southern division of the Vietnamese realm during feudal splits; contrasted with Đàng Ngoài."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southern domain ruled by the Nguyễn lords."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyễn lords' southern realm in central Viet Nam, the political region targeted by Tây Sơn campaigns and the Nguyễn‑Lords conflict."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đàng Trong is the southern region of Vietnam ruled by the Nguyễn lords and a center of early Catholic influence and governance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đàng Trong refers to the southern Vietnamese domain ruled by the Nguyễn Lords, contrasted with the northern Đàng Ngoài led by the Trịnhs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đàng Trong was the Southern realm of Vietnam during the feudal era, another macro-region."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LOẠN 12 SỨ QUÂN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A period of political fragmentation in early 10th-century Vietnam, where power was exercised by twelve local warlords before centralized rule was reestablished."&lt;SEP&gt;"Loạn 12 sứ quân was a large-scale civil war among twelve local warlords that began after the death of Ngô Xương Văn in 965 and was resolved when Đinh Bộ Lĩnh unified the realm by 968."&lt;SEP&gt;"Loạn 12 sứ quân was a large-scale rebellion by twelve Vietnamese warlords that destabilized the realm and lasted until 968, when Đinh Bộ Lĩnh pacified the rivals and set the stage for unification under the Đinh dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Loạn 12 sứ quân, the first large-scale internal war by twelve warlords that destabilized the realm until the rise of Đinh Bộ Lĩnh's unification."&lt;SEP&gt;"Loạn 12 sứ quân refers to the 12 warlords' rebellion period in early Vietnamese history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Loạn 12 sứ quân, the first large-scale civil war in Vietnam sparked by succession struggles, later quelled by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh."&lt;SEP&gt;"Loạn 12 sứ quân was the first large-scale civil war in Vietnam fought among twelve regional warlords, eventually subdued by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh, leading to unification."&lt;SEP&gt;"The 12 Warlords Uprising, a regional fragmentation era in Vietnam's early history."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐINH BỘ LĨNH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Đinh Bộ Lĩnh is the warlord who defeated the 12 rival lords, unified the realm, and founded the Đinh dynasty; he later became known as Đinh Tiên Hoàng."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Bộ Lĩnh was a brilliant military leader who pacified the twelve warlords, founded the Đinh dynasty in 968, and renamed the country's state to Đại Cồ Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Bộ Lĩnh, a military leader who unified Đại Cồ Việt, founded the Đinh dynasty, and assumed the title Đinh Tiên Hoàng."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Bộ Lĩnh, a military strongman who defeated the Loạn 12 sứ quân, unified Đại Cồ Việt, founded the Đinh dynasty, and became Đinh Tiên Hoàng."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Bộ Lĩnh was the military leader who defeated the twelve warlords (Loạn 12 sứ quân), unified the realm, founded the Đinh dynasty, and renamed the state to Đại Cồ Việt."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐINH TIÊN HOÀNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Đinh Bộ Lĩnh, who proclaimed himself as Đinh Tiên Hoàng and renamed the country Đại Cồ Việt, with Hoa Lư as capital."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Tiên Hoàng is the regnal name adopted by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh after founding the Đinh dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Tiên Hoàng is the regnal name adopted by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh after unification, and the founder of the Đinh dynasty; under his rule the state was renamed Đại Cồ Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Tiên Hoàng, the imperial title bestowed on Đinh Bộ Lĩnh upon unification, and the founder of the Đinh dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Tiên Hoàng is the regnal name adopted by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh when he ruled as king."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐINH LIỄN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Đinh Liễn is the eldest son of Đinh Tiên Hoàng; he was murdered with his father in 979, leaving Đinh Toàn as heir."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Liễn, the eldest son of Đinh Tiên Hoàng, held the title Nam Việt vương and was killed during the upheavals following his father's reign; his death leaves Đinh Toàn as heir."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Liễn, Nam Việt vương and the eldest son of Đinh Tiên Hoàng, was assassinated along with his father in 979, triggering a dynastic crisis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Liễn was the eldest son of Đinh Tiên Hoàng and held the title Nam Việt vương; he was a target of the assassination carried out by Đỗ Thích."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐỖ THÍCH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Đỗ Thích is the official who assassinated Đinh Tiên Hoàng and the heir Đinh Liễn, precipitating a dynastic crisis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đỗ Thích was the official who assassinated Đinh Tiên Hoàng in 979, precipitating a period of succession struggles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đỗ Thích, an official who assassinated Đinh Tiên Hoàng and Đinh Liễn, precipitating a dynastic crisis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đỗ Thích, a high official who assassinated Đinh Tiên Hoàng and his heir Đinh Liễn in 979."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đỗ Thích was an official who assassinated Đinh Tiên Hoàng and his eldest son Đinh Liễn, precipitating a dynastic succession."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐINH TOÀN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Đinh Toàn is the six-year-old son who survived the assassination and ascended to the throne."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Toàn was the six-year-old son who survived the assassination and eventually ascended as ruler as a child."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Toàn, the six-year-old son who succeeded after the assassination and became the child sovereign."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Toàn, the six-year-old son of Đinh Tiên Hoàng who ascended the throne after the assassination."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Toàn was the surviving younger son of Đinh Tiên Hoàng, who ascended as a potential heir after the assassination of his brother."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÊ HOÀN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lê Hoàn (Lê Đại Hành) was a military leader who defeated the Song invasion at Chi Lăng and founded the Tiền Lê dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Hoàn was a king of Đại Việt; his death preceded the ascent of Lý Công Uẩn amid a period of turmoil following his reign."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Hoàn was a military leader who repelled the Song invasion at Chi Lăng and was later proclaimed Lê Đại Hành Hoàng đế, founder of the Tiền Lê lineage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Hoàn, known as Thập Đạo Tướng quân, was proclaimed emperor after defeating the Song invaders and founding the Tiền Lê dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vua trước thời Lý, được nhắc đến như vị vua tiền triều Lê mất đi quyền lực trước khi Lý Công Uẩn đăng cơ."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Hoàn, Thập Đạo Tướng quân who became emperor Lê Đại Hành and defeated the Song invasion at Chi Lăng in 981."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Hoàn was a ruler whose death led to the power transition that eventually brought Lý Công Uẩn to prominence and the rise of the Lý dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Hoàn, Thập Đạo Tướng quân, later known as Lê Đại Hành, renowned for defeating the Song invasion at Chi Lăng and founding the Tiền Lê dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Hoàn, former ruler of Đại Việt whose death preceded the rise of Lý Uẩn."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HẦU NHÂN BẢO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hầu Nhân Bảo was a Song dynasty general who was killed by Lê Hoàn's forces at Chi Lăng Pass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hầu Nhân Bảo was the Song dynasty commander killed by Lê Hoàn's ambush at ải Chi Lăng."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hầu Nhân Bảo was the Song dynasty commander who led the invasion and was killed in Lê Hoàn's ambush at ải Chi Lăng in 981."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hầu Nhân Bảo, the Song general killed by Lê Hoàn's forces at Chi Lăng in 981."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hầu Nhân Bảo, the Song dynasty general defeated by Lê Hoàn at Chi Lăng."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hầu Nhân Bảo was the Song dynasty general killed in the Chi Lăng ambush orchestrated by Lê Hoàn."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÊ LONG ĐĨNH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lê Long Đĩnh is the later king who emerged after a bloody succession following Lê Hoàn's era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Long Đĩnh was a later king in the Lê line, noted for his brutal rule before Lý and Trần ascendancy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Long Đĩnh was a monarch who died in 1009, and his death preceded the rise of Lý Công Uẩn to the throne and the founding of the Lý dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Long Đĩnh, later notorious king of Đại Cồ Việt who reigned during the late 10th century and died at a young age."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Long Đĩnh was a king who died in 1009; his death paved the way for Lý Công Uẩn to be enthroned by the court and marked a transition toward the Lý dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Long Đĩnh, son of Lê Hoàn, known for ruthless punishments and died at age 24, marking a violent period of succession."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Long Đĩnh was a Vietnamese king who died in 1009, whose death preceded the rise of Lý Công Uẩn and the founding of the Lý dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Long Đĩnh was a king of the later Lê period known for his ruthless rule and died young at 24."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PARAMESVARAVARMAN I&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Paramesvaravarman I was the Champa king who attacked Đại Cồ Việt and supported Ngô Nhật Khánh during the Loạn 12 sứ quân era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Paramesvaravarman I was the king of Champa who attacked Đại Cồ Việt in 979 under the pretext of aiding Ngô Nhật Khánh."&lt;SEP&gt;"Paramesvaravarman I, king of Champa who attacked Đại Cồ Việt in 979 and allied with Vietnamese warlords against Đinh and later campaigns."&lt;SEP&gt;"Paramesvaravarman I, king of Champa who attacked Đại Cồ Việt in 979, with involvement of Ngô Nhật Khánh."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NGÔ NHẬT KHÁNH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ngô Nhật Khánh was a Vietnamese ex-warlord who sought refuge in Champa and allied with Paramesvaravarman I against Đại Cồ Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Nhật Khánh was a Vietnamese figure whose status as pretext allowed Cham forces to oppose Đại Cồ Việt during the Cham attack."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Nhật Khánh was a Vietnamese former chieftain whose status is used as a pretext for Champa's attack on Đại Cồ Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Nhật Khánh, a former Vietnamese warlord who sought Champa's support against Đại Cồ Việt and was involved in Champa-Anglo interactions during the Loạn 12 sứ quân era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ngô Nhật Khánh, a former Việt warlord who sought Champa support; his actions precipitated Champa attacks on Đại Cồ Việt, and he drowned when plans failed."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HOA LƯ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hoa Lư is the capital city established by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh in 968, located in present-day Ninh Bình."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hoa Lư là kinh đô cổ của Đại Việt trước khi dời đô, nơi Lý Công Uẩn bắt đầu triều đại mới."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hoa Lư is the capital city of Đại Cồ Việt, located in present-day Ninh Bình Province."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hoa Lư was the capital of Đại Cồ Việt before the 1010 relocation of the capital."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hoa Lư, the capital city established by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh in what is now Ninh Bình Province."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hoa Lư was the early capital of Đại Cồ Việt; the capital was moved as part of Lý Công Uẩn's reforms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hoa Lư, the capital chosen by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh upon unification; located in Ninh Bình."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hoa Lư was the capital city of Đại Cồ Việt, located in present-day Ninh Bình Province."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hoa Lư, the former capital of Đại Cồ Việt before the capital relocation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NINH BÌNH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ninh Bình Province, the region that encompasses the Hoa Lư capital."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ninh Bình is the province where Hoa Lư is situated."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ninh Bình is the province where the capital Hoa Lư sits."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ninh Bình is a northern Vietnamese province where Hoa Lư, the capital established by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh, is located."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ninh Bình is the northern Vietnamese province where Hoa Lư sits as the capital location."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐINH DYNASTY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Đinh Dynasty was founded by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh in 968, marking the first unified Vietnamese state after fragmentation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đinh Dynasty; early Vietnamese dynasty after Ngô rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Triều Đinh, triều được thành lập sau khi Đinh Bộ Lĩnh thống nhất đất nước và đổi quốc hiệu thành Đại Cồ Việt."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÝ CÔNG UẨN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lý Công Uẩn là võ tướng cao cấp nắm quyền binh tại kinh đô, được triều thần đồng lòng suy tôn lên ngôi hoàng đế và sáng lập triều Lý; ông đăng cơ, ban Chiếu dời đô và mở đầu thời kỳ thịnh vượng của Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Công Uẩn was a key figure who later founded the Lý dynasty in the early 11th century, following earlier dynastic changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Công Uẩn was a senior military commander (Điện tiền Chỉ huy sứ) who became the founder of the Lý dynasty; in 1010 he moved the capital from Hoa Lư to Đại La, which he later renamed Thăng Long."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Công Uẩn was proclaimed emperor after Lê Đĩnh's death and founded the Lý dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Công Uẩn, the official who rose to throne and founded the Lý dynasty as part of the transition after the Đinh era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Công Uẩn was a high-ranking military commander at the capital who, after Lê Long Đĩnh's death, was unanimously enthroned as emperor in 1009 and founded the Lý dynasty; he moved the capital and initiated state-building in Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Công Uẩn, the Điện tiền Chỉ huy sứ who was unanimously proclaimed emperor, founding the Lý dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Công Uẩn was the official who, with the court's support, was proclaimed emperor and founded the Lý dynasty in Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Công Uẩn, Điện tiền Chỉ huy sứ, became emperor, founded the Lý dynasty, moved the capital from Hoa Lư to Đại La and later Thăng Long."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NHÀ LÝ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lý Dynasty, founded by Lý Công Uẩn, continuing the Vietnamese dynastic succession after the Đinh and Tiền Lê periods."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Lý is a Vietnamese dynasty that consolidated power and expanded the state's administrative framework."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Lý is the Vietnamese dynasty established by Lý Công Uẩn, marking a new era after Đinh and Lê periods."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Lý is the dynasty founded by Lý Công Uẩn, marking a new era in Vietnamese history."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Lê Dynasty restored Vietnamese independence and ruled for centuries."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Lê Dynasty was restored and continued to serve as the nominal ruling house during the dynastic struggles described."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Lê dynasty that restored national independence in the 15th century after Ming occupation, expanding Vietnamese territory."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Lê dynasty, restored after Mạc rule; its rulers included Lê Chiêu Tông and Lê Trang Tông, supported by Nguyễn Kim."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Lê dynasty, restored in the mid-16th century with Nguyễn Kim's support, ruled in parts of Vietnam while real power was contested by Trịnh and Nguyễn lords."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Lý dynasty, a Vietnamese imperial house, ruled Đại Việt during the 11th–12th centuries and laid cultural foundations before its decline at the end of the century."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Lý dynasty, established by Lý Công Uẩn and a major ruling house in medieval Vietnam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4943e96cb569799b168fc03f9b7f0d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÊ ĐẠI HÀNH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lê Hoàn, còn được gọi là Lê Đại Hành Hoàng đế, người sáng lập triều Tiền Lê."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Đại Hành is the regal title of Lê Hoàn, used as an alias in historical sources."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Đại Hành is the regnal name of Lê Hoàn after he declared himself emperor; he founded the Tiền Lê dynasty and led the defense against the Song invasion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Đại Hành, the throne name of Lê Hoàn after he ascended as emperor, renowned for defeating the Song invasion at Chi Lăng in 981."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NHÀ LÝ, NHÀ TRẦN AND NHÀ HỒ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Lý, Trần, and Hồ dynasties are successive ruling houses in Vietnamese history covering later medieval periods."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ẢI CHI LĂNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chi Lăng Pass, a key battlefield where Vietnamese forces defeated the Song invasion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chi Lăng Pass, the site of the ambush that killed Liễu Thăng."&lt;SEP&gt;"ải Chi Lăng is a strategic mountain pass in northern Vietnam where Lê Hoàn lured the Song invaders and defeated Hầu Nhân Bảo in 981, a key moment in resisting invasion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ải Chi Lăng is the mountain pass where Lê Hoàn lured the Song forces into an ambush, leading to a decisive defeat of Hầu Nhân Bảo."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-561d10717f3ff788e1032021a4cf0614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VIỆN SMITHSONIAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Smithsonian Institution, a prominent American museum organization mentioned in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Smithsonian Institution, referenced in relation to Trần-era artifacts in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;"The Smithsonian Institution is a major network of museums and research facilities referenced for its role in preserving and contextualizing historical artifacts and knowledge."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BẢO TÀNG NGHỆ THUẬT METROPOLITAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bảo tàng Nghệ thuật Metropolitan refers to the Metropolitan Museum of Art, cited in the text as a source of artwork examples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bảo tàng nghệ thuật ở Mỹ, nơi trưng bày thạp gốm hoa lam thời Trần (thế kỷ 14)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cleveland Museum of Art, a major American art museum located in Cleveland, Ohio, known for its diverse collections and rotating exhibitions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cleveland Museum of Art, a major art museum located in Cleveland, Ohio."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metropolitan Museum of Art, a major museum referenced in the text in relation to artifacts from the Trần era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metropolitan Museum of Art; listed in the artifact captions in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Cleveland Museum of Art, an art museum located in Cleveland."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Cleveland Museum of Art, located in Cleveland, Ohio, United States, is a major public art museum renowned for its broad collections and rotating exhibitions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Metropolitan Museum of Art (Met) is a world-renowned museum cited here as a reference point for artifacts and cultural history of the region."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Metropolitan Museum of Art in New York City, one of the world's largest museums; noted here for Vietnamese ceramics and related artifacts."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Metropolitan Museum of Art, a flagship museum in New York with a vast collection spanning cultures and eras."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Metropolitan Museum of Art, a major museum referenced in the document."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Metropolitan Museum of Art, cited alongside Smithsonian as institutions showcasing artifacts from the period.&lt;SEP&gt;"The Metropolitan Museum of Art; a major art museum in New York City; referenced here as hosting a ceramic plate in the text."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-561d10717f3ff788e1032021a4cf0614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4943e96cb569799b168fc03f9b7f0d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THĂNG LONG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Capital city besieged and captured by Nguyễn Ánh in 1802; modern Hanoi."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Đại Việt (present-day Hanoi), seized by Tây Sơn in under two months during their northern campaign (1786)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of Đại Việt during the Trần era; repeatedly attacked and burned by Cham; later renamed Đông Đô."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of Đại Việt; later renamed Đông Đô during Hồ Quý Ly's rule; repeatedly targeted during Cham invasions (1370s–1390s)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historic capital name for Hanoi; sieged by Nguyễn Ánh in 1802 and associated with the execution of Toản.&lt;SEP&gt;"Historic name for Hanoi, the capital city associated with the Đại Việt and the Lê dynasty era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical capital name used for Hanoi during the Lê dynasty; Lê Lợi moved the capital back to Thăng Long and the city was associated with Đông Kinh (Đông Đô)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical capital of Đại Việt, relocated before Đông Quan; later associated with Hà Nội."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical name for Hanoi before being renamed Đông Kinh."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical name for Hanoi; used as the capital after restoration and reorganization.&lt;SEP&gt;"Kinh thành Thăng Long (Hà Nội) bị hạ thành trong một chiến dịch của Nghĩa quân Tây Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"The capital city of Đại Việt, where Trần Khắc Chung brought Huyền Trân after the rescue."&lt;SEP&gt;"The name given to Đại La after the capital relocation; the historical precursor to Hanoi (Hà Nội)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thăng Long (Hanoi) was captured by Tây Sơn forces during their northern campaign."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thăng Long is the name adopted for the capital after the move from Đại La, associating with the legend of a dragon and the rise of Hanoi."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thăng Long was the capital of Đại Việt; it suffered Cham Pa raids and sackings, and was renamed Đông Đô during Hồ Quý Ly's reforms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thăng Long, the capital city after relocation, later known as Hanoi."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tên gọi sau này của kinh đô Đại La; nay là Hà Nội."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-561d10717f3ff788e1032021a4cf0614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4943e96cb569799b168fc03f9b7f0d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;QUỐC TỬ GIÁM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Quốc Tử Giám was established as the first national university under the Lý dynasty, serving as the premier institution for higher learning and hosting the Tam khoa exams to select talents."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trường đại học đầu tiên được sáng lập dưới triều Lý nhằm tuyển chọn hiền tài và giáo dục quan viên."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quốc Tử Giám was established by the Lý dynasty as the first national university, central to educating scholars selected through imperial examinations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quốc Tử Giám, the first national university established under the Lý dynasty; symbol of imperial education."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÝ THƯỜNG KIỆT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Danh tướng nhà Lý nổi tiếng với chiến thuật và chỉ huy quân Đại Việt chống Tống; thực hiện chiến dịch biên giới và phòng thủ sông Như Nguyệt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Thường Kiệt was a renowned general who led Đại Việt's campaigns against the Song dynasty, employing the preemptive strike tactic and raiding border bases at Ung Châu, Khâm Châu, and Liêm Châu."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Thường Kiệt, renowned general who led defense against the Song invasion in 1075–1076 and conducted preemptive raids on Song bases at Ung Châu, Khâm Châu, Liêm Châu."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TÔNG ĐẢN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Danh tướng dưới sự lãnh đạo của Lý Thường Kiệt trong chiến dịch biên giới chống Tống."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tông Đản was a general who fought alongside Lý Thường Kiệt during the campaigns against the Song invasion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tông Đản, high-ranking general who collaborated with Lý Thường Kiệt in campaigns against Song."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KHÂM CHÂU&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Khâm Châu was one of the border bases raided by Đại Việt forces in 1075."&lt;SEP&gt;"Một căn cứ biên giới nơi diễn ra các chiến dịch của Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khâm Châu was one of the three Song border bases attacked by Đại Việt during the same campaign."&lt;SEP&gt;"Khâm Châu, Song base attacked during the 1075 campaign."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIÊM CHÂU&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Liêm Châu was one of the border bases raided by Đại Việt forces in 1075."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đồn trú biên giới liên quan đến chiến dịch của Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Liêm Châu was one of the three Song border bases attacked by Đại Việt during the 1075–1076 campaign."&lt;SEP&gt;"Liêm Châu, Song base attacked during the 1075 campaign."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÝ HUỆ TÔNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Emperor of the Lý dynasty who was pressured to abdicate and installed his daughter Lý Chiêu Hoàng on the throne."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Huệ Tông was a king of the Lý dynasty who was forced to abdicate and enter monastic life by Trần Thủ Độ, leading to Lý Chiêu Hoàng's ascension to the throne."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vua nhà Lý bị ép xuất gia và bị thay thế bởi Lý Chiêu Hoàng theo sắp đặt của Trần Thủ Độ."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Huệ Tông was the king who abdicated in 1224, allowing his daughter Lý Chiêu Hoàng to ascend the throne under pressure from Trần Thủ Độ."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Huệ Tông was a king of the Lý dynasty whose throne was displaced when power passed to the Trần."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Huệ Tông, the Lý emperor who was forced to abdicate in 1224."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4943e96cb569799b168fc03f9b7f0d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÝ CHIÊU HOÀNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Con gái út của Lý Huệ Tông, lên ngôi vua Lý năm 1224 dưới sự sắp đặt của Trần Thủ Độ."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Chiêu Hoàng was the youngest daughter of the Lý dynasty who was placed on the throne by Trần Thủ Độ as a figurehead during the dynastic transition to the Trần dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"The eight-year-old princess who was placed on the throne by Lý Huệ Tông, later married Trần Cảnh as part of the political maneuver to establish the Trần Dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Chiêu Hoàng was an eight-year-old queen placed on the throne in 1224 by Trần Thủ Độ, initiating Trần authority over Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Chiêu Hoàng was an 8-year-old queen who ascended the throne of Lý and was later married to Trần Cảnh, enabling the Trần dynasty's rise."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lý Chiêu Hoàng, eight-year-old daughter installed on the throne by Trần Thủ Độ in 1224."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4943e96cb569799b168fc03f9b7f0d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRẦN THỦ ĐỘ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A high-ranking Trần official who orchestrated the purge of the Lý royal family and arranged the marriage between Lý Chiêu Hoàng and Trần Cảnh, enabling the founding of the Trần Dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thái sư nhà Trần, tổ chức việc đưa Lý Chiêu Hoàng lên ngai vàng và ép Lý Huệ Tông xuất gia."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trần Thủ Độ was the powerful Grand Chancellor who orchestrated the abdication of Lý Huệ Tông and installed Lý Chiêu Hoàng on the throne, enabling the transition to the Trần dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trần Thủ Độ was a powerful Thai sư who forced Lý Huệ Tông to abdicate and placed Lý Chiêu Hoàng on the throne in 1224, initiating a Trần influence over the imperial succession."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trần Thủ Độ was a high-ranking regent (Thái sư) who orchestrated the marriage between Lý Chiêu Hoàng and Trần Cảnh and oversaw the transition of power to the Trần dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trần Thủ Độ, Thái sư who orchestrated the 1224 coup and installed Lý Chiêu Hoàng on the throne, beginning Trần–Lý transition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4943e96cb569799b168fc03f9b7f0d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHIẾU DỜI ĐÔ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chiếu dời đô is the 1010 edict issued by Lý Công Uẩn moving the capital from Hoa Lư to Đại La (today Hà Nội), marking a major political shift."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chiếu dời đô was the 1010 imperial edict relocating the capital from Hoa Lư to Đại La, initiating the move to Thăng Long."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chỉ thị năm 1010 chuyển đô từ Hoa Lư sang Đại La, đánh dấu bước ngoặt đô hộ và sự mở rộng của Đại Việt."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRẬN CHIẾN TRÊN QUẢNG TÂY 1075&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Trận đánh cuối năm 1075 giữa Đại Việt và quân Tống tại Quảng Tây, kết thúc bằng thắng lợi của Đại Việt."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CUỘC KHÁNG CHIẾN QUY MÔ LỚN CHỐNG LẠI QUÂN XÂM LƯỢC TỐNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cuộc chiến kháng chiến của Đại Việt chống quân xâm lược Nhà Tống dưới thời nhà Lý."&lt;SEP&gt;"The large-scale resistance by Đại Việt against the Song invasion, circa 1075–1076."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TÂY ĐÔ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Capital moved to Tây Đô in Thanh Hóa under Hồ Quý Ly."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kinh đô Đại Việt từ năm 1397 đến 1407, còn gọi là Tây Đô; Cổng Nam thành Tây Đô là một phần của thành cổ."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tây Đô was the capital of Đại Ngu established under Hồ Quý Ly (in Thanh Hóa)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tây Đô was the western capital of Đại Việt from 1397 to 1407, referenced as the capital city during that period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tây Đô served as the capital of Đại Việt from 1397 to 1407; its site is listed as UNESCO World Heritage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tây Đô, the capital city from 1397 to 1407, later designated as World Heritage site by UNESCO."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHÙA THANH MAI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chùa Thanh Mai (Thanh Mai Pagoda) is an ancient Vietnamese temple; the text references a bronze bell dated 798 CE associated with it."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chùa Thanh Mai is a temple in the region notable for stone inscriptions in Nom and Han characters (1366)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chùa Thanh Mai is a temple where a 1366 stone inscription in Nôm and Han scripts was carved, reflecting cultural heritage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chùa Thanh Mai is a temple where a stone stele inscribed with Nôm and Han characters dating to 1366 was found, reflecting contemporary inscriptions at the site."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chùa Thanh Mai nổi tiếng với bia đá khắc chữ Nôm và chữ Hán (1366)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thanh Mai Pagoda, an ancient temple with a bronze bell dated to 798 CE, mentioned in the left-side image."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chùa Thanh Mai, site of a stone stele carved in Nôm and Han characters (1366)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;UNESCO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"International organization that designates World Heritage Sites; mentioned in connection with Cửa Ngọ Môn and Kinh thành Huế.&lt;SEP&gt;"UNESCO (Tổ chức Giáo dục, Khoa học và Văn hóa của Liên Hợp Quốc) được nhắc tới như đơn vị công nhận và duy trì di sản thế giới, liên quan đến di sản của thời đại Lý–Trần."&lt;SEP&gt;"UNESCO is the international organization that designated Tây Đô (the Nam Thành Tây Đô site&lt;SEP&gt;"UNESCO, the international organization referenced as recognizing the Tây Đô area as a World Heritage site."&lt;SEP&gt;"United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization; Hue's imperial citadel is described as a World Heritage Site by UNESCO."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KỲ THI NHO HỌC TAM KHOA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Kỳ thi Nho học Tam khoa là kỳ thi được tổ chức định kỳ ba năm một lần nhằm tuyển chọn hiền tài trong dân gian thời Nhà Lý."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CỔNG NAM THÀNH TÂY ĐÔ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cổng Nam thành Tây Đô là một phần của Tây Đô, kinh đô Đại Việt từ năm 1397 đến 1407."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cổng Nam thành Tây Đô, gate in Tây Đô, part of UNESCO-listed site."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHẾ MÂN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chế Mân is the Champa king who married Huyền Trân; his marriage alliance with Đại Việt led to the transfer of Cham provinces to Đại Việt, and he is also known by the royal name Jaya Simhavarman III."&lt;SEP&gt;"King of Champa (Champa king Jaya Simhavarman III) who married Huyền Trân, leading to territorial exchanges between Champa and Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chế Mân was the Champa king (Jaya Simhavarman III) who married Princess Huyền Trân and received territories from Đại Việt as part of peace negotiations."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c4943e96cb569799b168fc03f9b7f0d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐẠI VIỆT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A medieval Vietnamese state that was the principal rival of Champa, with Thăng Long as its capital, experiencing invasions, capital relocations, and later reforms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historic Vietnamese kingdom that ruled most of northern Vietnam; its capital was Thăng Long; endured Cham invasions and dynastic upheaval, later renamed Đại Ngu under Hồ Quý Ly with the capital moved to Tây Đô."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical Vietnamese kingdom in northern Vietnam; the political center of the Lê–Mạc conflict and dynastic struggles described in the passage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical Vietnamese kingdom/state that resisted Ming occupation and shaped early modern Vietnamese nationalism."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical Vietnamese realm ruled by successive dynasties, central to the narrative of the Lý and Trần periods."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical Vietnamese state encompassing northern and central territories; used here to denote the broader Vietnamese polity in play during the period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical Vietnamese state that unified and ruled large regions during the Lê dynasty era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Medieval Vietnamese kingdom; the polity for which Lê Lợi fought to restore independence and which later became the basis for the Lê dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Name of the Vietnamese state during the Lê period, i.e., the country ruled by Vietnamese dynasties."&lt;SEP&gt;"The name of Vietnam as a unified kingdom following the early independence period and Ngô Quyền's era."&lt;SEP&gt;"The unified Vietnamese state founded by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh, succeeding the Tĩnh Hải quân.&lt;SEP&gt;"Vương quốc cổ ở miền Bắc Việt Nam; trước khi đổi tên thành Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Cồ Việt is the Vietnamese state name established by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh, with its capital at Hoa Lư; it succeeded the Tĩnh Hải quân."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Cồ Việt is the state founded by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh in 968 after unifying the realm, renaming from Tĩnh Hải quân."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Cồ Việt was the Vietnamese kingdom established by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh after unification, with its capital at Hoa Lư."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Cồ Việt was the early Vietnamese kingdom preceding Đại Việt; its capital Hoa Lư served as a military and administrative center before the relocation to Đại La and the establishment of Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Cồ Việt was the predecessor state to Đại Việt, with Hoa Lư as its early capital before the capital relocation initiated by Lý Công Uẩn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Cồ Việt, the early Vietnamese state established by Đinh Bộ Lĩnh; capital Hoa Lư situates within this realm."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Cồ Việt, the early Vietnamese state that Lý dynasty succeeded."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Việt is the Vietnamese kingdom that faced Cham Pa invasions, experienced late-Trần and Hồ period turmoil, and later confronted Ming domination; its history in this text centers on dynastic struggles and territorial conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Việt is the historical Vietnamese kingdom formed and sustained under the Lý dynasty, representing the nation-building and political sovereignty of the era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Việt is the historical Vietnamese state that emerged after Đinh Bộ Lĩnh's consolidation; Hoa Lư served as its early political center."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Việt was the Vietnamese kingdom established under the Lê dynasty; its late 15th to early 16th century history includes the transition to the Mạc dynasty and a subsequent period of internal strife and decline."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Việt was the Vietnamese state that continued governance after the Lý period and overlapped with the Trần era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Việt was the historic Vietnamese state ruled by successive dynasties; its fortunes fluctuated with dynastic power struggles, including the decline following Lê Thánh Tông's death."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Việt was the historical Vietnamese state in the era mentioned, providing the wider political context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Việt was the historical name for the Vietnamese state and its territory during this period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Việt was the name of the Vietnamese kingdom during the Lý era, a precursor to the modern Vietnamese state."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Việt, the name of the Vietnamese kingdom during and after the Lý dynasty."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277e0e3e66eb14031c1d448dfa63e42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27e11721fea6555c621a81136e468f9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-561d10717f3ff788e1032021a4cf0614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6033ac15f370150074084b2248ba04f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4943e96cb569799b168fc03f9b7f0d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHẾ BỒNG NGA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cham king (Po Binasuor) who reigned 1360–1390; led Cham campaigns against Đại Việt, defeated Trần Duệ Tông at Vijaya in 1377, and conducted raids on Đại Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chế Bồng Nga (Po Binasuor) was the Cham Pa king who reigned circa 1360–1390, led major Cham campaigns against Đại Việt, and was associated with the 1377 assault that killed Trần Duệ Tông at Vijaya."&lt;SEP&gt;"King of Champa who led campaigns against Đại Việt; his forces attacked Thăng Long and Vijaya; associated with the death of Trần Duệ Tông at Vijaya; alias Po Binasuor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRẦN DUỆ TÔNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Emperor of the Trần dynasty who was killed during Cham invasions at Vijaya in 1377."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trần Duệ Tông was a Trần dynasty king who was killed in 1377 during Cham Pa attacks at Vijaya."&lt;SEP&gt;"Emperor of the Trần dynasty; killed in 1377 during Cham invasions, at Vijaya, by Cham forces under Chế Bồng Nga."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRẦN KHÁT CHÂN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"General of Đại Việt who repelled the final Cham naval assault on Thăng Long in 1390, using firepower (huo pháo) to sink Cham ships and help kill Cham king."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trần Khát Chân was a Vietnamese general who defended Thăng Long in 1390 against Cham Pa incursions, successfully halting their naval assault."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trần Khát Chân was a Vietnamese general who halted the final Cham naval assault on Thăng Long in 1390 using firepower, contributing to the defense."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HỒ QUÝ LY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hồ Quý Ly was a powerful official who seized control, established the Hồ dynasty, reformed the state, renamed Đại Việt to Đại Ngu, and moved the capital to Tây Đô."&lt;SEP&gt;"Powerful official who manipulated the Trần dynasty, established the Hồ rule, instituted reforms (education, currency, artillery), and abdicated in 1401."&lt;SEP&gt;"Powerful official who overthrew the Trần dynasty, established the Hồ dynasty, renamed Đại Việt to Đại Ngu, and moved the capital to Tây Đô; abdicated in 1401 in favor of his son Hồ Hán Thương."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HỒ HÁN THƯƠNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hồ Hán Thương was the son of Hồ Quý Ly and a key figure during the transitional period when the Hồ dynasty held power."&lt;SEP&gt;"Son of Hồ Quý Ly; captured by the Ming during the 1407 invasion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Son of Hồ Quý Ly; ascended to the throne as emperor after his father abdicated in 1401; later faced Ming invasion and the fall of the Hồ dynasty."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRẦN QUÝ KHOÁNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Leader of the early resistance against Ming invasion; his faction achieved initial victories but internal purges led to defeat in 1413."&lt;SEP&gt;"Leader of the initial resistance against Ming domination; early victories but internal betrayal (executing two generals) led to defeat by 1413."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trần Quý Khoáng led early resistance against the Ming invasion; his leadership initially yielded some victories but internal mistrust and betrayal weakened the rebellion, culminating in defeat in 1413."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GIAO CHỈ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Han administrative division centered in the Red River Delta, referenced as a prefecture in ancient records and a focal point of Han governance in the area."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Han-administered district in the Red River Delta within Nam Việt."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Han-era district (quận) within Nam Việt, corresponding to the Red River Delta region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Administrative region governed by Chinese authorities; later divided into districts as part of Dương Kiên's governance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Administrative unit under Ming rule, formed when Đại Việt's territory was annexed and reorganized as part of Ming territory (quận Giao Chỉ)."&lt;SEP&gt;"An ancient Chinese administrative region in northern Vietnam; center of governance for Chinese authorities in the area; Lưu Phương governed it."&lt;SEP&gt;"An ancient northern Vietnamese administrative region referenced as the land governed by the commander Khâu Hòa during the period described."&lt;SEP&gt;"An ancient region in present-day northern Vietnam; came under multiple dynastic influences and served as a strategic administrative area."&lt;SEP&gt;"An ancient region in what is now northern Vietnam; contested by Chinese dynasties and local polities; captured by Jin and later retaken during the Three Kingdoms era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ancient Vietnamese prefecture that was incorporated under Ming rule as part of the occupation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Giao Châu is a frontier region in northern Vietnam; a Viên general died en route from Chăm Pa to the north, and Khâu Hòa was sent to govern the land."&lt;SEP&gt;"Giao Châu is a historic region in northern Vietnam; in the text, Khâu Hòa is sent to govern this frontier region, illustrating Tang–Champa–Vietnamese border dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Giao Châu was a frontier region of northern Vietnam under Tang administration."&lt;SEP&gt;"Giao Châu was a historic Chinese-administered region in northern Vietnam, described in the text as part of the administrative framework during Tang-era governance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Giao Châu was the northern Vietnamese region under Tang rule and a focal point for Tang administration and Nam tuyển recruitment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Giao Châu was the region the appointed governor Khâu Hòa was sent to govern."&lt;SEP&gt;"Giao Chỉ là vùng đất ở miền Bắc Việt Nam, nơi diễn ra xâm nhập và chiếm đóng giữa các thế lực Trung Hoa cổ đại (266–271) và sau này được Đông Ngô tái chiếm."&lt;SEP&gt;"Giao Chỉ was a historical Vietnamese region; under Ming rule its administration was restructured as part of the occupation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Han-era prefecture in the Red River Delta; part of Nam Việt under Han administration."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01995274690fe1835d7836e3a481a678&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3369f1880c271bd6eae21c84158803a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐẠI NGU&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Name adopted by Đại Việt under Hồ Quý Ly; its capital was moved to Tây Đô"&lt;SEP&gt;"Đại Việt renamed as Đại Ngu by Hồ Quý Ly; its capital was moved to Tây Đô during the Hồ dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"State name adopted by Hồ Quý Ly when he took power; a new regime with reforms, later affected by Ming occupation."&lt;SEP&gt;"State name adopted by Hồ Quý Ly after seizing the throne; capital moved; represents the Hồ dynasty period."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VĂN MIẾU&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Temple of Literature in Hanoi; mentioned in the text as a landmark in Vietnamese cultural memory."&lt;SEP&gt;"Temple of Literature in Hà Nội, housing stone stelae and names of successful candidates from 1442–1779."&lt;SEP&gt;"Văn Miếu, the Temple of Literature in Hanoi, contains stone stele and reflects the Confucian scholarly tradition of Đại Việt."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1095d21a9a487ebf90afcd96d02737d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHONG TRÀO KHÁNG CHIẾN (TRẦN QUÝ KHOÁNG)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
