    <node id="&quot;THỜI ĐẠI KHÁM PHÁ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Age of Discovery when Western merchants and missionaries began to arrive in Vietnam, signaling increased contact with Europe and broader global exchange."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Age of Exploration during which Western merchants and missionaries began arriving in Vietnam, initiating sustained cross-cultural contact, especially from Portugal."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Age of Exploration in which Western merchants and missionaries began to reach Vietnam, notably the Portuguese, marking early contact and Christianization."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Age of Exploration, during which Western merchants and missionaries began arriving in Vietnam and beginning cross-cultural contact."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BỒ ĐÀO NHA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bồ Đào Nha (Portugal) is cited as a source of many missionaries, illustrating European involvement in Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bồ Đào Nha (Portugal) were among the first Western powers to reach Vietnam, marking early contact and the beginning of Christian missionary activity in the region (notably around 1533)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bồ Đào Nha is a European country whose missionaries and traders played a central role in early Catholic missions in Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bồ Đào Nha, the Portuguese merchants who arrived in Vietnam in 1516, helping establish early Western trade networks."&lt;SEP&gt;"The nation of Portugal, whose merchants and missionaries arrived in Vietnam during the Age of Exploration, introducing Christianity from around 1533."&lt;SEP&gt;"Portugal; early European traders who transmitted firearms and advanced military technology to Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Portugal (Bồ Đào Nha) is referenced as part of early Western contact and missionary activity in Vietnam via Portuguese Jesuit missions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Portugal; origin of the missionary priests described in the text, who were active in Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Portugal; supplied fortification tech and firearms to the Nguyễn defense network."&lt;SEP&gt;"Portugal; origin of many missionaries active in Vietnam's early Catholic missions and cultural exchanges."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NHÀ MẠC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A dynasty established by Mạc Đăng Dung after deposing the Lê; ruled the northern Đại Việt; toppled in 1592 after Trịnh Tùng's campaigns."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Mạc dynasty, ruling in the north after seizing the throne in 1527, engaged in conflict with the Lê and later faced dissolution of power in northern Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Mạc dynasty, established by Mạc Đăng Dung in 1527 after ousting the Lê, ruled the northern part of Đại Việt during the Lê–Mạc conflict and persisted until its defeat by the Trịnh–Nguyễn forces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Mạc was a short-lived Vietnamese dynasty that intervened in the post-Lê era power struggles."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Mạc Dynasty was the northern regime established after Mạc Đăng Dung's coup, opposing the Lê dynasty."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NGUYỄN KIM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A noble who led the opposition to the Mạc, helped restore the Lê dynasty (Lê Chiêu Tông), and positioned his family to dominate southern Vietnam, founding a power base in Đàng Trong."&lt;SEP&gt;"A powerful figure who led anti-Mạc efforts, helped restore the Lê dynasty, and installed Lê Trang Tông as part of the Lê revival."&lt;SEP&gt;"A prominent general who opposed the Mạc regime and helped restore the Lê dynasty by promoting Lê Trang Tông; father of Nguyễn Hoàng."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Kim was a powerful minister whose actions led to the restoration of the Lê dynasty by placing Lê Trang Tông on the throne; he shaped the early southern power base and influenced the northern succession."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÊ CHIÊU TÔNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Le king restored during the Lê–Mạc conflict by Nguyễn Kim, enabling the temporary restoration of Lê authority in central Vietnam (Thanh Hóa region)."&lt;SEP&gt;"An Lê king who was deposed by Mạc Đăng Dung; father of Lê Trang Tông."&lt;SEP&gt;"An Lê monarch displaced during the Mạc usurpation, later part of the dynastic restoration narrative."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Chiêu Tông was a Lê king installed during the restoration efforts led by Nguyễn Kim to reestablish the Lê dynasty in Thanh Hóa after Mạc usurpation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÊ TRANG TÔNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Lê king installed by Nguyễn Kim in the south to reestablish the Lê dynasty's rule in Đàng Trong's sphere, contributing to the dynasty's northern-southern flux."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Lê king restored to the throne by Nguyễn Kim; son of Lê Chiêu Tông."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Lê monarch installed by Nguyễn Kim to restore the Lê dynasty in Thanh Hóa during the revival period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Trang Tông was a Lê king restored to the throne with Nguyễn Kim's support, central to the restoration of the Lê dynasty in the south."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MẠC ĐĂNG DUNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A powerful minister who, in 1527, forced Lê to abdicate, seized the throne to establish the Mạc dynasty, and later abdicated in favor of his son Mạc Đăng Doanh; he also engaged symbolically with the Ming dynasty to preserve peace."&lt;SEP&gt;"A powerful official who seized the throne in 1527, founding the Mạc dynasty; later symbolically surrendered to the Ming to preserve peace, and fathered Mạc Đăng Doanh."&lt;SEP&gt;"A powerful official who toppled the Lê crown, established the Mạc dynasty in the north, and later performed a symbolic surrender to the Ming to preserve peace."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mạc Đăng Dung was a high-ranking official (Thái sư) who seized the throne from the Lê to found the Mạc dynasty, later consolidating power and navigating opposition, including the conflict with Nguyễn Kim and the Lê restoration."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRỊNH KIỂM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Trịnh lord who centralized power in the north after Nguyễn Kim's line and played a key role in the period's dynastic politics."&lt;SEP&gt;"A powerful general who, after Nguyễn Kim's assassination, seized military control and governed the northern realm; father of Trịnh Tùng."&lt;SEP&gt;"A powerful military leader, son-in-law of Nguyễn Kim, who consolidated northern power after Nguyễn Kim's influence waned and guided the Trịnh–Nguyễn regime dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Trịnh lord defeated in Tây Sơn's northern campaign; his forces were routed, and he reportedly suicide during the campaign against Tây Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh Khải là vua của Trịnh lords; quân Trịnh bị Tây Sơn đánh tan và sau đó Trịnh Khải tự sát khi bị áp giải."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh Khải, a Trịnh lord who faced Tây Sơn forces; his regime collapsed and he died by suicide as Tây Sơn forces swept north."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh Kiểm was Nguyễn Kim's son-in-law who seized power in the north after Nguyễn Kim's death, guiding northern politics and military efforts against the Mạc."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRỊNH TÙNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Trịnh lord who captured Hà Nội in 1592, ending major Mạc resistance and executing the last Mạc king Mạc Mậu Hợp."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh Tùng was the Trịnh lord who died, leading to the accession of Trịnh Tráng in 1623."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Trịnh lord who led efforts and was the predecessor to Trịnh Tráng; his death preceded the succession of Trịnh Tráng in 1623, marking a transition in Đàng Ngoài's leadership."&lt;SEP&gt;"Leader of the Trịnh lords who captured Hanoi in 1592 and ended Mạc remnants; executed Mạc Mậu Hợp."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Trịnh lord who led campaigns against the Mạc in the north, captured Hà Nội in 1592, and executed Mạc Mậu Hợp, consolidating northern rule for a period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh Tùng was the Trịnh lord whose death preceded Trịnh Tráng's rise and shaped the early phase of the Trịnh–Nguyễn division."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh Tùng was the son of Trịnh Kiểm who led campaigns against the Mạc in the north and captured Hà Nội in 1592, effectively ending major Mạc resistance there."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MẠC MẬU HỢP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mạc Mậu Hợp was the last Mạc king, killed by Trịnh Tùng's forces in Hà Nội in 1592, marking the decline of the Mạc dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Mạc king who ruled the northern realm and was killed by Trịnh Tùng in 1592, marking a decisive defeat of the Mạc in the north."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Mạc king who was executed by Trịnh Tùng in 1592, marking the collapse of Mạc power in the north."&lt;SEP&gt;"The last Mạc king who was defeated and executed during the northern conflicts in 1592."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THUẬN HÓA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Historical central-southern Vietnamese region appointed as the initial governance region for Nguyễn Hoàng."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical southern region governed by Nguyễn Hoàng, spanning from Quảng Bình to Bình Định and central Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thuận Hóa is a central southern Vietnamese region that served as a governance base for Nguyễn Hoàng in Đàng Trong, stretching from modern-day Quảng Bình to Bình Định."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thuận Hóa was a central-southern province governed by Nguyễn Hoàng, anchoring the Đàng Trong domain."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;QUẢNG BÌNH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A central Vietnamese province mentioned as part of the southern domain under Nguyễn Hoàng's governance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coastal central Vietnam region; associated with Nhật Nam in certain periods."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coastal region included in the southern domain influenced by Nguyễn Hoàng, extending the Nguyễn reach northwards in the central coast area."&lt;SEP&gt;"Province in central Vietnam within the southern domain of Nguyễn Hoàng."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quảng Bình Province in central Vietnam; part of Cham Pa's coastal territorial extent along the central Vietnamese coast."&lt;SEP&gt;"Part of Nhật Nam; region cited in the Nam Việt administrative context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Province in central Vietnam; part of the Cham Pa territorial extent."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phía nam của Nhật Nam; một phần của vùng đất Nhật Nam theo mô tả thời Bắc thuộc."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quảng Bình là tỉnh ven biển miền Trung Việt Nam, được mô tả như một phần phạm vi lãnh thổ của Champa từ Quảng Bình tới Phan Thiết (Bình Thuận)."&lt;SEP&gt;"A central Vietnamese province; included within Nhật Nam's reach in the Han administrative system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quảng Bình Province in central Vietnam; part of the land governed by Nguyễn Hoàng as he expanded southward from Thanh Hóa toward central Vietnam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01995274690fe1835d7836e3a481a678&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BÌNH ĐỊNH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bình Định Province in central Vietnam; part of the southern Đàng Trong domain established by Nguyễn Hoàng."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coastal province in central Vietnam, part of the Đàng Trong region under Nguyễn Hoàng and the southern Vietnamese polity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Region in central Vietnam within Nguyễn Hoàng's southern domain."&lt;SEP&gt;"South-central Vietnamese province forming part of the Nguyễn southern domain."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnamese province; Vijaya is located here."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NAM–BẮC TRIỀU&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The division between the northern Mạc regime and the southern Lê restoration, a central feature of the Lê–Mạc conflict (1527–1592)."&lt;SEP&gt;"The dynastic civil war between the Northern Mạc regime and the Southern Lê revival that defined early period divisions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THỜI KỲ CHIA CẮT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Period of Division (1527–1802) in Vietnam, characterized by northern Mạc dominance and southern Lê restoration.&lt;SEP&gt;"The broader period (1527–1802) of southern–northern dynastic division in Vietnam, setting the stage for later Trịnh–Nguyễn partition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THỜI KỲ TRỊNH – NGUYỄN PHÂN TRANH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A long-running period of rivalry between the Trịnh lords in the north and the Nguyễn lords in the south (roughly 1600–1777)."&lt;SEP&gt;"The long-running north–south power struggle between the Trịnh Lords and the Nguyễn Lords (1600–1777)."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Trịnh–Nguyễn split (1600–1777) describes a long power struggle between northern Trịnh lords and southern Nguyễn lords."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MẠC PHỦ TOKUGAWA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Tokugawa Shogunate of Japan, which maintained diplomatic exchanges with Nguyễn Hoàng and influenced Japanese activity in Hội An."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Tokugawa shogunate of Japan; conducted diplomatic correspondence with Nguyễn Hoàng (1589–1612) and is linked to the Japanese quarter in Hội An."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Tokugawa shogunate of Japan; engaged in diplomacy with Vietnam and facilitated the Japanese presence in Hội An during the period of Vietnamese foreign relations."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Tokugawa shogunate was the Japanese ruling authority during Edo, which maintained diplomatic correspondence with Đại Việt and supported a Japanese presence in Hội An."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NHẬT BẢN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Han administrative zone from Quảng Bình to Huế, part of the governance structure in the southern half of Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Han-administered region from Quảng Bình to Huế."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Han-era district from Quảng Bình to Huế."&lt;SEP&gt;"Empire of Japan; its occupation shaped Indochina politics and facilitated the establishment of the Đế quốc Việt Nam during World War II."&lt;SEP&gt;"Han authorities established Nhật Nam as a Han commandery to administer the region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Imperial Japan, the occupying force in Vietnam, whose forces battled Việt Minh in multiple engagements and used tanks to pressure resistance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Imperial Japan, which invaded and occupied parts of Indochina and was a rival in the anti-colonial struggle."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan, a country that established early contact with Vietnam through merchants and missionaries; its diplomatic and trade exchanges contributed to regional dynamics, including a Japanese quarter in Hội An."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan, aggressive imperial power that invaded and occupied parts of Indochina during World War II."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan, mentioned as the country whose fascist forces indirectly supported the crackdown in Saigon."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan, referenced as the country associated with fascist influence in the text (via indirect support to suppression in Saigon)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan, the country that hosted Vietnamese reformers and provided a base for movements like Đông Du and Duy Tân."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan, the country that hosted Vietnamese reformists and provided a base for education and revolutionary activities (contextual to Đông Du and related movements)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan, the country where Vietnamese reformists studied and where nationalist organizations established branches."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan, where Vietnamese reformists went to study under Đông Du and where revolutionary organizations in Japan were established."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan, whose occupation of Indochina shaped WWII-era politics and Vietnamese resistance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan; described as the origin of ships trading with Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan; its merchants and missionaries engaged with Vietnam during this era, facilitating relations and exchange."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan; maintained long-standing trade and diplomatic exchanges with Vietnam, including the 1591 diplomatic relation and presence of a Japanese quarter in Hội An."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan; occupied Indochina in 1940 and influenced Vietnamese resistance efforts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan; occupying power whose troops are described as stationed near the Governor-General's Palace during the event."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan; origin of some missionaries in the history described."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan; origin of some missionaries mentioned in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Japan; referenced as a country whose fascist leadership indirectly supported the crackdown in Saigon."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhật (Japan) is referenced with respect to occupying or guarding the Governor-General's palace area."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhật Bản (Japan) is mentioned as a source of missionaries for Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhật Bản (Japan) is the country where Vietnamese reform movements (notably Đông Du) sought education and modernization, shaping cross-cultural political strategies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhật Bản (Japan) is the occupying power in Indochina at the time and a key player in the 1945 political-military context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhật Bản (Japan) maintained long-standing diplomatic and trade relations with Đại Việt, with formal ties beginning around 1591."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhật Bản is a country of origin for Catholic missionaries mentioned in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhật Bản, the country whose merchant ships traded with Vietnam during the period, signaling early cross-Asian trade links."&lt;SEP&gt;"Region from Quảng Bình to Huế; part of Nam Việt under Han administration."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Japanese Empire, which invaded and occupied parts of Indochina and Vietnam around 1940, contributing to the dual domination by colonial powers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01995274690fe1835d7836e3a481a678&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0858721f576cf19954031c342a7d2bff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bcac0a2ed12c8c14d19b01dcb829b2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-750f8e96d1bcbbd9a06d573268723339&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e0d2b12d6dc3ace3b2354166252a164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fff36ca9411ec2bf48b0a366dd7589f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b60881720494b2801d1866f2d58b8eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf0b29bf76d345dfa9da0349d9f546d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dae09351baefd2c96da656733c534ccd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HỘI AN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A major Vietnamese port town where a bustling Japanese quarter formed as a result of Japan–Vietnam relations (around 1604)."&lt;SEP&gt;"A port city in Quảng Nam where a bustling Japanese quarter developed after diplomatic exchanges with Japan."&lt;SEP&gt;"A port city where a Japanese quarter formed as part of early contact with Nhật Bản and Tokugawa-era diplomacy; a hub of East Asian trade and exchange in this era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hội An is a port city where a lively Japanese quarter developed as Japan–Vietnam trade intensified."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SÔNG HỒNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Red River (Sông Hồng), a major northern Vietnam river that forms a geographic landmark around Hà Nội (Đông Kinh)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Red River (Sông Hồng), a major river in northern Vietnam that shapes the geography and the Red River Delta, and is described as central to early agriculture and state formation in the region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Red River that flows past Hà Nội and was a geographic reference in the narrative."&lt;SEP&gt;"Red River, a major geographic feature near Hà Nội and the capital region."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Red River (Sông Hồng) is a geographic landmark referenced in relation to Hanoi and northern Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Red River, a key geographic feature around which campaigns near Hanoi unfolded."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Red River, a major geographic landmark near Hanoi used in strategic calculations."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Red River; identified as a key waterway ('tuyến đường thủy') used for moving goods from An Nam upstream toward Vân Nam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sông Hồng is the Red River from which the capital area (Đông Kinh/Hà Nội) is observed in 1688."&lt;SEP&gt;"Red River; major geographic feature shaping northern Vietnamese settlement and Bronze Age cultures."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Red River; a maritime/riverine route used to move goods northward from An Nam toward Vân Nam (Yunnan) and toward Kunming and Chengdu.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc782d7f1eac567ce6188dd679cd4c53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc55d62fe8c98a4db1992322d16a5280&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHÚA TRỊNH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A northern ruler (Trịnh family) who held power in Đàng Ngoài and challenged Nguyễn influence, central to the Trịnh–Nguyễn divide."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chúa Trịnh is the lord who held power in Đàng Ngoại during the Trịnh–Nguyễn division and opposed the Nguyễn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chúa Trịnh is the northern feudal lord who held power in Đàng Ngoài and led military actions against the Nguyễn during the Trịnh–Nguyễn phân tranh (1600–1775)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chúa Trịnh is the title of the Trịnh family rulers who controlled Đàng Ngoài (the North) and pursued power in opposition to the Nguyễn, shaping the Trịnh–Nguyễn partition and northern governance in 17th-century Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Trịnh lords who held de facto power in the north during the Lê–Trịnh era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ruler in Đàng Ngoài who established centralized state structures, budgeting, coinage, decimal measurement, printing, and national historiography."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Trịnh lords were the northern rulers (Đàng Ngoài) who opposed the Nguyễn in the south and sought control over Phú Xuân."&lt;SEP&gt;"The ruling Trịnh lords who held real power in the Bắc triều during the Trịnh–Nguyễn era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rulers of the Bắc triều during the Trịnh–Nguyễn period; de facto power in the northern realm and key rival in the century-long Trịnh–Nguyễn contest."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chúa Trịnh was the Trịnh lord who governed Đàng Ngoài and modern central administration, including fiscal and military matters."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Trịnh Lords (Chúa Trịnh) were the ruling northern power after the defeat of the Mạc, holding real political control in Đại Việt."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHÚA NGUYỄN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A southern ruler in Đàng Trong who expanded southward and shaped Nguyễn-led policy and territorial growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chúa Nguyễn is the Nguyễn lord who rules Đàng Trong and consolidates southern domain, moving the capital to Phú Xuân (Huế)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chúa Nguyễn was the ruling dynasty of the Nguyễn lords who controlled Đàng Trong; they resisted Tây Sơn expansion."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyễn lords who ruled the southern domain and shaped the Trịnh–Nguyễn balance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chúa Nguyễn là dòng họ cai trị tại Đàng Trong, quản lý vùng Nam Trung Bộ và đối đầu với phong trào Tây Sơn.&lt;SEP&gt;"Ruler in Đàng Trong who expanded southward, consolidated control, and extended Vietnamese influence toward the Mekong Delta."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyễn lords ruled Đàng Trong (the southern domain), maintaining autonomy from the Trịnh while nominally honoring the Le dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyễn lords who ruled Đàng Trong and rivaled the Trịnh in the Trịnh–Nguyễn division."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chúa Nguyễn is the southern feudal lord ruling Đàng Trong, opposing the Trịnh and expanding Nguyễn-controlled territories, with the capital moved to Phú Xuân (Hue) under his line."&lt;SEP&gt;"Noble ruling house in southern Vietnam, rulers of Đàng Trong; central players in the Nguyễn–Tây Sơn conflict and governance of Gia Định region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rulers of Đàng Trong in the south; a central rival to the Trịnh lords in the north, shaping the Trịnh–Nguyễn partition era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chúa Nguyễn is the lord who ruled Đàng Trong; initiated policies to resist Trịnh; established Phú Xuân as capital."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chúa Nguyễn was the Nguyễn lord who expanded southward and consolidated control over the southern territories (Đàng Trong)."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyễn lords who ruled Đàng Trong; their authority was challenged and ultimately toppled by Tây Sơn campaigns."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyễn Lords (Chúa Nguyễn) ruled Đàng Trong in the south, expanding influence and sustaining a distinct regional state."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRỊNH TẠC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Trịnh general who led counterattacks and ultimately pushed Nguyễn forces back, leading to a settlement in the partition war."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Trịnh lord who defeated the final Mạc stronghold in Cao Bằng in 1677, consolidating the Bắc triều."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh Tạc led the Trịnh forces during the later phase of the Trịnh–Nguyễn conflict and ultimately conceded peace with Nguyễn Phúc Tần in 1672."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh Tạc was a Trịnh lord who extended northern power by defeating remaining Mạc forces, culminating in the destruction of the Mạc remnants in Cao Bằng in 1677 and solidifying Trịnh dominance in the north."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh Tạc commanded Trịnh forces, countering Nguyễn offensives and later negotiating peace; played a key role in the Trịnh–Nguyễn conflict."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh Tạc was a Trịnh lord who led military campaigns against the Mạc in the north and ultimately extinguished the final Mạc strongholds, consolidating northern power."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh Tạc became the ruling Trịnh lord who later accepted a ceasefire and negotiated peace with the Nguyễn."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NGUYỄN PHÚC NGUYÊN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nguyễn Phúc Nguyên continued Nguyễn's stance of independence from the Trịnh, expanded southern control, and faced Trịnh offensives."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Phúc Nguyên is the son of Nguyễn Hoàng who succeeded him and continued Nguyễn policy in Đàng Trong."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Phúc Nguyên was the sixth son of Nguyễn Hoàng, succeeding his father to continue the Nguyễn policy of independence from the Trịnh while nominally honoring the Lê dynasty; he further built southern governance and defenses such as the Lũy Thầy fortifications, and oversaw relations with foreign powers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Phúc Tần was a Nguyễn ruler during the Trịnh–Nguyễn conflict and engaged in ongoing hostilities and eventual conciliations with the Trịnh."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Phúc Tần was the Nguyễn ruler who concluded the Trịnh–Nguyễn conflict with Trịnh Tạc and established peace in 1672, stabilizing northern-southern relations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Phúc Tần, a Chúa Nguyễn, played a key role in concluding the peace with Trịnh Tạc."&lt;SEP&gt;"Son of Nguyễn Phúc Nguyên; continued Nguyễn rule and engaged in periodic peace talks with the Trịnh."&lt;SEP&gt;"The sixth son of Nguyễn Hoàng; succeeded his father and continued Nguyễn policy in Đàng Trong, resisting Trịnh influence and expanding southern defense."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRỊNH TRÁNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Trịnh leader who launched campaigns against the Nguyễn to seize Phú Xuân during the Trịnh–Nguyễn partition war, but failed to secure Phú Xuân."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh Tráng became the Trịnh lord in 1623 after Trịnh Tùng's death and led military campaigns against the Nguyễn, including a 1627 invasion aimed at southward expansion that ultimately failed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh Tráng became the Trịnh lord in 1623 after Trịnh Tùng's death, continuing northern leadership during the Trịnh–Nguyễn partition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh Tráng succeeded his father Trịnh Tùng as ruler and led major offensives against the Nguyễn in the south."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trịnh Tráng led campaigns against the Nguyễn and attempted to assert control in the south during the Trịnh–Nguyễn conflict."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KINH ĐÔ KẺ CHỢ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Capital of the Đàng Ngoại, located in Đông Kinh (Hà Nội)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kinh đô Kẻ Chợ is the historical capital described as Đông Kinh (Đông Kinh hay Hà Nội) in 1688, located along the Sông Hồng (Red River)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHÚ XUÂN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Capital city and imperial seat of Tây Sơn under Quang Trung, located in the central region (the city later associated with Huế)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of the Nguyễn dynasty; captured by Nguyễn Ánh in 1801; known today as Hue."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Đàng Trong; modern-day Huế."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital near Huế; captured by Nguyễn Ánh in 1801.&lt;SEP&gt;"Phú Xuân (Huế) functioned as the Tây Sơn capital and later the base of Nguyễn Huệ’s rule as Emperor Quang Trung."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phú Xuân (Huế) was the capital city (Huế) under Nguyễn dynasty control."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phú Xuân was the Nguyễn capital moved from the north; it lies in the region of present-day Huế."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phú Xuân was the capital of the Nguyễn lords (Đàng Trong) after relocation by Nguyễn Hoàng; later associated with Hue."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phú Xuân, còn gọi là Huế, là kinh đô và nơi triều Tây Sơn đặt thủ phủ; Phú Xuân được nhắc đến như trọng tâm quyền lực ở miền Trung."&lt;SEP&gt;"The southern capital moved by Nguyễn Hoàng; later identified with Huế."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SÔNG GIANH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A river that functioned as a geographic boundary during the Trịnh–Nguyễn partition wars, with Nguyễn forces launching campaigns across it to challenge Trịnh control (notably from 1651 onward)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sông Gianh served as a geographic boundary in campaigns between the Trịnh and Nguyễn territories."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sông Gianh is the river crossing used in campaigns; Nguyễn campaigns crossed it to assert control over southern lands."&lt;SEP&gt;"River used as a boundary and route for Nguyễn offensives into Trịnh territories."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LŨY THẦY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A fortification system built for defense; associated with Nguyễn efforts and fortified with artillery from Portugal."&lt;SEP&gt;"A fortress near Phú Xuân used by Nguyễn forces as a defensive stronghold in conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lũy Thầy refers to the fortified defensive works built by the Nguyễn to protect Phú Xuân and southern lands."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lũy Thầy are fortifications built by Nguyễn forces to defend against Trịnh offensives."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DÒNG TÊN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dòng Tên (Order of Jesus) was a Jesuit missionary order with significant influence in 17th-century Vietnam, shaping Catholic missions in both Đàng Ngoài and Đàng Trong."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dòng Tên (Society of Jesus), a Catholic religious order central to missionary work in Vietnam and to early efforts to systematize Vietnamese writing and education."&lt;SEP&gt;"Society of Jesus, a Catholic religious order that played a central role in early missionary activity in Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Jesuit order whose missionaries significantly influenced Catholicism in Vietnam and contributed to early linguistic work."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dòng Tên (Society of Jesus) is a Catholic missionary order with a significant role in 17th-century Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dòng Tên (the Society of Jesus) is a Catholic missionary order that played a central role in evangelization in Vietnam and was involved in efforts to Latinize Vietnamese script (Quốc ngữ)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jesuit order that established Catholic missions in 17th-century Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Society of Jesus, a Catholic order that played a leading role in Vietnam's missionary activities and in collaboration with other orders."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MARCO POLO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Marco Polo is cited as part of the early Western contact network with Asia, illustrating long-standing international connections that preceded later European influence in Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Venetian explorer associated with early European contact with Asia and the Roman world."&lt;SEP&gt;"Marco Polo visited in 1292, illustrating early Western engagement with the region that later connected to broader Western contact with An Nam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Venetian explorer who visited Champa and Đại Việt, documenting his impressions of the region."&lt;SEP&gt;"Venetian explorer whose 1292 voyage is referenced as part of early Western contact with Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Marco Polo, the Venetian explorer, visited both Champa and Đại Việt in the late 13th century."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4943e96cb569799b168fc03f9b7f0d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALEXANDRE DE RHODES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Jesuit priest who played a key role in early Vietnamese Christian history and the Latinization of Vietnamese writing."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alexandre de Rhodes was a Jesuit priest (Đắc Lộ) who played a pivotal role in Catholic missions in Vietnam and contributed to Latinizing the Vietnamese script; his 1651 dictionary work (Dictionarium Annamiticum Lusitanum et Latinum) is noted in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alexandre de Rhodes (Đắc Lộ) was a Jesuit priest from the papal domain who helped standardize Latin-based Vietnamese writing (Chữ Quốc ngữ) through scholarly work with Portuguese missionaries in the 17th century."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jesuit priest who played a major role in Christian missions in Vietnam and contributed to Latinizing Vietnamese scripts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GIROLAMO MAIORICA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Jesuit missionary whose Nôm writings are recognized as a milestone in Vietnamese literary history, reflecting Catholic scholarly activity in Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Jesuit priest whose Nôm writings contributed to early Vietnamese literature and language documentation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Girolamo Maiorica was a Jesuit missionary whose Nom-related writings in Vietnam are cited as part of the era's cultural and linguistic exchange."&lt;SEP&gt;"Girolamo Maiorica was a priest whose Vietnamese Nom writings are highlighted as a milestone in Vietnamese literature."&lt;SEP&gt;"Girolamo Maiorica was a missionary whose writings in chữ Nôm are regarded as a milestone in Vietnamese literary history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jesuit missionary whose Nôm works are cited as an example of Catholic influence in Vietnam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÃNH ĐỊA GIÁO HOÀNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Papal States, the papal territory from which Jesuit missions (Dòng Tên) originated and influenced Catholic missions in Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Papal States (Holy See) as the papal domain; referenced in context of Catholic mission origins in Vietnam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DICTIONARIUM ANNAMITICUM LUSITANUM ET LATINUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Latin-Vietnamese-Latin dictionary compiled by Alexandre de Rhodes, published in 1651, central to Latinizing Vietnamese script."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Latin-Vietnamese-Portuguese dictionary compiled by Alexandre de Rhodes, foundational to chữ Quốc ngữ."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Latin title for the Vietnamese–Latin dictionary mentioned in the text; the same work as Từ điển Việt–Bồ–La, illustrating cross-cultural linguistic standardization and missionary scholarship (1651 publication)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NGUYỄN ÁNH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Nguyễn lord who moved south to secure the southern frontier, appointed to govern Thuận Hóa–Quảng Nam and establishing Nguyễn lord control in Đàng Trong; father of later Nguyễn rulers."&lt;SEP&gt;"A journalist and nationalist who helped secure the license to open Đông Kinh Nghĩa Thục."&lt;SEP&gt;"A leading cadre of the Indochinese Communist Party; listed among cadres executed during the crackdown on the Nam Kỳ khởi nghĩa."&lt;SEP&gt;"A prominent Vietnamese journalist and administrator who helped secure the license to open Đông Kinh Nghĩa Thục."&lt;SEP&gt;"A prominent leader of the Indochinese Communist movement who was among those executed during the crackdown on the Nam Kỳ uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"A prominent leader of the Indochinese Communist movement who was executed by the French during the crackdown on the Nam Kỳ uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"A scholar who served as Giám học (head teacher) at Đông Kinh Nghĩa Thục, helping to oversee the school."&lt;SEP&gt;"A scholar who served as the head of instruction (Giám học) at Đông Kinh Nghĩa Thục."&lt;SEP&gt;"A senior member of the Indochinese Communist Party who was executed during the crackdown on the nam kỳ uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"Brother of Nguyễn Hoàng, whose potential challenge to power prompted Trịnh Kiểm to maneuver for greater control; his position reflects the intra-family power struggles of the era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bí thư Thành ủy who met with uprising organizers and was arrested by the French at Catinat."&lt;SEP&gt;"Founder/leader of the Tây Sơn movement, initiating the rebellion that challenged Nguyễn lords and laid groundwork for Tây Sơn rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"General Secretary of the Communist Party of Vietnam who officially launched the Đổi Mới reforms at the 1986 Party Congress."&lt;SEP&gt;"General Secretary of the Communist Party of Vietnam who officially launched Đổi Mới at the 6th Party Congress in 1986.&lt;SEP&gt;"General Secretary of the Indochina Communist Party; arrested in Saigon in 1940 along with other leaders."&lt;SEP&gt;"Leader of the Tây Sơn movement who spearheaded the northern campaigns, later became Hoàng đế Quang Trung, and oversaw major military and political actions of Tây Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Member of the Central Committee; arrested in 1940 during the crackdown; appointed as a central party official in the Nam Kỳ network."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Du is a renowned poet who wrote Truyện Kiều, a cornerstone of Vietnamese literature."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Hoàng began the southern consolidation, declared himself Chúa, ended supporting the Trịnh, and relocated the capital to Phú Xuân. (Today: Huế)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Hoàng officially proclaimed himself Chúa (ruler) of the southern domain and ended paying tribute to the Trịnh; he moved the capital to Phú Xuân, the precursor to Huế."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Hoàng officially proclaimed himself Chúa and moved the capital to Phú Xuân, founding a southern Nguyễn domain."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Hoàng was a Vietnamese feudal lord who formally proclaimed himself Chúa (ruler) in 1600, ended paying tribute or providing military support to the Trịnh, and moved the southern capital to Phú Xuân (present-day Huế); he ruled the southern domain for about 55 years until his death in 1613, laying the groundwork for Nguyễn-ruled Đàng Trong in the south."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Hoàng, Nguyễn Kim's son, moved south to govern Thuận Hóa and Quảng Nam, establishing the southern Nguyễn domain and laying groundwork for Đàng Trong."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Huệ là thủ lĩnh của Nghĩa quân Tây Sơn; sau này lên ngôi hoàng đế với niên hiệu Quang Trung và chỉ huy các chiến dịch lớn, bao quát cả miền Bắc và miền Nam nhằm thống nhất đất nước."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Huệ was one of the leaders of the Tây Sơn uprising, eventually becoming emperor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Huệ, leader of the Tây Sơn rebellion, led northern campaigns against the Trịnh, captured Thăng Long, and subsequently took the imperial title Quang Trung."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Hữu Tiến, a Central Party Committee member, arrested in Chợ Lớn on July 30, 1940."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Hữu Tiến, Ủy viên Trung ương Đảng, was arrested on 30 July 1940 at Chợ Lớn during the crackdown.&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Hữu Đang is a Viêt Minh journalist who reads the ceremony program and introduces the Provisional Government."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Khang was a Việt Minh representative who directed Hanoi uprising efforts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Lữ was one of the leaders of the Tây Sơn uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Như Hạnh was the Bí thư Thành ủy Nam Kỳ; a senior revolutionary leader who went to the meeting at 160 đường Dayot to hear and disseminate the lệnh khởi nghĩa; he was later arrested by the French authorities at bót Catinat."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Nhạc là một lãnh đạo của phong trào Tây Sơn; được xem như Thái Đức Hoàng đế trước khi nhường ngôi cho Quang Trung."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Nhạc was one of the leaders of the Tây Sơn uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Nhạc, an elder leader of the Tây Sơn, headed the movement before Nguyễn Huệ's rise to power."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Quyết was a Việt Minh representative involved in Hanoi uprising efforts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Quyền held the position of Giám học at Đông Kinh Nghĩa Thục, contributing to its governance and curriculum."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Uông was a son of Nguyễn Kim, involved in the early Nguyễn–Lê–Mạc power struggles; his existence and potential threat to the Nguyễn Hoàng line are referenced in the narrative as part of the dynastic contest and fear of rivals.&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Uông, elder brother of Nguyễn Hoàng; a member of the Nguyễn family involved in the dynastic power dynamics during the Lê–Mạc era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Văn Cừ was the General Secretary of the Indochinese Communist Party; he was arrested in Saigon on January 17, 1940."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Văn Cừ, Tổng Bí thư of the party, was arrested in Saigon as part of the crackdown on the Nam Kỳ uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Văn Linh, as General Secretary, led the Đổi Mới reforms initiated by the Communist Party."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Văn Linh, as General Secretary, led Đổi Mới reforms within the party."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Văn Linh, the General Secretary of the Communist Party of Vietnam, officially launched Đổi Mới reforms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Văn Vĩnh helped secure official authorization to open Đông Kinh Nghĩa Thục, signing for the license to operate the school."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Ánh là hoàng tôn của chúa Nguyễn, sống sót sau khi Tây Sơn chiếm Đàng Trong và quay trở lại tranh quyền lực; về sau được trợ giúp bởi Xiêm La và Pháp."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Ánh, later known as Gia Long, unified Vietnam and led campaigns against the Tây Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nguyễn Ánh, the last Nguyễn lord claimant, fled to Siam (Xiêm La) in 1784 to seek military aid against the Tây Sơn, returned with around 50,000 Siamese troops to regain power, and later pursued the recapture of southern territories such as Gia Định."&lt;SEP&gt;"Older brother of Nguyễn Hoàng; a member of the Nguyễn clan during the Lê–Mạc–Trịnh era."&lt;SEP&gt;"One of the authors of the painting Xô Viết Nghệ Tĩnh."&lt;SEP&gt;"One of the authors who painted the large lacquer painting Xô Viết Nghệ Tĩnh."&lt;SEP&gt;"One of the leaders of the Tây Sơn uprising, alongside Nguyễn Lữ and Nguyễn Huệ."&lt;SEP&gt;"One of the leaders of the Tây Sơn uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"One of the leaders of the Tây Sơn uprising; later crowned as Emperor Quang Trung."&lt;SEP&gt;"One of the three Nguyễn brothers who led the Tây Sơn uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"One of the three Nguyễn brothers who led the Tây Sơn uprising; a pivotal figure in Vietnamese history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Scholar who served as the Giám học (principal) of Đông Kinh Nghĩa Thục."&lt;SEP&gt;"Secretary of Thành ủy who met with local revolt organizers at 160 đường Dayot to hear and disseminate the revolt orders; later arrested by French authorities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Son of Nguyễn Kim who moved south to govern Thuận Hóa–Quảng Nam, consolidating southern power and shaping the Trịnh–Nguyễn balance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Son of Nguyễn Kim who moved south to Đàng Trong, seeking safety from Trịnh Kiểm and establishing a strong governance base in Thuận Hóa–Quảng Nam, shaping the southern domain."&lt;SEP&gt;"The surviving heir of the Nguyễn lords who fled to Siam after Tây Sơn ascended, returned with a Siamese military force to reclaim power, established Gia Định as a base, and eventually founded the Nguyễn dynasty as Gia Long."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnamese poet and author famous for Truyện Kiều."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnamese ruler who rebuilt southern forces, defeated the Tây Sơn, captured Phú Xuân (1801) and Thăng Long (1802), and founded the Nguyễn dynasty as Gia Long."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnamese ruler who unified parts of the country, founded the Nguyễn dynasty, and later became Emperor Gia Long; central figure in the era described."&lt;SEP&gt;"Writer who helped secure the license to open Đông Kinh Nghĩa Thục, contributing to its establishment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0858721f576cf19954031c342a7d2bff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bcac0a2ed12c8c14d19b01dcb829b2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bfa206adeee22a1ede2ef2ae0ad9711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65a62981dbf13b2b72920ca1960170b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-750f8e96d1bcbbd9a06d573268723339&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7da10df4c22499f6e1fbebde9e539db2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e0d2b12d6dc3ace3b2354166252a164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa6f1c379fafd63c9e5491579d159fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4820a393047d71ddfc9dd25445f040a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÊ CHIÊU THỐNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Final Lê dynasty ruler who sought aid from Qing (Càn Long) to regain the throne after Tây Sơn rise and fled to China when defeated."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Chiêu Thống là vua cuối cùng nhà Hậu Lê; chạy sang Trung Hoa cầu viện Hoàng đế Càn Long khi đối đầu với Tây Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Chiêu Thống, the last Lê emperor, fled to China and sought Qing (Trung Hoa) intervention to reclaim the throne."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BÁ ĐA LỘC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Vietnamese-French official who led volunteers (including Dayot) and died in Saigon in 1799."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bá Đa Lộc (Pigneaux de Behaine), a French diplomat and churchman who sought French military aid for Nguyễn Ánh and helped broker negotiations toward the Versailles treaty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bá Đa Lộc là giám mục Pháp đã lên đường tìm kiếm sự hỗ trợ quân sự cho Nguyễn Ánh và làm trung gian đàm phán Hiệp ước Versailles (1787); sau đó quay lại Việt Nam với tàu chiến và binh lính."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bá Đa Lộc was a Vietnamese official who organized Nguyễn Ánh's navy and military efforts in the late 18th century."&lt;SEP&gt;"French bishop who traveled to France to seek military aid for Nguyễn Ánh; served as intermediary in the negotiations that produced the Hiệp ước Versailles (1787)."&lt;SEP&gt;"A patron whose volunteers included Jean-Marie Dayot; Dayot was one of his volunteers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;JEAN-MARIE DAYOT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A French volunteer who reorganized Nguyễn Ánh's naval forces along European lines and aided in the 1792 victory over the Tây Sơn navy at Quy Nhơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jean-Marie Dayot, a French volunteer who reorganized Nguyễn Ánh's navy and helped defeat Tây Sơn at Quy Nhơn in 1792."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jean-Marie Dayot là tình nguyện viên người Pháp đã tổ chức lại lực lượng thủy quân của Nguyễn Ánh theo mô hình châu Âu và giúp đánh bại Tây Sơn tại Quy Nhơn (1792)."&lt;SEP&gt;"French volunteer who reorganized Nguyễn Ánh's naval forces along European lines and helped defeat Tây Sơn at Quy Nhơn in 1792."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jean-Marie Dayot was one of Bá Đa Lộc's volunteers who contributed to the military efforts around Quy Nhơn in 1792."&lt;SEP&gt;"A French volunteer who reorganized Nguyễn Ánh's naval forces in 1792 and led a naval engagement at Quy Nhơn, linked to Bá Đa Lộc."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LOUIS XVI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"King of France who hosted Bá Đa Lộc in Paris and became the central figure in negotiations for a Versailles treaty with Nguyễn Ánh's faction; his reign ended before the treaty could be fully realized due to the French Revolution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Louis XVI là vua Pháp; Hiệp ước Versailles (1787) được đàm phán trong thời kỳ ông trị vì, hứa viện trợ quân sự cho Nguyễn Ánh thông qua Bá Đa Lộc."&lt;SEP&gt;"Louis XVI, King of France, involved in discussions with Bá Đa Lộc about French support for Nguyễn Ánh and the Versailles negotiations."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRẬN RẠCH GẦM – XOÀI MÚT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A major river battle during the Nguyễn–Tây Sơn conflict where Nguyễn Ánh's Siamese-backed forces were defeated by Tây Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"A naval battle where the Siamese-backed Nguyễn Ánh forces were defeated by Tây Sơn under Nguyễn Huệ."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trận Rạch Gầm – Xoài Mút là trận đánh lớn mà đạo quân Xiêm được Nguyễn Ánh hỗ trợ nhằm giành lại quyền lực, nhưng đã bị đánh tan tác."&lt;SEP&gt;"Major river battle during the Nguyễn–Tây Sơn conflict, part of the late 18th-century civil war in southern Vietnam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIỆP ƯỚC VERSAILLES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Versailles Treaty (1787) negotiated with France for military aid to Nguyễn Ánh, though its implementation was disrupted by the French Revolution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Treaty negotiations around 1787 between Nguyễn Ánh's faction and France, mediated by Bá Đa Lộc; France pledged military aid in exchange for territorial concessions, but the plan collapsed after the French Revolution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;QUY NHƠN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Central Vietnamese city where Tây Sơn rebellion began in 1771."&lt;SEP&gt;"City in central Vietnam; birthplace of the Tây Sơn uprising and focal point of early insurgent activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coastal city in central Vietnam; site of the 1792 battle where Nguyễn Ánh's forces defeated the Tây Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quy Nhơn, a coastal city in Bình Định Province, was the site of the 1792 naval engagement where Tây Sơn defeated Dayot’s forces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quy Nhơn là địa điểm diễn ra trận chiến naval nơi Nguyễn Ánh đánh bại quân Tây Sơn tại Quy Nhơn vào năm 1792."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coastal city in central Vietnam; site of naval engagement where Dayot's reorganized Nguyễn Ánh fleet defeated Tây Sơn in 1792."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quy Nhơn is a coastal city where Nguyễn Ánh's forces defeated Tây Sơn in 1792."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quy Nhơn is the birthplace and early center of the Tây Sơn uprising in 1771."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coastal city in Bình Định where Nguyễn Ánh's forces defeated Tây Sơn naval forces in 1792; site of military action and fortifications."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GIA ĐỊNH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A historic province around present-day Ho Chi Minh City, part of the southern region that French forces expanded into."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gia Định (Sài Gòn) served as a strategic base retaken by Nguyễn Ánh in his struggle against the Tây Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gia Định is a historic citadel near Saigon, marking the southern frontier of the Nguyễn dynasty and a focal point of early French expansion into Nam Kỳ."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gia Định is the southern Vietnamese citadel cited in the text as the place referred to by the name Sài Gòn; it represents the early urban center that became Saigon (present-day Ho Chi Minh City) and was the target of French siege and expansion from 1859 to 1867."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gia Định province was a theater of uprising activity and host to several key actions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gia Định was the southern region and center of Saigon; it served as a key strategically important territory during the French campaign."&lt;SEP&gt;"Giá Định (Sài Gòn) là căn cứ địa của Nguyễn Ánh sau khi chiếm lại, trở thành căn cứ lực lượng của ông."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gia Định is a historic southern Vietnamese city/region, the core of the Saigon area; central to early French expansion and colonial administration in Cochinchina."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gia Định was the southern province and fortress area around which Saigon developed, later captured by French forces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Province including Hóc Môn, where the uprising operated."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historic city in southern Vietnam, the capital area that became part of Saigon; captured by French during the siege of Saigon (1859–1867) and later integrated into Nam Kỳ (Cochinchina)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Province/region encompassing Hóc Môn and other uprising activities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65a62981dbf13b2b72920ca1960170b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3e2d86a8a15344ff5c25829117f928c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PUDUCHERRY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"French colonial territory in India used as a base to recruit ships and volunteers for Nguyễn Ánh's military efforts in Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Port city in present-day India; a French colonial enclave used by Bá Đa Lộc to seek military aid and recruit troops for Nguyễn Ánh before returning to Vietnam in 1788."&lt;SEP&gt;"Puducherry (Ấn Độ) là nơi Bá Đa Lộc tập kết tàu chiến và binh lính trước khi trở về Việt Nam năm 1788."&lt;SEP&gt;"Puducherry, a French colonial enclave in India, was where Bá Đa Lộc travelled to secure support, purchase ships, and recruit troops."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VERSAILLES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Versailles, the site of diplomatic negotiations associated with the Versailles treaty between Nguyễn Ánh’s faction and France."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HẢI TẶC NAM TRUNG HOA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lực lượng hải tặc Nam Trung Hoa được Tây Sơn tài trợ và huấn luyện để mở rộng ảnh hưởng và chiến thuật lực lượng biển."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam Trung Hoa pirates were a powerful maritime group operating in the region and playing a notable role in the era's power dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pirate alliance in the Nam Trung Hoa region; financed and trained by Tây Sơn leadership as part of southern policy and sea-power."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pirate alliance in the southern China region; financed and trained by Hoàng đế Quang Trung as part of Tây Sơn's southern policy and sea-power strategy."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HOÀNG ĐẾ QUANG TRUNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Emperor Quang Trung, the imperial name adopted by Nguyễn Huệ when he established his rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Temple name of Nguyễn Huệ after he established Tây Sơn rule; ruled from Phú Xuân (Huế), led campaigns to the north, and was associated with rebuilding and reform efforts, including southern policy such as supporting pirate alliances."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NƯỚC PHÁP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"France, involved in diplomacy and potential military aid for Nguyễn Ánh via Bá Đa Lộc."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ẤN ĐỘ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A country in South Asia mentioned as the origin of volunteers who return to Vietnam in 1788."&lt;SEP&gt;"An Asian country referenced as the origin of French volunteers who return to Vietnam in 1788, establishing the context for foreign involvement in the Nguyễn–Tây Sơn conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historic South Asian country mentioned as part of the context related to French volunteers returning to Vietnam in 1788."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ấn Độ (India) provided Hindu-Buddhist cultural influences that interacted with Vietnamese culture through history.&lt;SEP&gt;"India is referenced as the origin of volunteers who returned to Vietnam in 1788."&lt;SEP&gt;"India; Buddhism is described as entering Vietnam via routes connected to India."&lt;SEP&gt;"India, referenced as the origin of French volunteers who returned to Vietnam in 1788."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phật giáo được truyền bá vào Việt Nam từ Ấn Độ thông qua Con đường tơ lụa trên biển."&lt;SEP&gt;"Region surrounding Puducherry; part of the broader context for Nguyễn Ánh's recruitment network and maritime support in the 1780s."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ấn Độ (India) was a key partner in Cham Pa's spice and silk trade routes across the Indian Ocean, mentioned in the narrative."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ấn Độ là nền văn hóa và vùng địa lý ở Nam Á, được nhắc tới như nguồn ảnh hưởng Ấn Độ hóa trong Đông Nam Á cổ đại, có tác động đến Phù Nam và Cham Pha."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bc2794dad524831d7084c6c23b70cd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-277de9a31fb7056270b32bed48f417c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01995274690fe1835d7836e3a481a678&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VICTOR OLIVIER DE PUYMANEL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"French volunteer who designed and built the fortified citadel (thành Quy kiên cố) in central Saigon."&lt;SEP&gt;"Victor Olivier de Puymanel was a volunteer who designed and built the Quy kiên cố fortress in central Saigon."&lt;SEP&gt;"Volunteer who designed a fortress at the center of Saigon (the fortress at central Saigon)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SÀI GÒN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A major southern Vietnamese city that became a focal point of French expansion in the Nguyễn period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of South Vietnam (VNCH), the political and administrative center of the southern state."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of VNCH and the epicenter of the South Vietnamese state, fell in 1975."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical name for Ho Chi Minh City; renamed in 1976 during unification."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ho Chi Minh City; the capital region for the Quốc gia Việt Nam during the period described."&lt;SEP&gt;"Major southern Vietnamese city; site of Puymanel's fortress construction and Nguyễn Ánh's base."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sài Gòn (Saigon) is the major southern city identified with Gia Định and a key site of French military and political activity in the south."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of the State of Vietnam located in southern Vietnam, administrative and political center."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of the Việt Nam Cộng hòa (South Vietnam)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical name of the southern city, Saigon."&lt;SEP&gt;"Major southern Vietnamese city in the Mekong Delta area, involved in Nguyễn expansion and regional conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sài Gòn (Gia Định) was a key southern city and fortress whose capture marked early French gains in Nam Kỳ and solidified French control."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sài Gòn (Saigon) is the major city where the uprising's plan was exposed and where authorities prepared to oppose the insurrection."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sài Gòn (Saigon) is the major urban center where many uprising activities occurred, including production sites and arrests."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sài Gòn (Saigon) was a major urban center involved in the uprising and its suppression."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sài Gòn is the city identified with Gia Định; it serves as the urban hub of southern Vietnam and the focal point of the 19th-century French campaign against the Nguyễn dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sài Gòn là thành phố thuộc Gia Định; được đề cập như một căn cứ địa và địa bàn hoạt động của Nguyễn Ánh sau khi chiếm lại từ Tây Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of Việt Nam Cộng hòa in the South; administrative center of VNCH."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southern Vietnamese city chosen as the capital for Quốc gia Việt Nam in the described context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sài Gòn (Saigon) is the major southern Vietnamese city, later known as Ho Chi Minh City; historically synonymous with Gia Định in early references."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sài Gòn is the major city in southern Vietnam; near which key uprising sites such as Cầu Bông and Hóc Môn are located, and which saw French actions during the revolt."&lt;SEP&gt;"The city in southern Vietnam where the uprising was met with French resistance; the plan was betrayed there and authorities acted to suppress it."&lt;SEP&gt;"A major southern Vietnamese city where the uprising plan was exposed, prompting French authorities to prepare for suppression."&lt;SEP&gt;"City center of actions and repression during the uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"Commercial hub and capital of South Vietnam during the early period of the conflict."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sài Gòn (Gia Định) was the southern fortress and city targeted in the French assault, representing the southern theater of operations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sài Gòn (Saigon) is the major southern Vietnamese city where revolutionary committees operated and where several arrests and activities occurred, including in the Chợ Lớn area."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sài Gòn (Saigon) was the southern center under Nguyễn Ánh's influence; Bá Đa Lộc died there in 1799."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sài Gòn is a major city in southern Vietnam and the site of the siege (1859–1867) during French expansion, and later the central hub of colonial administration."&lt;SEP&gt;"A major southern Vietnamese city where Nam Kỳ khởi nghĩa plans were exposed and the French authorities prepared to crack down."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Việt Nam Cộng hòa (South Vietnam)."&lt;SEP&gt;"City in southern Vietnam, known as Saigon, the historic site of the 1859–1867 siege that expanded French control and became part of Nam Kỳ under Cochinchina."&lt;SEP&gt;"Major southern Vietnamese city where much of the uprising planning and industrial activity took place."&lt;SEP&gt;"Major southern Vietnamese city; Nguyễn Ánh strengthened control there and later central Saigon elements were developed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Saigon (Ho Chi Minh City) area central to the uprising and French responses."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southern Vietnamese city that became a focal point of Nguyễn expansion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Southern city where delegates traveled to urge uprisings as part of the nationwide movement."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sài Gòn (Saigon) is the southern hub where Việt Minh leadership and mass demonstrations take place during the August events."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sài Gòn, a major southern Vietnamese city where thousands gathered and where events including the Norodom Square gathering took place."&lt;SEP&gt;"The capital city of the State of Vietnam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c715893072e90e3f1536ec249629f939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e1e11930ab01fb061749acffe85b755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65a62981dbf13b2b72920ca1960170b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-750f8e96d1bcbbd9a06d573268723339&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3e2d86a8a15344ff5c25829117f928c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ebc4b0a454e2ea3adc019759de704c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f9c5b331f58bfebf51e7a9e8de7972&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e0d2b12d6dc3ace3b2354166252a164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bfa206adeee22a1ede2ef2ae0ad9711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf0b29bf76d345dfa9da0349d9f546d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4549a0a33a97f39084dfcd2d857e18f8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NGUYỄN QUANG TOẢN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nguyễn Quang Toản, son of Quang Trung, ascended the throne as Cảnh Thịnh Hoàng đế."&lt;SEP&gt;"Son of Quang Trung; ascended the Tây Sơn throne at age 10 as Cảnh Thịnh Hoàng đế."&lt;SEP&gt;"Son of Quang Trung; ascends to the throne as Cảnh Thịnh Hoàng đế during the Tây Sơn period; later executed by Nguyễn Ánh in 1802."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CẢNH THỊNH HOÀNG ĐẾ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cảnh Thịnh Hoàng đế is the regnal name of Nguyễn Quang Toản when he became emperor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regnal name of Nguyễn Quang Toản; the third emperor of the Tây Sơn dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Emperor of Tây Sơn, the throne name taken by Nguyễn Quang Toản after Quang Trung's death."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HOÀNG ĐẾ GIA LONG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Emperor enabling the Nguyễn dynasty; reign 1802–1820; throne name associated with Nguyễn Ánh's rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Emperor of the Qing dynasty who dispatched a large expeditionary force to Vietnam in 1788 to help Lê Chiêu Thống regain the throne against Tây Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hoàng đế Càn Long là nhà vua nhà Thanh (Trung Hoa) đã phái một đạo quân viễn chinh đến giúp Lê Chiêu Thống trong cuộc đối đầu với Tây Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Qing emperor Càn Long who dispatched a large expeditionary force to assist Lê Chiêu Thống in restoring the throne."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regnal title of Nguyễn Ánh after his victory; founder of the Nguyễn dynasty; reigned 1802–1820."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HOÀNG ĐẾ MÃN THANH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Emperor of the Qing dynasty; referenced in the text regarding naming changes to Việt Nam; represents the Qing monarch.&lt;SEP&gt;"Emperors of the Qing (Manchu) dynasty; referenced in regional political context."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CỬA NGỌ MÔN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cửa Ngọ Môn is a gate at the imperial citadel in Huế (Kinh thành Huế)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gate of the Imperial City in Huế; part of Kinh thành Huế; recognized as a UNESCO World Heritage Site.&lt;SEP&gt;"Gate of the Hue Imperial City; part of Kinh thành Huế; cited in relation to Hue's UNESCO World Heritage designation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KINH THÀNH HUẾ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hue's Imperial City; central to royal monuments and the UNESCO World Heritage designation noted in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kinh thành Huế is the imperial citadel complex in Huế, housing Cửa Ngọ Môn."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Imperial City in Huế; contains Cửa Ngọ Môn; designated as a UNESCO site."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NHÀ NGUYỄN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dynasty that ruled Vietnam from 1802 to 1945; founded by Nguyễn Ánh and consolidated after his victories."&lt;SEP&gt;"Historical Nguyễn dynasty period dominating Vietnam from 1802 to 1945."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyễn Dynasty, the ruling house of Vietnam, pursued centralized monarchy and resisted Western religious influence during the 19th century."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Nguyễn refers to the Nguyễn dynasty, the imperial, centralized monarchy ruling Vietnam in the 19th century, pursuing resistance to Western influence and campaigns against Catholic influence while managing internal and external pressures."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyễn Dynasty ruled large parts of Vietnam in the late medieval to early modern periods and shaped governance and territorial boundaries."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyễn dynasty was the ruling Vietnamese imperial house; its court (triều đình) engaged in treaties with France and led resistance movements such as Cần Vương and Can Vuong.&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Nguyễn was a Vietnamese dynasty whose rise is referenced in relation to the later imperial period."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyễn dynasty, the imperial rulers of Vietnam from 1802 to 1945, whose end marked the transition to republican governance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Nguyễn is the historical Vietnamese imperial dynasty that ruled the country and, together with the French colonial administration, suppressed the Xô Viết Nghệ Tĩnh movement."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Nguyễn refers to the Nguyễn dynasty, ruling Vietnam from 1802 to 1945."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyễn dynasty, ruling house of Vietnam, centralized political power and governed the country through a centralized monarchy during this period."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyễn dynasty, the ruling royal house that rose to power in the late medieval/early modern period; active and rising during the era described as French influence grew, associated with protecting religious freedom and commerce."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Nguyễn refers to the Nguyễn dynasty, a major ruling dynasty in Vietnamese history.&lt;SEP&gt;"Nhà Nguyễn represents the Nguyễn dynasty's governance context, mentioned as the authority against which the movement and uprisings occurred."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyen dynasty, Vietnam's imperial ruling house referenced here as an oppressor alongside the French protectorate during the suppression of the Xô Viết Nghệ Tĩnh uprising."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Nguyễn Lords governed Đàng Trong in the south, forming a competing power center to the Trịnh-led north."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43ffd57cec3dd07166a619a76148c755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3e2d86a8a15344ff5c25829117f928c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6322ad53528ea4e193eaf9be34d2898&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0468eaa8e1dded2ae27a39363308b486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dab4a6d06213a3107d9e1ca29469f707&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd3c75fb34fd08ca28a6bdea4defc0f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;QUANG TRUNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Also known as Nguyễn Huệ; death in 1792 triggers the succession of Nguyễn Quang Toản in Tây Sơn."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quang Trung là niên hiệu trị vì của Nguyễn Huệ khi lên ngôi hoàng đế; ông lãnh đạo Tây Sơn và thực hiện các chiến dịch quân sự lớn để mở rộng quyền lực và thống nhất đất nước."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vietnamese emperor (Nguyễn Huệ); father of Nguyễn Quang Toản; his death precipitated Toản's ascension."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quang Trung, a prominent Tây Sơn emperor, died in 1792 and was the father of Nguyễn Quang Toản."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ca8856a3c05c7e29106b40dae03794b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRUYỆN KIỀU&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Truyện Kiều is a classic Vietnamese narrative poem by Nguyễn Du, a cornerstone of Vietnamese literature."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MINH MẠNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Minh Mạng is the emperor of the Nguyễn dynasty who consolidated the central monarchy and actively sought to neutralize Western influence, including suppression of Catholic missionary activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Minh Mạng was a Vietnamese emperor who reigned from 1820 to 1841 and is cited in historical works referenced in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Minh Mạng was the Nguyễn dynasty emperor who centralized royal authority and pursued strict centralization, including measures to curb Catholic influence in Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Minh Mạng was the Vietnamese king of the Nguyễn dynasty who centralized royal power, pursued a tightly controlled monarchical system, and sought to neutralize Western influence, including enforcing stringent anti-Catholic measures to maintain internal stability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Minh Mạng reinforced centralized monarchical rule within the Nguyễn dynasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Minh Mạng was the emperor who centralized the Nguyễn dynasty's authority and enforced strict anti-Western and anti-Catholic policies to solidify centralized rule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Minh Mạng was a Vietnamese emperor of the Nguyễn dynasty who pursued strong centralization of power and implemented policies that suppressed Western influence, especially Catholic missions, as part of his effort to strengthen royal authority and control."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e0ff15a988913e85c0426ea19d7198&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3692b44304ff5da1927dfcb9ab90461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THIỆU TRỊ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thiệu Trị was Minh Mạng's successor, continuing centralization and anti-Catholic policies as part of the Nguyễn court's governance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thiệu Trị was a Nguyễn dynasty king and a conservative Confucian scholar-king who resisted Western influence and continued strict isolation along with anti-Catholic policies to preserve traditional authority."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thiệu Trị was the successor emperor who continued the Nguyễn dynasty's consolidation and conservative isolationist stance."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TỰ ĐỨC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tự Đức was Thiệu Trị's successor and presided over continued anti-Catholic policies and centralized rule during the later Nguyễn era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tự Đức was the Nguyễn dynasty monarch who followed Thiệu Trị and sustained an isolationist stance, intensifying suppression of Catholic activities and maintaining centralized control."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HỘI THỪA SAI PARIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hội Thừa sai Paris (MEP) is the Paris Foreign Missions Society, a Catholic missionary organization that operated in Vietnam and collaborated with other orders to spread the Catholic faith."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hội Thừa sai Paris is a Catholic missionary society based in Paris whose activities in Vietnam were linked to missionary expansion and provoked local resistance and persecution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Paris Foreign Mission Society, a Catholic missionary organization active in Vietnam as part of the broader Catholic missionary network."&lt;SEP&gt;"Paris Foreign Missions Society, an organization that dispatched Catholic missionaries such as Joseph Marchand to Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Paris Foreign Missions Society, a Catholic missionary organization active in Vietnam, whose presence and activities were cited in the context of anti-Catholic policies and colonial conflict."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Paris Missionary Society, a Catholic mission organization active in Vietnam during the period described (MEP)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fca97c72fe1e0e81ac8addb98deb7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;JOSEPH MARCHAND&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Joseph Marchand (Cố Du) was a French Catholic missionary accused of inciting local Catholics to rebel, contributing to unrest and the pretext for stronger anti-Catholic actions by the Nguyễn regime."&lt;SEP&gt;"Joseph Marchand was a French Catholic missionary (Cố Du) whose presence and actions incited local Catholic communities and triggered anti-French sentiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Joseph Marchand, a French Catholic missionary (Cố Du), was accused of inciting local Catholics to rebel and expanding French influence in Vietnam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NAPOLÉON III&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Napoléon III was the French emperor who ordered naval action against Vietnam, providing political backing for expansion and the bombardment of Đà Nẵng."&lt;SEP&gt;"Napoléon III, Emperor of France, ordered bombardment of Da Nang to protect missionary activity and expand French influence in Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Napoléon III was the French emperor who ordered the attack on Vietnam, initiating broader colonial intervention."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐÔ ĐỐC CHARLES RIGAULT DE GENOUILLY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Charles Rigault de Genouilly commanded the French expedition, led assaults on Đà Nẵng and Gia Định, and captured key sites."&lt;SEP&gt;"French admiral who commanded the naval assault against Vietnamese ports, including the bombardment of Tourane (Đà Nẵng) in 1858."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TOURANE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The historic French name for Đà Nẵng, used in 19th-century naval campaigns against Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tourane is the historical French name for Đà Nẵng, referenced in the 1858 bombardment and related naval actions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tourane is the historical Western name for Đà Nẵng, the site of significant French military action in 1858."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ĐÀ NẴNG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A city in Vietnam mentioned as a location in which prisons and movement-related facilities were established during the colonial suppression period."&lt;SEP&gt;"A major central Vietnamese port city that was bombarded by French forces as part of the early phase of European colonial intervention."&lt;SEP&gt;"City in central Vietnam; referenced as publishing location for Nhà xuất bản Đà Nẵng."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đà Nẵng, historically Tourane, was the port city bombarded by the French in 1858, marking the start of sustained French intervention in Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đà Nẵng (Tourane) is a port city in central Vietnam that was attacked by the French during the invasion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đà Nẵng is the location of a Vietnamese publisher mentioned in the bibliography (Nhà xuất bản Đà Nẵng)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Đà Nẵng is a central Vietnamese port city that became a focal point of the 1858 French invasion and served as a strategic site in the Cochinchina campaigns."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec018c17c8753decfbcce379313be41b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0468eaa8e1dded2ae27a39363308b486</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NAM KỲ LỤC TỈNH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nam Kỳ lục tỉnh comprises the six southern provinces of Nam Kỳ; it represents the administrative unit established under French colonial authority in the south."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam Kỳ lục tỉnh refers to the six southern provinces administered under Nam Kỳ within French Indochina."&lt;SEP&gt;"The six southern Vietnamese provinces under French colonial administration, including Gia Định."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam Kỳ lục tỉnh refers to the six southern provinces that formed Cochinchina under French rule, central to the colonial expansion."&lt;SEP&gt;"The six southern provinces of Nam Kỳ (Cochinchina) under French rule; a subregion of the Nam Kỳ entity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nam Kỳ lục tỉnh refers to the six southern provinces of Vietnam that became a focal area of French expansion and rebellion response."&lt;SEP&gt;"The six southern provinces under French control after the 1859–1867 campaign, forming part of Cochinchina."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3e2d86a8a15344ff5c25829117f928c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LÊ VĂN KHÔI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Historian who authored the Đại Việt sử ký, the national chronicle used to record events of the era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Văn Hưu was a Vietnamese scholar who authored the Đại Việt sử ký chronicle documenting early Trần-era events."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Văn Khôi led a significant uprising against the Nguyễn authorities and the colonial powers within the period of religious strife."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Văn Khôi was a local rebel leader whose uprising reflected internal resistance and broader religious and political tensions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Văn Khôi was a rebel leader whose uprising against the Nguyễn court was linked to anti-Catholic and internal political tensions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lê Văn Khôi was a rebel leader whose uprising against the Nguyễn regime is referenced in connection with religious tensions and anti-regime sentiment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4943e96cb569799b168fc03f9b7f0d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIÊN QUÂN PHÁP – TÂY BAN NHA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Franco-Spanish alliance formed to assault Vietnamese ports and cities during the early phase of French colonial expansion in Vietnam (mid-19th century)."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Franco-Spanish coalition that carried out operations in Vietnam, including attacks on Tourane."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHIẾN TRANH PHÁP – THANH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Franco–Qing War context connected to Vietnamese history, influencing French actions in the region and the broader colonial conflicts."&lt;SEP&gt;"War between France and the Qing Dynasty that is referenced as part of the broader colonial era in Southeast Asia."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88c2283fe59c1e2798b0a0aafedc3142</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHIẾN DỊCH BẮC KỲ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
